// Pictures from the teacher slides and student notes (generated by build_images.py).
SD.images.push(
  {"id": "9dc48db810f5", "topic": "genetics", "outcome": "gen-bg", "src": "img/slides/9dc48db810f5.webp", "caption": "From cell to gene: the nucleus holds chromosomes, each chromosome is one long DNA molecule coiled tightly, and DNA is a double helix. The bracketed lengths of DNA represent genes, which are sections of DNA that code for one protein.", "from": "DNA Genetics Worksheet · page 2"},
  {"id": "459a9cb5cd2f", "topic": "genetics", "outcome": "gen-bg", "src": "img/slides/459a9cb5cd2f.webp", "caption": "DNA is read in triplets of bases called <b>codons</b>. The mRNA copy has the same sequence as the DNA coding strand except that U replaces T, and each codon codes for one amino acid (GAG = Glu, CCC = Pro and so on) in the protein chain.", "from": "Mutations · slide 4"},
  {"id": "b99b02f08909", "topic": "genetics", "outcome": "gen-o6", "src": "img/slides/b99b02f08909.webp", "caption": "Gregor Mendel, the 'father of genetics'. He bred garden peas and worked out the laws of inheritance (segregation and independent assortment) before chromosomes or genes had been seen.", "from": "Inheritance Patterns_ PUNNETT & PEDIGREES · slide 13"},
  {"id": "e368cdc25234", "topic": "genetics", "outcome": "gen-o6", "src": "img/slides/e368cdc25234.webp", "caption": "Some of the contrasting pea traits Mendel studied: flower colour, seed colour, seed shape, pod colour and pod shape. Each trait has only two clear forms, and in each pair shown the left-hand form (purple, yellow seed, round seed, green pod, inflated pod) is dominant.", "from": "Inheritance Patterns_ PUNNETT & PEDIGREES · slide 13"},
  {"id": "b327d42cd02e", "topic": "genetics", "outcome": "gen-o6", "src": "img/slides/b327d42cd02e.webp", "caption": "Mendel's parental cross: a pure-breeding tall plant (TT) crossed with a pure-breeding dwarf plant (tt). Each parent can only make one type of gamete, so every F<sub>1</sub> plant is Tt and tall.", "from": "Inheritance Patterns_ PUNNETT & PEDIGREES · slide 15"},
  {"id": "798df7ed350b", "topic": "genetics", "outcome": "gen-o6", "src": "img/slides/798df7ed350b.webp", "caption": "Crossing two F<sub>1</sub> plants (Tt × Tt) gives Mendel's classic F<sub>2</sub> result. The genotypic ratio is 1 TT : 2 Tt : 1 tt, and the phenotypic ratio is 3 tall : 1 short (75% : 25%).", "from": "Inheritance Patterns_ PUNNETT & PEDIGREES · slide 17"},
  {"id": "15c5aa053116", "topic": "genetics", "outcome": "gen-o7", "src": "img/slides/15c5aa053116.webp", "caption": "A pair of homologous chromosomes carries the same gene at the same <b>locus</b>, but the alleles there can differ. AA and aa are <b>homozygous</b> (two identical alleles), while Aa is <b>heterozygous</b> (two different alleles).", "from": "Inheritance Patterns_ PUNNETT & PEDIGREES · slide 3"},
  {"id": "0d8afdf1ba53", "topic": "genetics", "outcome": "gen-o7", "src": "img/slides/0d8afdf1ba53.webp", "caption": "The dominant allele (B, brown) is expressed whenever it is present, so BB and Bb both give brown eyes; blue eyes need two recessive alleles (bb). This one-gene model is a simplification, because real eye colour is controlled by several genes.", "from": "Inheritance Patterns_ PUNNETT & PEDIGREES · slide 5"},
  {"id": "8db58d6122ef", "topic": "genetics", "outcome": "gen-o7", "src": "img/slides/8db58d6122ef.webp", "caption": "A trait controlled by one gene shows <b>discontinuous</b> variation (a few separate categories, like hairline shape here). A <b>polygenic</b> trait such as skin colour or height shows <b>continuous</b> variation, with a range of phenotypes that form a bell-shaped spread.", "from": "Inheritance Patterns_ PUNNETT & PEDIGREES · slide 41"},
  {"id": "eb7a205209f0", "topic": "genetics", "outcome": "gen-o7b", "src": "img/slides/eb7a205209f0.webp", "caption": "Substitution (point) mutations change one base, and so one codon. A <b>silent</b> mutation still codes for the same amino acid (Lys), a <b>nonsense</b> mutation creates a STOP codon so the protein is cut short, and a <b>missense</b> mutation codes for a different amino acid (Arg or Thr).", "from": "Mutations · slide 7"},
  {"id": "0b01b1718408", "topic": "genetics", "outcome": "gen-o7b", "src": "img/slides/0b01b1718408.webp", "caption": "<b>Frameshift</b> mutations: inserting or deleting one base shifts the reading frame, so every codon after that point changes. This is why they usually do more damage than a substitution. (The slide labels the new AUU codon as Asn, but AUU codes for isoleucine, so the insertion row should read Ile – Arg – Tyr – Ser.)", "from": "Mutations · slide 8"},
  {"id": "b2d1cd8d3929", "topic": "genetics", "outcome": "gen-o7b", "src": "img/slides/b2d1cd8d3929.webp", "caption": "Chromosomal (structural) mutations affect large sections of a chromosome, usually many genes at once. In a <b>duplication</b> a section is copied, in an <b>inversion</b> it is flipped, in a <b>deletion</b> it is lost, in an <b>insertion</b> a section from another chromosome is added, and in a <b>translocation</b> sections are swapped between non-homologous chromosomes.", "from": "Mutations · slide 10"},
  {"id": "7286b005e340", "topic": "genetics", "outcome": "gen-o7b", "src": "img/slides/7286b005e340.webp", "caption": "<b>Nondisjunction</b> means chromosomes fail to separate during meiosis. (A) shows normal meiosis, (B) shows failure in meiosis I, where all four gametes are abnormal, and (C) shows failure in meiosis II, where two gametes are normal. Gametes end up with an extra chromosome (disomic) or a missing one (nullisomic).", "from": "Mutations · slide 12"},
  {"id": "a775ca3421f0", "topic": "genetics", "outcome": "gen-o7b", "src": "img/slides/a775ca3421f0.webp", "caption": "Karyotype of a female (XX) with three copies of chromosome 21 (shown in green), making 47 chromosomes in total. This is trisomy 21 (Down syndrome), a change in chromosome number usually caused by nondisjunction.", "from": "DNA Molecule of Life · page 4"},
  {"id": "f6d9d684c1a6", "topic": "genetics", "outcome": "gen-o7b", "src": "img/slides/f6d9d684c1a6.webp", "caption": "A karyotype lines up the 22 pairs of <b>autosomes</b> (1–22) and the <b>sex chromosomes</b> (X, Y). This one has two X chromosomes and one Y, which is 47,XXY (Klinefelter syndrome), an abnormal number of sex chromosomes.", "from": "Mutations · slide 9"},
  {"id": "4c5323dae901", "topic": "genetics", "outcome": "gen-o8a", "src": "img/slides/4c5323dae901.webp", "caption": "A heterozygous plant (Yy) crossed with a homozygous recessive plant (yy) gives 2 Yy : 2 yy, so 50% show the dominant phenotype and 50% the recessive. This 1 : 1 result is what a test cross gives when the parent is heterozygous. (The slide shows yellow pods as dominant, but in Mendel's peas green pod colour is dominant; yellow is the dominant <i>seed</i> colour.)", "from": "Inheritance Patterns_ PUNNETT & PEDIGREES · slide 8"},
  {"id": "f591da5df5c8", "topic": "genetics", "outcome": "gen-o8a", "src": "img/slides/f591da5df5c8.webp", "caption": "A test cross in <i>Drosophila</i>: a long-winged fly of unknown genotype is crossed with a short-winged (vv) fly. If all offspring have long wings, the parent was VV (Cross 1); if half have short wings, the parent was Vv (Cross 2).", "from": "Inheritance Patterns_ PUNNETT & PEDIGREES · slide 23"},
  {"id": "28b4788fc3b2", "topic": "genetics", "outcome": "gen-o8b", "src": "img/slides/28b4788fc3b2.webp", "caption": "Snapdragons show <b>incomplete dominance</b>: red (R<sub>1</sub>R<sub>1</sub>) × white (R<sub>2</sub>R<sub>2</sub>) gives pink heterozygotes (R<sub>1</sub>R<sub>2</sub>). The heterozygote makes only half as much red pigment, so its phenotype is a blend between the two parents.", "from": "Inheritance Patterns_ PUNNETT & PEDIGREES · slide 26"},
  {"id": "e8a02e79121e", "topic": "genetics", "outcome": "gen-o8b", "src": "img/slides/e8a02e79121e.webp", "caption": "Punnett square for red (R<sub>1</sub>R<sub>1</sub>) × white (R<sub>2</sub>R<sub>2</sub>) snapdragons. Every F<sub>1</sub> plant is R<sub>1</sub>R<sub>2</sub> and pink; crossing two of these pink plants gives 1 red : 2 pink : 1 white.", "from": "Inheritance Patterns_ PUNNETT & PEDIGREES · slide 28"},
  {"id": "d64e4187d65c", "topic": "genetics", "outcome": "gen-o8b", "src": "img/slides/d64e4187d65c.webp", "caption": "Roan cattle show <b>co-dominance</b>: a red (C<sup>R</sup>C<sup>R</sup>) × white (C<sup>W</sup>C<sup>W</sup>) cross gives C<sup>R</sup>C<sup>W</sup> calves. Both alleles are fully expressed, so each hair is either red or white, which gives the mixed roan coat. Unlike incomplete dominance, the colours are not blended.", "from": "Inheritance Patterns_ PUNNETT & PEDIGREES · slide 29"},
  {"id": "b9f07ad8558b", "topic": "genetics", "outcome": "gen-o8b", "src": "img/slides/b9f07ad8558b.webp", "caption": "ABO blood groups involve <b>multiple alleles</b> (I<sup>A</sup>, I<sup>B</sup>, i). I<sup>A</sup> and I<sup>B</sup> are co-dominant, so I<sup>A</sup>I<sup>B</sup> gives type AB with both antigens on the red blood cell, and i is recessive, so only ii gives type O with no antigens.", "from": "Inheritance Patterns_ PUNNETT & PEDIGREES · slide 11"},
  {"id": "039e4c69983b", "topic": "genetics", "outcome": "gen-o8c", "src": "img/slides/039e4c69983b.webp", "caption": "Haemophilia cross: carrier mother (X<sup>H</sup>X<sup>h</sup>) × unaffected father (X<sup>H</sup>Y). None of the daughters are affected (half are carriers), but each son has a 1 in 2 chance of haemophilia, because a son gets his only X from his mother.", "from": "Inheritance Patterns_ PUNNETT & PEDIGREES · slide 36"},
  {"id": "a8123987c3af", "topic": "genetics", "outcome": "gen-o8c", "src": "img/slides/a8123987c3af.webp", "caption": "Haemophilia in Queen Victoria's descendants, a famous X-linked recessive pedigree. Victoria was a carrier, the affected individuals are almost all males (e.g. Leopold, Alexei), and the allele passed down through carrier daughters such as Alice of Hesse.", "from": "Inheritance Patterns_ PUNNETT & PEDIGREES · slide 35"},
  {"id": "416ff41ff239", "topic": "genetics", "outcome": "gen-o8c", "src": "img/slides/416ff41ff239.webp", "caption": "The X chromosome is much larger than the Y (about 830 genes compared with about 72). This is why there are many X-linked traits but very few Y-linked ones, and most Y genes are involved in male sex determination and fertility.", "from": "Inheritance Patterns_ PUNNETT & PEDIGREES · slide 39"},
  {"id": "8e999346b250", "topic": "genetics", "outcome": "gen-o8d", "src": "img/slides/8e999346b250.webp", "caption": "Standard pedigree symbols: squares are males, circles are females, shaded means affected, a horizontal line joins a couple, and a vertical line leads down to their children. A half-shaded symbol marks a heterozygous carrier, used for X-linked recessive traits.", "from": "Inheritance Patterns_ PUNNETT & PEDIGREES · slide 42"},
  {"id": "991661034957", "topic": "genetics", "outcome": "gen-o8d", "src": "img/slides/991661034957.webp", "caption": "Autosomal dominant pedigree (freckles, F dominant). The trait appears in every generation and affects males and females about equally, and every affected child has an affected parent.", "from": "Inheritance Patterns_ PUNNETT & PEDIGREES · slide 45"},
  {"id": "799d85dc4b9e", "topic": "genetics", "outcome": "gen-o8d", "src": "img/slides/799d85dc4b9e.webp", "caption": "Unaffected parents (I-1 × I-2 and II-3 × II-4) have affected children, so the trait is recessive. The affected daughter III-10 has an unaffected father, which rules out X-linked recessive, so this is autosomal recessive.", "from": "Inheritance Patterns_ PUNNETT & PEDIGREES · slide 48"},
  {"id": "f58a7f958fe6", "topic": "genetics", "outcome": "gen-o8d", "src": "img/slides/f58a7f958fe6.webp", "caption": "X-linked recessive pedigree (colour blindness). The trait mostly affects males and seems to skip a generation: the colour-blind grandfather passes the allele to his daughters (carriers), who then pass it to some of their sons.", "from": "Inheritance Patterns_ PUNNETT & PEDIGREES · slide 63"},
  {"id": "15df563e67fd", "topic": "genetics", "outcome": "gen-o8d", "src": "img/slides/15df563e67fd.webp", "caption": "X-linked dominant pedigree (vitamin D-resistant rickets). The affected father II-2 passes his X to every daughter, so all his daughters are affected and none of his sons are. An affected heterozygous mother passes the trait to about half of her children of either sex.", "from": "Inheritance Patterns_ PUNNETT & PEDIGREES · slide 38"},
  {"id": "903a214ca785", "topic": "genetics", "outcome": "gen-o8d", "src": "img/slides/903a214ca785.webp", "caption": "Y-linked pedigree: only males are affected, and every son of an affected father is affected. The trait never passes through females, because the Y chromosome goes only from father to son.", "from": "Inheritance Patterns_ PUNNETT & PEDIGREES · slide 65"},
  {"id": "8f5c9cb41e19", "topic": "genetics", "outcome": "gen-o8d", "src": "img/slides/8f5c9cb41e19.webp", "caption": "Ruling out sex linkage: an affected father (I-1) with an unaffected daughter (II-6) rules out X-linked dominant. In the same pedigree, an affected mother with an unaffected son rules out X-linked recessive, and an affected father with an unaffected son rules out Y-linked, leaving an autosomal pattern.", "from": "Inheritance Patterns_ PUNNETT & PEDIGREES · slide 68"},
  {"id": "939665d23eb8", "topic": "evolution", "outcome": "evo-o1", "src": "img/slides/939665d23eb8.webp", "caption": "Lamarck compared with Darwin–Wallace. Lamarck thought giraffes stretched their necks during their lives and passed on the longer neck (inheritance of acquired characteristics). Darwin and Wallace argued that neck length already varied, longer-necked giraffes survived and reproduced more, and the trait became more common over generations.", "from": "Evolution ~ Theories · slide 11"},
  {"id": "50d218330d9c", "topic": "evolution", "outcome": "evo-o1", "src": "img/slides/50d218330d9c.webp", "caption": "Lamarck's idea of use and disuse: each giraffe stretches to reach higher leaves and passes its stretched neck to its offspring. This is incorrect, because changes gained during a lifetime do not change the DNA in sex cells and so cannot be inherited.", "from": "Evolution ~ Theories · slide 7"},
  {"id": "13b41507b48f", "topic": "evolution", "outcome": "evo-o1", "src": "img/slides/13b41507b48f.webp", "caption": "Jean-Baptiste Lamarck (1744–1829) was one of the first to propose that species change over time. He was wrong about the mechanism (inheritance of acquired characteristics).", "from": "Evolution ~ Theories · slide 6"},
  {"id": "1c7fba182042", "topic": "evolution", "outcome": "evo-o1", "src": "img/slides/1c7fba182042.webp", "caption": "Charles Darwin (1809–1882) proposed evolution by natural selection, published in <i>On the Origin of Species</i> (1859), with Alfred Russel Wallace reaching the same idea independently. Evidence from his voyage on the <i>Beagle</i>, including the Galápagos finches, supported the theory.", "from": "Evolution ~ Theories · slide 6"},
  {"id": "3df251dfec31", "topic": "evolution", "outcome": "evo-o2", "src": "img/slides/3df251dfec31.webp", "caption": "Peppered moths (<i>Biston betularia</i>) on soot-darkened bark: the dark form is camouflaged while the light form stands out to birds. During the Industrial Revolution, birds ate more light moths, so dark moths survived and reproduced more and became common.", "from": "Evolution ~ Theories · slide 30"},
  {"id": "08994b2b45e3", "topic": "evolution", "outcome": "evo-o2", "src": "img/slides/08994b2b45e3.webp", "caption": "Antibiotic resistance is natural selection in action. A few bacteria already carry resistance, and the antibiotic (the selection pressure) kills the susceptible ones, so the resistant survivors multiply until the population is mostly resistant. The antibiotic does not cause the resistance mutation.", "from": "Evolution ~ Theories · slide 32"},
  {"id": "77e83133f04e", "topic": "evolution", "outcome": "evo-o2", "src": "img/slides/77e83133f04e.webp", "caption": "Organisms produce more offspring than can survive, and limited resources (food, habitat, mates) cause a struggle for existence. So not all individuals survive and reproduce, and the ones with favourable variations are more likely to.", "from": "Evolution ~ Theories · slide 25"},
  {"id": "b80a7e609dad", "topic": "evolution", "outcome": "evo-o3", "src": "img/slides/b80a7e609dad.webp", "caption": "In natural selection, the environment selects which individuals survive and reproduce (e.g. giraffes). In artificial selection, humans choose which individuals breed for traits they want, producing very different dog breeds from wolf ancestors in a relatively short time.", "from": "Evolution ~ Theories · slide 43"},
  {"id": "998a62670d97", "topic": "evolution", "outcome": "evo-o3", "src": "img/slides/998a62670d97.webp", "caption": "Merino sheep are an Australian example of artificial selection. Breeders use genetic data (e.g. the MerinoSelect evaluation system) to choose rams and ewes with desired traits such as fine, heavy fleece, so those traits increase in the flock over generations.", "from": "Evolution ~ Theories · slide 46"},
  {"id": "b601989ef1c5", "topic": "evolution", "outcome": "evo-o3b", "src": "img/slides/b601989ef1c5.webp", "caption": "<b>Divergent evolution</b>: related species descended from a common ancestor become more different (dog breeds from the wolf). <b>Convergent evolution</b>: unrelated groups evolve similar features (wings in birds, bats and insects) because of similar selection pressures. They do share a very distant ancestor, but not one that had wings.", "from": "Evolution ~ Theories · slide 40"},
  {"id": "dde9f50af814", "topic": "evolution", "outcome": "evo-o3b", "src": "img/slides/dde9f50af814.webp", "caption": "A species is a group that can interbreed to produce fertile offspring. A horse (2n = 64) and a donkey (2n = 62) can produce a mule (2n = 63), but its odd chromosome number means the chromosomes cannot pair in meiosis, so the mule is infertile and the horse and donkey are separate species.", "from": "Evolution ~ Theories · slide 17"},
  {"id": "ffdcf9afea5b", "topic": "evolution", "outcome": "evo-o3b", "src": "img/slides/ffdcf9afea5b.webp", "caption": "Darwin's Galápagos finches: one ancestral species from the South American mainland diverged into many species. Beaks became adapted to different foods (seed eaters, cactus eaters, bud eaters, insect eaters), a classic example of divergent evolution and speciation on isolated islands.", "from": "Evolution ~ Theories · slide 18"},
  {"id": "69d6a0380a9e", "topic": "evolution", "outcome": "evo-o3b", "src": "img/slides/69d6a0380a9e.webp", "caption": "A shark (fish), an ichthyosaur (reptile) and a dolphin (mammal) all evolved a streamlined body, fins and a tail fin independently. The same selection pressure (moving fast through water) produced similar shapes in unrelated groups.", "from": "Evolution ~ Theories · slide 38"},
  {"id": "c526f49204ad", "topic": "evolution", "outcome": "evo-o4a", "src": "img/slides/c526f49204ad.webp", "caption": "The <b>pentadactyl limb</b>: bat wing, mouse forelimb, whale flipper and human arm all have the same bones (humerus, radius, ulna, metacarpals, fingers), modified for different jobs. These <b>homologous structures</b> point to a common ancestor.", "from": "Evolution ~ Evidence (10SCL) · slide 7"},
  {"id": "d6ee3847b33e", "topic": "evolution", "outcome": "evo-o4a", "src": "img/slides/d6ee3847b33e.webp", "caption": "<b>Analogous structures</b> do the same job but have different structures and evolved separately: an insect leg and a horse leg, a fish fin and a dolphin flipper, an insect wing and a bird wing. They show convergent evolution, not close relationship.", "from": "Evolution ~ Evidence (10SCL) · slide 8"},
  {"id": "67f4e913a012", "topic": "evolution", "outcome": "evo-o4a", "src": "img/slides/67f4e913a012.webp", "caption": "Whales have a small <b>vestigial pelvis</b> (and sometimes leg bones) buried in their body with no use for walking. It is left over from four-legged land-mammal ancestors.", "from": "Evolution ~ Evidence (10SCL) · slide 9"},
  {"id": "b7217194a488", "topic": "evolution", "outcome": "evo-o4a", "src": "img/slides/b7217194a488.webp", "caption": "Early embryos of a fish, reptile, bird and human look very alike, all with a tail and pharyngeal slits. The slide calls these 'gill slits', but only fish develop them into gills; in humans they become parts of the jaw, ear and throat. Shared embryo features suggest a common ancestor (comparative embryology).", "from": "Evolution ~ Evidence (10SCL) · slide 11"},
  {"id": "773601c8aa0b", "topic": "evolution", "outcome": "evo-o4b", "src": "img/slides/773601c8aa0b.webp", "caption": "Ratites (flightless birds such as the ostrich, rhea, emu, cassowary, kiwi and the extinct moa and elephant bird), together with their flying relatives the tinamous, live on southern continents that were once joined as Gondwana. Biogeography explains this as descent from a common ancestor whose populations were split up as the continents drifted apart.", "from": "Evolution ~ Evidence (10SCL) · slide 18"},
  {"id": "a4c4a28ccbdf", "topic": "evolution", "outcome": "evo-o4b", "src": "img/slides/a4c4a28ccbdf.webp", "caption": "Continental drift: Pangaea (225 million years ago) split into Laurasia and Gondwana, which broke into today's continents. This explains why closely related species and fossils are found on continents now separated by oceans.", "from": "Evolution ~ Evidence (10SCL) · slide 14"},
  {"id": "743c790883c0", "topic": "evolution", "outcome": "evo-o4b", "src": "img/slides/743c790883c0.webp", "caption": "DNA hybridisation: single strands from two species are joined and then heated. The more similar the DNA, the more bases pair up and the higher the temperature needed to separate the hybrid strands, so the more closely related the species are.", "from": "Evolution ~ Evidence (10SCL) · slide 20"},
  {"id": "e25685061d1c", "topic": "evolution", "outcome": "evo-o4b", "src": "img/slides/e25685061d1c.webp", "caption": "Biochemical evidence compares the amino acid sequence of the same protein (haemoglobin) in different species. Fewer differences mean a more recent common ancestor ('Iso' on the slide is isoleucine, normally abbreviated Ile).", "from": "Evolution ~ Evidence (10SCL) · slide 23"},
  {"id": "d496d3d64ba1", "topic": "evolution", "outcome": "evo-o4c", "src": "img/slides/d496d3d64ba1.webp", "caption": "<b>Law of superposition</b> (relative dating): in undisturbed sedimentary rock, lower layers were laid down first and are older, and upper layers are younger. Fossils can be put in order of age but not given an age in years.", "from": "Evolution ~ Evidence (10SCL) · slide 28"},
  {"id": "03a2e8418d30", "topic": "evolution", "outcome": "evo-o4c", "src": "img/slides/03a2e8418d30.webp", "caption": "Absolute (radiometric) dating uses the constant decay rate of a radioactive isotope. Carbon-14 has a half-life of about 5,730 years, so after each half-life only half of the remaining <sup>14</sup>C is left. It is only useful for remains up to about 50,000 years old; older rocks are dated with isotopes such as potassium-40.", "from": "Evolution ~ Evidence (10SCL) · slide 30"},
  {"id": "30ea295f0e61", "topic": "evolution", "outcome": "evo-o4c", "src": "img/slides/30ea295f0e61.webp", "caption": "The horse fossil record over about 55 million years runs from the small, many-toed <i>Hyracotherium</i> to modern <i>Equus</i>. Body size increased and the forefoot reduced to one hoofed toe, changes linked to the shift from forest to open grassland, where speed helped escape predators.", "from": "Evolution ~ Evidence (10SCL) · slide 42"},
  {"id": "4181f2ff82c9", "topic": "evolution", "outcome": "evo-o4c", "src": "img/slides/4181f2ff82c9.webp", "caption": "A limitation of the fossil record is that it is incomplete. Fossilisation is rare and soft-bodied organisms seldom fossilise, so gaps hide some gradual changes; a gap is not evidence against evolution.", "from": "Evolution ~ Evidence (10SCL) · slide 43"},
  {"id": "5f17dd3eb748", "topic": "evolution", "outcome": "evo-o5", "src": "img/slides/5f17dd3eb748.webp", "caption": "How a fossil forms: (a) the organism dies, (b) soft tissues decay and the remains are quickly buried in sediment, (c) more sediment builds up and hardens into rock while minerals replace the bone, and (d) uplift and erosion expose it until (e) it is discovered.", "from": "Evolution ~ Evidence (10SCL) · slide 33"},
  {"id": "532360e84bfc", "topic": "evolution", "outcome": "evo-o5", "src": "img/slides/532360e84bfc.webp", "caption": "The stages of fossilisation: <b>death</b>, rapid <b>burial</b>, <b>lithification</b> (sediment compacts into rock), <b>petrification</b> (water carrying minerals seeps in and replaces the bone, turning it to stone), then <b>exposure</b> by erosion and uplift. The process takes thousands to millions of years.", "from": "Evolution ~ Evidence (10SCL) · slide 35"},
  {"id": "54d7f6c2ca15", "topic": "evolution", "outcome": "evo-o6", "src": "img/slides/54d7f6c2ca15.webp", "caption": "<i>Archaeopteryx</i> is the classic <b>transitional fossil</b> between reptiles (dinosaurs) and birds. It has bird features (feathers, wings) and reptile features (teeth, clawed fingers, a long bony tail).", "from": "Evolution ~ Evidence (10SCL) · slide 36"},
  {"id": "7d37e0c2f6ca", "topic": "evolution", "outcome": "evo-o6", "src": "img/slides/7d37e0c2f6ca.webp", "caption": "Transitional fossils of whale evolution: land-dwelling ancestors such as <i>Pakicetus</i> (about 52 million years ago) lead through <i>Ambulocetus</i> and <i>Rodhocetus</i> to fully aquatic <i>Dorudon</i> and modern whales. Over time the hind limbs shrink and the body becomes streamlined.", "from": "Evolution ~ Evidence (10SCL) · slide 43"},
  {"id": "7d25642621b0", "topic": "evolution", "outcome": "evo-o6", "src": "img/slides/7d25642621b0.webp", "caption": "<b>Index fossils</b> (like this ammonite) were widespread but lived for only a short time. Finding the same index fossil in layer C at two different sites shows the layers are the same age, so rocks can be matched across sites.", "from": "Evolution ~ Evidence (10SCL) · slide 38"},
  {"id": "9ad13a7e2370", "topic": "evolution", "outcome": "evo-o6", "src": "img/slides/9ad13a7e2370.webp", "caption": "Combining an index fossil with relative dating: fossil B occurs at both sites. At Site One A lies above B, and at Site Two B lies above C, so A is younger than C even though they are never found together.", "from": "Evolution ~ Evidence (10SCL) · slide 39"},
  {"id": "d7cadfd4dbfb", "topic": "evolution", "outcome": "evo-o6", "src": "img/slides/d7cadfd4dbfb.webp", "caption": "The coelacanth is a <b>living fossil</b>: today's fish (top) looks very similar to fossils (bottom) from hundreds of millions of years ago. It has still evolved, but its stable deep-sea environment meant little selection pressure to change its body form.", "from": "Evolution ~ Evidence (10SCL) · slide 41"},
  {"id": "a986ba3eca01", "topic": "universe", "outcome": "uni-o1", "src": "img/slides/a986ba3eca01.webp", "caption": "Side-on view of the Milky Way: a flat disc of stars about 100 000 light-years across with a bulging centre. The Sun sits in the disc about 28 000 light-years from the galactic centre.", "from": "The Universe Summary + TECHNOLOGIES Powerpoint · slide 16"},
  {"id": "3fdd8db05ff3", "topic": "universe", "outcome": "uni-o1", "src": "img/slides/3fdd8db05ff3.webp", "caption": "The Orion Nebula, a huge glowing cloud of hydrogen, helium and dust about 1 300 light-years away. Nebulae like this are <b>stellar nurseries</b>, where new stars are forming.", "from": "The Universe Summary + TECHNOLOGIES Powerpoint · slide 4"},
  {"id": "8c62dc81f939", "topic": "universe", "outcome": "uni-o1", "src": "img/slides/8c62dc81f939.webp", "caption": "The Horsehead Nebula in Orion: a dark cloud of cold dust seen in silhouette against glowing pink hydrogen gas. Depending on their gas, dust and nearby stars, nebulae can glow, reflect starlight or block light.", "from": "The Universe Summary + TECHNOLOGIES Powerpoint · slide 7"},
  {"id": "c656a863ec5a", "topic": "universe", "outcome": "uni-o1", "src": "img/slides/c656a863ec5a.webp", "caption": "The Pleiades (Seven Sisters, M45), an <b>open cluster</b> of young, hot blue stars that formed together from the same nebula. Stars are grouped in clusters rather than spread evenly through a galaxy; the blue haze is dust reflecting their light.", "from": "The Universe Summary + TECHNOLOGIES Powerpoint · slide 13"},
  {"id": "0d3b57df8386", "topic": "universe", "outcome": "uni-o1", "src": "img/slides/0d3b57df8386.webp", "caption": "An all-sky map of galaxies, coloured by distance (the band across the middle is our own Milky Way). Galaxies are not spread evenly: they gather in clusters and thread-like filaments with huge, nearly empty voids between them.", "from": "The Universe Summary + TECHNOLOGIES Powerpoint · slide 25"},
  {"id": "3492e26fd143", "topic": "universe", "outcome": "uni-o1", "src": "img/slides/3492e26fd143.webp", "caption": "A computer simulation of the 'cosmic web'. Galaxies and galaxy clusters (bright knots) lie along filaments, the largest known structures in the universe, with dark cosmic voids between them; the inset zooms in on one massive cluster.", "from": "The Universe Summary + TECHNOLOGIES Powerpoint · slide 21"},
  {"id": "faaff6a413e7", "topic": "universe", "outcome": "uni-o2", "src": "img/slides/faaff6a413e7.webp", "caption": "Stars make their own light by <b>nuclear fusion</b> and are made of plasma, while planets and natural satellites (moons) only reflect light. Planets orbit stars, and moons orbit planets.", "from": "Major Features of the Universe · slide 6"},
  {"id": "ca48dfa6d03d", "topic": "universe", "outcome": "uni-o3", "src": "img/slides/ca48dfa6d03d.webp", "caption": "The 'Pillars of Creation' in the Eagle Nebula: columns of cold gas and dust several light-years tall. Inside them, gravity pulls denser clumps together until they collapse into protostars.", "from": "The Universe Summary + TECHNOLOGIES Powerpoint · slide 5"},
  {"id": "570580b74592", "topic": "universe", "outcome": "uni-o3", "src": "img/slides/570580b74592.webp", "caption": "The 'Cosmic Cliffs' in the Carina Nebula, photographed by the James Webb Space Telescope. Stars are born in clusters where the densest parts of a nebula collapse under their own gravity to form protostars.", "from": "Major Features of the Universe · slide 7"},
  {"id": "754ddd796a98", "topic": "universe", "outcome": "uni-o3", "src": "img/slides/754ddd796a98.webp", "caption": "Artist's impression of an <b>accretion disc</b>: gravity draws gas and dust from a nebula into a spinning disc. The hot, dense centre becomes a star once nuclear fusion of hydrogen begins.", "from": "The Universe Summary + TECHNOLOGIES Powerpoint · slide 10"},
  {"id": "f0e9656cef1f", "topic": "universe", "outcome": "uni-o3", "src": "img/slides/f0e9656cef1f.webp", "caption": "Artist's impression of a young solar system. In the outer accretion disc, clumps of dust stick together and grow into planets, dwarf planets and asteroids, all held in orbit around the new star by gravity.", "from": "The Universe Summary + TECHNOLOGIES Powerpoint · slide 11"},
  {"id": "0260c1286033", "topic": "universe", "outcome": "uni-o4", "src": "img/slides/0260c1286033.webp", "caption": "Every star begins in a nebula. A low- or medium-mass star like the Sun goes main sequence → red giant → planetary nebula → white dwarf, while a high-mass star goes main sequence → red supergiant → supernova → neutron star or (if very massive) black hole.", "from": "Colours of Stars and their Age, Size, Distance from Earth and the HR Diagram · slide 4"},
  {"id": "ee6678d5868d", "topic": "universe", "outcome": "uni-o4", "src": "img/slides/ee6678d5868d.webp", "caption": "A detailed life cycle: a Sun-like star goes main sequence → red giant → planetary nebula → white dwarf, which slowly cools (a 'black dwarf' is predicted, but the universe is too young for any to exist yet). A more massive star becomes a supergiant and explodes as a supernova, leaving a neutron star or a black hole; the core does not really 'vanish', it collapses so densely that not even light can escape.", "from": "Major Features of the Universe · slide 10"},
  {"id": "c85910f9f8b6", "topic": "universe", "outcome": "uni-o4", "src": "img/slides/c85910f9f8b6.webp", "caption": "Artist's impression of a red giant. When the Sun runs out of hydrogen in its core (in about 5 billion years) it will swell into a huge, cooler, red star that fuses helium into carbon and oxygen.", "from": "Major Features of the Universe · slide 11"},
  {"id": "23959ff0113c", "topic": "universe", "outcome": "uni-o4", "src": "img/slides/23959ff0113c.webp", "caption": "The Helix Nebula, a <b>planetary nebula</b>: the glowing shell of gas puffed off by a dying Sun-like star, with the hot white-dwarf core at its centre. Despite the name, it has nothing to do with planets.", "from": "The Universe Summary + TECHNOLOGIES Powerpoint · slide 6"},
  {"id": "999c77adbbce", "topic": "universe", "outcome": "uni-o4", "src": "img/slides/999c77adbbce.webp", "caption": "A gallery of planetary nebulae shown at the same scale (scale bar = 4 light-years). Each is the cast-off outer layers of a dying Sun-like star, and some are light-years across.", "from": "The Universe Summary + TECHNOLOGIES Powerpoint · slide 5"},
  {"id": "1a502ca2ec70", "topic": "universe", "outcome": "uni-o5", "src": "img/slides/1a502ca2ec70.webp", "caption": "A massive star before (left) and the expanding supernova remnant after (right). When a supergiant runs out of fuel its core collapses and the outer layers are blasted into space, spreading heavy elements that later form new stars and planets.", "from": "Major Features of the Universe · slide 19"},
  {"id": "04cd34dcfc2e", "topic": "universe", "outcome": "uni-o5", "src": "img/slides/04cd34dcfc2e.webp", "caption": "The Crab Nebula, a <b>supernova remnant</b>: the expanding debris of a massive star whose explosion was recorded by astronomers in 1054 AD. A rapidly spinning neutron star (a pulsar) lies at its centre.", "from": "The Universe Summary + TECHNOLOGIES Powerpoint · slide 8"},
  {"id": "784b9b0786f0", "topic": "universe", "outcome": "uni-o5", "src": "img/slides/784b9b0786f0.webp", "caption": "Artist's impression of a neutron star, the collapsed core left after a supernova. It packs about 1.4–2 times the Sun's mass into a ball only about 20 km across, so a teaspoon of it would weigh billions of tonnes.", "from": "Major Features of the Universe · slide 20"},
  {"id": "ea429f9dbfdf", "topic": "universe", "outcome": "uni-o5", "src": "img/slides/ea429f9dbfdf.webp", "caption": "A black hole forms when the core of a very massive star collapses after a supernova. The <b>event horizon</b> is the boundary beyond which nothing, not even light, can escape; the <b>singularity</b> is the point at the centre where the mass is concentrated.", "from": "Big Bang Theory Summarised · slide 8"},
  {"id": "d45b23008527", "topic": "universe", "outcome": "uni-o7", "src": "img/slides/d45b23008527.webp", "caption": "The apparent magnitude scale runs 'backwards': the more negative the number, the brighter an object looks from Earth. The Sun (about −27) is brightest, the unaided eye can see down to about +6, and the James Webb Space Telescope can detect objects fainter than +29.", "from": "Colours of Stars and their Age, Size, Distance from Earth and the HR Diagram · slide 7"},
  {"id": "884aee5a62fb", "topic": "universe", "outcome": "uni-o7", "src": "img/slides/884aee5a62fb.webp", "caption": "Each step of 1 magnitude is about 2.5 times dimmer, so the scale is not linear. A magnitude 6 star is about 100 times dimmer than a magnitude 1 star (5 steps: 2.5<sup>5</sup> ≈ 100).", "from": "Colours of Stars and their Age, Size, Distance from Earth and the HR Diagram · slide 5"},
  {"id": "6d93494b1a35", "topic": "universe", "outcome": "uni-o8", "src": "img/slides/6d93494b1a35.webp", "caption": "Apparent magnitude (m) is how bright a star looks from Earth; absolute magnitude (M) is how bright it would look from a standard distance of 10 parsecs. Sirius (m = −1.5) looks about 4 times brighter than Rigel (m = +0.1) only because it is about 100 times closer; Rigel (M = −7.0) is really around 2000 times more luminous.", "from": "Colours of Stars and their Age, Size, Distance from Earth and the HR Diagram · slide 10"},
  {"id": "e3210b4be699", "topic": "universe", "outcome": "uni-o8", "src": "img/slides/e3210b4be699.webp", "caption": "The <b>inverse square law</b>: light from a star spreads over a larger area as it travels, so doubling the distance spreads it over 4× the area and the star looks ¼ as bright (3× the distance gives 1/9). This is why distance strongly affects how bright a star appears from Earth.", "from": "Colours of Stars and their Age, Size, Distance from Earth and the HR Diagram · slide 13"},
  {"id": "35332f335968", "topic": "universe", "outcome": "uni-o9", "src": "img/slides/35332f335968.webp", "caption": "The spectral classes O, B, A, F, G, K, M run from hottest (blue, about 30 000 K and above) to coolest (red, about 3 000 K). The Sun is a yellowish G-type star at about 5 800 K.", "from": "Colours of Stars and their Age, Size, Distance from Earth and the HR Diagram · slide 15"},
  {"id": "e20ab04fd4bf", "topic": "universe", "outcome": "uni-o9", "src": "img/slides/e20ab04fd4bf.webp", "caption": "PhET blackbody simulation: each curve shows how much light a star gives off at each wavelength. Hotter stars give off more light overall and their peak shifts to shorter (bluer) wavelengths, which is why hot stars look blue and cool stars look red.", "from": "Colours of Stars and their Age, Size, Distance from Earth and the HR Diagram · slide 22"},
  {"id": "465912b21301", "topic": "universe", "outcome": "uni-o11", "src": "img/slides/465912b21301.webp", "caption": "A Hertzsprung–Russell diagram plots absolute magnitude (brightness) against spectral type/temperature, with the hottest stars on the <b>left</b>. Most stars lie on the main sequence; giants and supergiants (top right) are cool but bright because they are huge, and white dwarfs (bottom left) are hot but dim because they are small.", "from": "Colours of Stars and their Age, Size, Distance from Earth and the HR Diagram · slide 17"},
  {"id": "bbd3d77172e5", "topic": "universe", "outcome": "uni-o11", "src": "img/slides/bbd3d77172e5.webp", "caption": "An H–R diagram with luminosity (Sun = 1) going up and temperature decreasing to the right; dot size shows star size. The Sun is an average main-sequence star, while supergiants like Betelgeuse and Antares are cooler than the Sun but tens of thousands of times more luminous.", "from": "Colours of Stars and their Age, Size, Distance from Earth and the HR Diagram · slide 9"},
  {"id": "232c91b6600d", "topic": "universe", "outcome": "uni-o11", "src": "img/slides/232c91b6600d.webp", "caption": "Along the main sequence, more massive stars are bigger, hotter, bluer and brighter, but they use up their fuel much faster. An O star (~40 000 K) lasts only about 10 million years, the Sun (G) about 10 billion years, and a small red M star over 100 billion years.", "from": "Colours of Stars and their Age, Size, Distance from Earth and the HR Diagram · slide 19"},
  {"id": "76287bfcfbac", "topic": "universe", "outcome": "uni-o11", "src": "img/slides/76287bfcfbac.webp", "caption": "Star sizes compared: the Sun is tiny next to Sirius, and the giant stars Pollux and Arcturus are many times wider still (at this scale Jupiter is about 1 pixel and Earth is invisible). Giants are bright despite their cooler surfaces because they are so large, which puts them at the top right of the H–R diagram.", "from": "Major Features of the Universe · slide 14"},
  {"id": "418ca5c0de37", "topic": "universe", "outcome": "uni-o13", "src": "img/slides/418ca5c0de37.webp", "caption": "The four main galaxy types: <b>spiral</b> (flat disc, central bulge, curved arms), <b>barred spiral</b> (arms start from the ends of a straight bar of stars across the centre), <b>elliptical</b> (smooth, rounded oval with no arms) and <b>irregular</b> (no definite shape).", "from": "Major Features of the Universe · slide 25"},
  {"id": "76262d7b4bef", "topic": "universe", "outcome": "uni-o13", "src": "img/slides/76262d7b4bef.webp", "caption": "Artist's impression of the Milky Way seen from above (we cannot photograph our own galaxy from outside). It is a barred spiral galaxy: notice the straight bar of stars through the bright centre, with spiral arms curving out from its ends.", "from": "The Universe Summary + TECHNOLOGIES Powerpoint · slide 18"},
  {"id": "da798f7fcbf3", "topic": "universe", "outcome": "uni-o13", "src": "img/slides/da798f7fcbf3.webp", "caption": "Galaxy type is linked to mass and angular momentum (spin): galaxies with more spin for their mass form flat discs with spiral arms, while those with little spin are rounded ellipticals. Lenticular galaxies are in between: disc-shaped but without clear arms.", "from": "The Universe Summary + TECHNOLOGIES Powerpoint · slide 17"},
  {"id": "b0a710daf69d", "topic": "universe", "outcome": "uni-o13", "src": "img/slides/b0a710daf69d.webp", "caption": "Illustrations of elliptical, irregular, spiral and barred spiral galaxies. Each type can look quite different depending on the angle we view it from, so classify by features (arms, bar, smooth oval or no shape), not just outline.", "from": "The Universe Summary + TECHNOLOGIES Powerpoint · slide 17"},
  {"id": "0dfeb5ddc5b3", "topic": "universe", "outcome": "uni-o14", "src": "img/slides/0dfeb5ddc5b3.webp", "caption": "A scale of distances in kilometres where each tick is 100× further than the last. Use kilometres for Earth-sized distances, astronomical units (AU) within the solar system, and light-years or parsecs for distances to stars and galaxies, so the numbers stay sensible.", "from": "Measuring Distances in Space · slide 10"},
  {"id": "f0c6afeb24c9", "topic": "universe", "outcome": "uni-o14", "src": "img/slides/f0c6afeb24c9.webp", "caption": "One <b>astronomical unit (AU)</b> is the average Earth–Sun distance, about 150 million km. It is the handy unit within the solar system: Mercury is 0.39 AU from the Sun, Jupiter 5.2 AU and Neptune about 30 AU.", "from": "Measuring Distances in Space · slide 8"},
  {"id": "4554f5bb5095", "topic": "universe", "outcome": "uni-o14", "src": "img/slides/4554f5bb5095.webp", "caption": "Light-travel distances from Earth: the Moon is 1.3 light-seconds away, the Sun 8 light-minutes, Proxima Centauri (the nearest star) 4.2 light-years and the Andromeda Galaxy 2.5 million light-years. Because light takes time to travel, we see distant objects as they were in the past.", "from": "Measuring Distances in Space · slide 9"},
  {"id": "8513d1505e99", "topic": "universe", "outcome": "uni-o14", "src": "img/slides/8513d1505e99.webp", "caption": "Proxima Centauri, a small red dwarf in the Alpha Centauri system, is the closest star to the Sun at about 4.24 light-years (diagram not to scale). It has an exoplanet, Proxima Centauri b, in its habitable zone.", "from": "The Universe Summary + TECHNOLOGIES Powerpoint · slide 15"},
  {"id": "cb82a265b68c", "topic": "universe", "outcome": "uni-o14", "src": "img/slides/cb82a265b68c.webp", "caption": "How the AU was measured accurately: a radar signal is bounced off Venus when it is closest to Earth (0.3 AU away). Timing the echo and using the speed of light gives the Earth–Venus distance in km, which is then scaled up to find 1 AU ≈ 150 million km.", "from": "The Universe Summary + TECHNOLOGIES Powerpoint · slide 28"},
  {"id": "da86655caf14", "topic": "universe", "outcome": "uni-o16", "src": "img/slides/da86655caf14.webp", "caption": "<b>Stellar parallax</b>: a nearby star is observed from opposite sides of Earth's orbit, 6 months apart, and appears to shift against the much more distant background stars. The smaller the parallax angle <i>p</i>, the further away the star (distance <i>d</i>).", "from": "The Universe Summary + TECHNOLOGIES Powerpoint · slide 33"},
  {"id": "f760c76848e1", "topic": "universe", "outcome": "uni-o16", "src": "img/slides/f760c76848e1.webp", "caption": "Parallax seen from Earth in January and July: the nearby star (yellow) appears in a different position against the fixed distant stars. Measuring this shift gives the star's distance, but the method only works for fairly nearby stars because the shift becomes too small to measure for distant ones.", "from": "The Universe Summary + TECHNOLOGIES Powerpoint · slide 33"},
  {"id": "7462c21ce60e", "topic": "universe", "outcome": "uni-o16", "src": "img/slides/7462c21ce60e.webp", "caption": "The thumb demonstration of parallax: seen with the left eye and then the right eye, your thumb appears to jump against the distant background. Closer objects shift more, just as nearby stars shift more than distant ones.", "from": "The Universe Summary + TECHNOLOGIES Powerpoint · slide 32"},
  {"id": "986b15e10cf6", "topic": "universe", "outcome": "uni-o16", "src": "img/slides/986b15e10cf6.webp", "caption": "Definition of a <b>parsec</b>: the distance at which 1 AU makes an angle of 1 arcsecond (1/3600 of a degree). A star with a parallax angle of 1 arcsecond is 1 parsec away; 1 parsec ≈ 3.26 light-years.", "from": "The Universe Summary + TECHNOLOGIES Powerpoint · slide 31"},
  {"id": "5c9c93f2cf97", "topic": "universe", "outcome": "uni-o17", "src": "img/slides/5c9c93f2cf97.webp", "caption": "A reflecting telescope: a large curved mirror gathers light and reflects it to a small mirror, which sends the image into the eyepiece. Bigger mirrors collect more light, so astronomers can see fainter, more distant objects.", "from": "The Universe Summary + TECHNOLOGIES Powerpoint · slide 37"},
  {"id": "626d2b52c530", "topic": "universe", "outcome": "uni-o17", "src": "img/slides/626d2b52c530.webp", "caption": "A telescope like the one Galileo built: a simple refracting telescope using lenses. From 1609 Galileo was the first to study the sky scientifically with a telescope, discovering craters on the Moon, the phases of Venus and four moons of Jupiter.", "from": "The Universe Summary + TECHNOLOGIES Powerpoint · slide 35"},
  {"id": "9bd16d24c744", "topic": "universe", "outcome": "uni-o17", "src": "img/slides/9bd16d24c744.webp", "caption": "Jupiter with four of its moons (Io, Europa, Ganymede and Callisto) through a small telescope, the view Galileo recorded night after night in 1610. Seeing bodies orbit Jupiter showed that not everything orbits Earth, supporting the Sun-centred model.", "from": "The Universe Summary + TECHNOLOGIES Powerpoint · slide 36"},
  {"id": "4ee0523b25b0", "topic": "universe", "outcome": "uni-o17", "src": "img/slides/4ee0523b25b0.webp", "caption": "The Australia Telescope Compact Array near Narrabri, NSW: six radio dishes linked to work together as one large telescope. Radio telescopes detect radio waves from cold gas, galaxies and pulsars, and work day or night, even through cloud.", "from": "The Universe Summary + TECHNOLOGIES Powerpoint · slide 38"},
  {"id": "f99a466c8d3e", "topic": "universe", "outcome": "uni-o17", "src": "img/slides/f99a466c8d3e.webp", "caption": "The Whirlpool Galaxy seen in radio, infrared, visible, ultraviolet and X-ray light. Telescopes detecting different parts of the electromagnetic spectrum reveal different things: cold gas (radio), cool stars and dust (infrared), hot young stars (ultraviolet) and very hot gas (X-rays).", "from": "The Universe Summary + TECHNOLOGIES Powerpoint · slide 42"},
  {"id": "bda9efd453b6", "topic": "universe", "outcome": "uni-o17", "src": "img/slides/bda9efd453b6.webp", "caption": "Pluto as seen by the Hubble Space Telescope (left) and up close by the New Horizons spacecraft in 2015 (right). Space probes that travel to an object reveal far more detail than even the best telescopes.", "from": "The Universe Summary + TECHNOLOGIES Powerpoint · slide 41"},
  {"id": "c513aee77a51", "topic": "universe", "outcome": "uni-o17", "src": "img/slides/c513aee77a51.webp", "caption": "A NASA rover on Mars (a 'selfie'). Planetary rovers carry cameras and chemical-analysis instruments to study the rocks, soil and atmosphere of other worlds up close.", "from": "The Universe Summary + TECHNOLOGIES Powerpoint · slide 44"},
  {"id": "aac7cc755805", "topic": "universe", "outcome": "uni-o18", "src": "img/slides/aac7cc755805.webp", "caption": "Timeline of the Big Bang: inflation in the first tiny fraction of a second, then quarks combine into protons and neutrons, nuclei form within minutes, atoms after about 380 000 years, and gravity later builds stars and galaxies. As the universe expanded and cooled, energy was converted into matter (E = mc<sup>2</sup>); note the present day is now dated at about 13.8 billion years, not the 15 billion shown.", "from": "Big Bang Theory Summarised · slide 1"},
  {"id": "bdac2c0db5b8", "topic": "universe", "outcome": "uni-o18", "src": "img/slides/bdac2c0db5b8.webp", "caption": "Hydrogen (1 proton, 1 electron) and helium (2 protons, 2 neutrons, 2 electrons) were the first atoms. They formed about 380 000 years after the Big Bang, once the universe had cooled enough for nuclei to hold on to electrons.", "from": "Big Bang Theory Summarised · slide 4"},
  {"id": "ee3156f13edb", "topic": "universe", "outcome": "uni-o18", "src": "img/slides/ee3156f13edb.webp", "caption": "Artist's impression of the first stars and galaxies, which formed a few hundred million years after the Big Bang as gravity pulled clouds of hydrogen and helium together.", "from": "Big Bang Theory Summarised · slide 5"},
  {"id": "4a6cd959d081", "topic": "universe", "outcome": "uni-o18", "src": "img/slides/4a6cd959d081.webp", "caption": "Artist's impression of a quasar: an extremely bright galaxy core, powered by gas falling into a supermassive black hole, that outshines its whole galaxy. Quasars are only found very far away (so we see them as they were long ago), which is evidence that the universe has changed over time, as the Big Bang theory predicts.", "from": "Big Bang Theory Summarised · slide 12"},
  {"id": "06e72b5ef691", "topic": "universe", "outcome": "uni-o19", "src": "img/slides/06e72b5ef691.webp", "caption": "A prism splits white light into a continuous spectrum from red to violet. Astronomers spread starlight out in the same way: dark absorption lines reveal a star's elements, and a shift of those lines towards red (away) or blue (towards) shows how the star or galaxy is moving.", "from": "Big Bang Theory Summarised · slide 10"},
  {"id": "f5791f5118d2", "topic": "universe", "outcome": "uni-o20", "src": "img/slides/f5791f5118d2.webp", "caption": "Hubble's graph of galaxies' recession velocity against distance. The straight-line trend (Hubble's law, v = H<sub>0</sub> × d) shows that the further away a galaxy is, the faster it is moving away (the more red-shifted its light), which is evidence that the universe is expanding.", "from": "Big Bang Theory Summarised · slide 10"},
  {"id": "98353797f69f", "topic": "universe", "outcome": "uni-o21", "src": "img/slides/98353797f69f.webp", "caption": "Map of the <b>cosmic microwave background radiation (CMBR)</b> across the whole sky, made by the WMAP satellite. The CMBR is the leftover heat of the Big Bang, now cooled to about 2.7 K; the colours show tiny temperature differences (about 1 part in 100 000) that later grew into galaxies.", "from": "The Universe Summary + TECHNOLOGIES Powerpoint · slide 21"},
  {"id": "f1f9889eb9ee", "topic": "universe", "outcome": "uni-o21", "src": "img/slides/f1f9889eb9ee.webp", "caption": "Looking out into space is looking back in time: the Big Bang, then the cosmic microwave background released at 'recombination' (about 380 000 years, when atoms formed and light could travel freely), the dark ages, and finally the first stars and galaxies. The CMBR is the oldest light we can detect, and its discovery in 1965 by Penzias and Wilson supported the Big Bang over the steady state theory.", "from": "Big Bang Theory Summarised · slide 11"},
  {"id": "96b46ea27b93", "topic": "chemistry", "outcome": "chem-pre", "src": "img/slides/96b46ea27b93.webp", "caption": "Test for carbon dioxide: bubbling CO<sub>2</sub> through clear limewater (calcium hydroxide solution) turns it milky. Acids react with carbonates and hydrogen carbonates to release CO<sub>2</sub>, so this test identifies them.", "from": "Chemical Reactions · slide 19"},
  {"id": "21d1ddc87628", "topic": "chemistry", "outcome": "chem-pre", "src": "img/slides/21d1ddc87628.webp", "caption": "Acid + metal pattern: zinc + hydrochloric acid → zinc chloride + hydrogen, Zn + 2HCl → ZnCl<sub>2</sub> + H<sub>2</sub>. Reactive metals react with acids to give a salt and hydrogen gas (squeaky pop test).", "from": "Chemical Reactions · slide 11"},
  {"id": "80fd8c79d966", "topic": "chemistry", "outcome": "chem-pre", "src": "img/slides/80fd8c79d966.webp", "caption": "Conservation of mass in 2H<sub>2</sub> + O<sub>2</sub> → 2H<sub>2</sub>O: there are 4 hydrogen atoms and 2 oxygen atoms on each side. Atoms are only rearranged, never created or destroyed, which is why equations must be balanced.", "from": "Chemical Reactions · slide 7"},
  {"id": "0b5adb076c95", "topic": "chemistry", "outcome": "chem-o1", "src": "img/slides/0b5adb076c95.webp", "caption": "Energy changes form: chemical energy is converted to electrical energy (battery), thermal energy (fire) and light (chemiluminescence), and photosynthesis stores radiant energy as chemical energy. Chemical reactions always involve a transfer of energy.", "from": "Energy Transformations · slide 9"},
  {"id": "5a115e9f06f2", "topic": "chemistry", "outcome": "chem-o1", "src": "img/slides/5a115e9f06f2.webp", "caption": "The <b>system</b> is the reacting chemicals and the <b>surroundings</b> are everything else. Exothermic reactions release heat to the surroundings (ΔH negative); endothermic reactions absorb heat from the surroundings (ΔH positive).", "from": "Energy Transfer and Rates of Reaction · slide 26"},
  {"id": "6827be91f489", "topic": "chemistry", "outcome": "chem-o2", "src": "img/slides/6827be91f489.webp", "caption": "Endothermic: heat flows <i>into</i> the reaction from the surroundings, so the surroundings feel colder. Exothermic: heat flows <i>out</i> to the surroundings, so they feel hotter. Energy is conserved in both.", "from": "Energy Transfer and Rates of Reaction · slide 17"},
  {"id": "3a3f87212df0", "topic": "chemistry", "outcome": "chem-o2", "src": "img/slides/3a3f87212df0.webp", "caption": "Endothermic (ΔH &gt; 0): energy is a reactant and the surroundings get cooler. Exothermic (ΔH &lt; 0): energy is a product and the surroundings get warmer. Note: the slide's endothermic equation 2H<sub>2</sub>O → 2H<sub>2</sub> + O<sub>2</sub> is the decomposition of water, not melting (melting is a physical change), and the combustion of ethene balances as C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>4</sub> + 3O<sub>2</sub> → 2CO<sub>2</sub> + 2H<sub>2</sub>O.", "from": "Energy Transfer and Rates of Reaction · slide 16"},
  {"id": "e6452341b7c2", "topic": "chemistry", "outcome": "chem-o2", "src": "img/slides/e6452341b7c2.webp", "caption": "Breaking bonds absorbs energy (endothermic); making bonds releases energy (exothermic). A reaction is exothermic overall if more energy is released forming product bonds than is absorbed breaking reactant bonds, and endothermic if the reverse.", "from": "Energy Transfer and Rates of Reaction · slide 18"},
  {"id": "c5dd7186a680", "topic": "chemistry", "outcome": "chem-o3", "src": "img/slides/c5dd7186a680.webp", "caption": "Labelled energy profiles. Activation energy is measured from the reactants up to the peak. In an endothermic reaction the products are higher than the reactants (energy absorbed); in an exothermic reaction they are lower (energy released).", "from": "Energy Transfer and Rates of Reaction · slide 24"},
  {"id": "a3ea3c384114", "topic": "chemistry", "outcome": "chem-o3", "src": "img/slides/a3ea3c384114.webp", "caption": "Exothermic vs endothermic energy profiles. Blue arrow = activation energy (reactants to peak); red arrow = overall energy released (exothermic) or absorbed (endothermic) between the reactant and product levels.", "from": "Energy Transfer and Rates of Reaction · slide 22"},
  {"id": "a70b5e2320e8", "topic": "chemistry", "outcome": "chem-o3", "src": "img/slides/a70b5e2320e8.webp", "caption": "Analogy for activation energy: getting out of bed is the hardest part. The peak of the curve is the <b>transition state</b>, an unstable high-energy arrangement of atoms; reactants need at least the activation energy to reach it.", "from": "Energy Transfer and Rates of Reaction · slide 6"},
  {"id": "b9e5545b6547", "topic": "chemistry", "outcome": "chem-o4", "src": "img/slides/b9e5545b6547.webp", "caption": "Collision theory: particles only react if they collide with the correct orientation <b>and</b> with energy greater than or equal to the activation energy. Otherwise they bounce apart unchanged.", "from": "Energy Transfer and Rates of Reaction · slide 4"},
  {"id": "27a79402f23f", "topic": "chemistry", "outcome": "chem-o4", "src": "img/slides/27a79402f23f.webp", "caption": "Distribution of particle energies. Only the particles in the shaded tail to the right of the activation energy line have enough energy to react when they collide; most particles do not.", "from": "Energy Transfer and Rates of Reaction · slide 4"},
  {"id": "03153693596c", "topic": "chemistry", "outcome": "chem-o5", "src": "img/slides/03153693596c.webp", "caption": "As the reaction A → B proceeds, reactant A (purple) decreases and product B (green) increases. The curves are steepest at the start, so the rate is fastest at the beginning and slows as A is used up.", "from": "Energy Transfer and Rates of Reaction · slide 2"},
  {"id": "839059a1464b", "topic": "chemistry", "outcome": "chem-o5", "src": "img/slides/839059a1464b.webp", "caption": "Concentration–time graph: reactant (reagent) concentration falls and product concentration rises. Rate = change in concentration ÷ time, which is the gradient; both curves level off when the reaction stops.", "from": "Energy Transfer and Rates of Reaction · slide 3"},
  {"id": "11adb7ec05b7", "topic": "chemistry", "outcome": "chem-o6", "src": "img/slides/11adb7ec05b7.webp", "caption": "Effect of temperature: at a higher temperature the energy curve flattens and shifts right, so a larger fraction of particles has energy above the activation (threshold) energy. Particles also collide more often, so the rate increases.", "from": "Energy Transfer and Rates of Reaction · slide 8"},
  {"id": "5ece1c2c8699", "topic": "chemistry", "outcome": "chem-o6", "src": "img/slides/5ece1c2c8699.webp", "caption": "Effect of surface area: acid particles (red) can only hit the outside of a magnesium lump. Cutting it into smaller pieces exposes more atoms, so there are more collisions per second and a faster rate.", "from": "Energy Transfer and Rates of Reaction · slide 9"},
  {"id": "d1588f5cb991", "topic": "chemistry", "outcome": "chem-o6", "src": "img/slides/d1588f5cb991.webp", "caption": "Effect of concentration: more reactant particles in the same volume means more frequent collisions, so the reaction goes faster.", "from": "Energy Transfer and Rates of Reaction · slide 10"},
  {"id": "7ca764dac437", "topic": "chemistry", "outcome": "chem-o7", "src": "img/slides/7ca764dac437.webp", "caption": "A catalyst provides an alternative pathway with a lower activation energy (E<sub>a1</sub> &lt; E<sub>a2</sub>). The reactant and product energies, and so the overall energy change, are unchanged.", "from": "Energy Transfer and Rates of Reaction · slide 34"},
  {"id": "910e2c7d2d63", "topic": "chemistry", "outcome": "chem-o7", "src": "img/slides/910e2c7d2d63.webp", "caption": "Surface catalyst: reactant particles attach to the catalyst surface, which holds them close together in a good orientation to react. The product then leaves, and the catalyst is unchanged and reused.", "from": "Energy Transfer and Rates of Reaction · slide 33"},
  {"id": "6b70aebae23d", "topic": "chemistry", "outcome": "chem-o7", "src": "img/slides/6b70aebae23d.webp", "caption": "Catalysts speed up a reaction and are not used up. Correction to the slide: a catalyst lowers the activation <b>energy</b> (not the activation 'rate'), and the graph's axes should be energy (y) against reaction progress (x), not activation energy against time.", "from": "Energy Transfer and Rates of Reaction · slide 32"},
  {"id": "d6b67dfc852a", "topic": "chemistry", "outcome": "chem-o8", "src": "img/slides/d6b67dfc852a.webp", "caption": "Lock-and-key model: the substrate fits the enzyme's specifically shaped active site to form an enzyme–substrate complex. The products are released and the enzyme is unchanged, ready to be used again.", "from": "Energy Transfer and Rates of Reaction · slide 35"},
  {"id": "5dcf7e0a707a", "topic": "chemistry", "outcome": "chem-o8", "src": "img/slides/5dcf7e0a707a.webp", "caption": "Enzymes are biological catalysts: the enzyme-catalysed pathway (right) has a much lower activation energy than the uncatalysed pathway (left), so reactions in cells happen quickly at body temperature.", "from": "Energy Transfer and Rates of Reaction · slide 5"},
  {"id": "b06886f4eabb", "topic": "chemistry", "outcome": "chem-o8", "src": "img/slides/b06886f4eabb.webp", "caption": "Digestive enzymes are specific: carbohydrases (e.g. amylase, lactase) break carbohydrates into sugars, proteases (pepsin, trypsin) break proteins into amino acids, and lipase breaks fats into fatty acids (and glycerol).", "from": "Energy Transfer and Rates of Reaction · slide 36"},
  {"id": "ed7c61cc4064", "topic": "chemistry", "outcome": "chem-o10", "src": "img/slides/ed7c61cc4064.webp", "caption": "Redox: A loses electrons (is <b>oxidised</b>) and B gains them (is <b>reduced</b>). A is the reducing agent and B is the oxidising agent. Remember OIL RIG: Oxidation Is Loss, Reduction Is Gain of electrons.", "from": "REDOX (Oxidation numbers) · page 1"},
  {"id": "a248638c49d5", "topic": "chemistry", "outcome": "chem-o10", "src": "img/slides/a248638c49d5.webp", "caption": "Using oxidation numbers: Mn goes from +7 in MnO<sub>4</sub><sup>−</sup> to +4 in MnO<sub>2</sub> (decrease = reduction), and I goes from −1 in I<sup>−</sup> to 0 in I<sub>2</sub> (increase = oxidation).", "from": "REDOX (Oxidation numbers) · page 8"},
  {"id": "3b5ad463fba7", "topic": "chemistry", "outcome": "chem-o10", "src": "img/slides/3b5ad463fba7.webp", "caption": "Oxidation numbers of each element in common substances: elements are 0, O is usually −2, H is usually +1, and the numbers add up to the overall charge. Corrections: in NH<sub>3</sub>, N is <b>−3</b> and H is <b>+1</b> (the slide shows +3 and −1). An oxidation number is the charge an atom would have if all its bonds were ionic.", "from": "REDOX (Oxidation numbers) · page 2"},
  {"id": "6bd9665127e8", "topic": "chemistry", "outcome": "chem-o10b", "src": "img/slides/6bd9665127e8.webp", "caption": "Zinc–copper galvanic cell: Zn (anode, −) is oxidised, Zn → Zn<sup>2+</sup> + 2e<sup>−</sup>; electrons flow through the wire to Cu (cathode, +), where Cu<sup>2+</sup> + 2e<sup>−</sup> → Cu. In the NaCl salt bridge, Cl<sup>−</sup> moves to the anode side and Na<sup>+</sup> to the cathode side. Photo (b): the zinc anode is eaten away while copper is deposited on the cathode.", "from": "REDOX (Galvanic Cells) · page 5"},
  {"id": "a07e870688d4", "topic": "chemistry", "outcome": "chem-o10b", "src": "img/slides/a07e870688d4.webp", "caption": "Copper–silver cell: Cu is more reactive, so it is the anode (oxidised to Cu<sup>2+</sup>), and electrons flow through the wire to the silver cathode, where Ag<sup>+</sup> is reduced to Ag. In the KNO<sub>3</sub> salt bridge, NO<sub>3</sub><sup>−</sup> moves toward the anode and K<sup>+</sup> toward the cathode to keep each solution neutral.", "from": "REDOX (Galvanic Cells) · page 6"},
  {"id": "2c16c1e55dce", "topic": "chemistry", "outcome": "chem-o10b", "src": "img/slides/2c16c1e55dce.webp", "caption": "Zn–Cu cell with half-equations and shorthand notation Zn(s) | Zn<sup>2+</sup>(aq) || Cu<sup>2+</sup>(aq) | Cu(s). The anode (oxidation) is written on the left, the cathode (reduction) on the right, and || is the salt bridge.", "from": "REDOX (Galvanic Cells) · page 9"},
  {"id": "d376b73a5da3", "topic": "chemistry", "outcome": "chem-o10b", "src": "img/slides/d376b73a5da3.webp", "caption": "Voltaic (galvanic) cell: zinc in ZnSO<sub>4</sub> is the anode (oxidation half-cell) and copper in CuSO<sub>4</sub> is the cathode (reduction half-cell). The salt bridge completes the circuit by letting ions move between the half-cells.", "from": "REDOX (Galvanic Cells) · page 3"},
  {"id": "6988e611a9ba", "topic": "chemistry", "outcome": "chem-o10b", "src": "img/slides/6988e611a9ba.webp", "caption": "Practice cell: a lead electrode in Pb<sup>2+</sup> solution and a zinc electrode in Zn<sup>2+</sup> solution, joined by a salt bridge and voltmeter. Zinc is more reactive, so it is the anode (−), lead is the cathode (+), and electrons flow from Zn to Pb.", "from": "REDOX (Galvanic Cells) · page 15"},
  {"id": "e9f41f3c5b7a", "topic": "chemistry", "outcome": "chem-o10b", "src": "img/slides/e9f41f3c5b7a.webp", "caption": "Worked example Cu–Ag cell: Cu → Cu<sup>2+</sup> + 2e<sup>−</sup> at the copper anode (−) and Ag<sup>+</sup> + e<sup>−</sup> → Ag at the silver electrode, so overall Cu + 2Ag<sup>+</sup> → Cu<sup>2+</sup> + 2Ag. Correction: the slide labels silver as an 'anode', but silver is the <b>cathode</b>.", "from": "REDOX (Galvanic Cells) · page 8"},
  {"id": "185e142930d4", "topic": "chemistry", "outcome": "chem-o10b", "src": "img/slides/185e142930d4.webp", "caption": "Worked example Mg–Ag cell: magnesium (more reactive) is the anode, Mg → Mg<sup>2+</sup> + 2e<sup>−</sup>. Silver is the cathode, Ag<sup>+</sup> + e<sup>−</sup> → Ag. Electrons flow from Mg to Ag through the wire, and the cell is written Mg | Mg<sup>2+</sup> || Ag<sup>+</sup> | Ag.", "from": "REDOX (Galvanic Cells) · page 12"},
  {"id": "60ec0e8d0f54", "topic": "chemistry", "outcome": "chem-o10b", "src": "img/slides/60ec0e8d0f54.webp", "caption": "Worked example Al–Fe cell: aluminium is the anode, Al → Al<sup>3+</sup> + 3e<sup>−</sup>, and iron is the cathode, Fe<sup>2+</sup> + 2e<sup>−</sup> → Fe; overall 2Al + 3Fe<sup>2+</sup> → 2Al<sup>3+</sup> + 3Fe. Corrections: the cathode is <b>iron</b> (the slide says silver), and electrons are written e<sup>−</sup>, not e<sup>−</sup>(aq).", "from": "REDOX (Galvanic Cells) · page 14"},
  {"id": "da9b050f9998", "topic": "chemistry", "outcome": "chem-o10b", "src": "img/slides/da9b050f9998.webp", "caption": "A dry cell (common battery) is a galvanic cell: the zinc case is the anode (oxidised) and the graphite rod is the cathode, surrounded by a moist paste of MnO<sub>2</sub>, NH<sub>4</sub>Cl and carbon as the electrolyte.", "from": "REDOX (Galvanic Cells) · page 2"},
  {"id": "c9ff8c3ba278", "topic": "chemistry", "outcome": "chem-o10b", "src": "img/slides/c9ff8c3ba278.webp", "caption": "Electrolytic cell (electrolysis of water): a power supply forces a non-spontaneous reaction, turning electrical energy into chemical energy. Hydrogen forms at the negative cathode (reduction) and oxygen at the positive anode (oxidation), in a 2:1 volume ratio. In a galvanic cell the anode is negative instead.", "from": "Combustion, Decomposition, Biological Reactions · slide 17"},
  {"id": "ac0f6a5c8919", "topic": "chemistry", "outcome": "chem-o11", "src": "img/slides/ac0f6a5c8919.webp", "caption": "Rusting is a redox reaction: iron is oxidised (Fe → Fe<sup>2+</sup>/Fe<sup>3+</sup>, losing electrons) while oxygen is reduced (gaining electrons). Water must also be present for iron to corrode.", "from": "REDOX (Oxidation numbers) · page 8"},
  {"id": "f37f6f72262c", "topic": "reactions", "outcome": "rxn-n1", "src": "img/slides/f37f6f72262c.webp", "caption": "A sodium atom (2,8,1) loses one electron to become Na<sup>+</sup> (2,8), and a chlorine atom (2,8,7) gains one electron to become Cl<sup>−</sup> (2,8,8). Metals form positive ions (cations) and non-metals form negative ions (anions).", "from": "Naming compounds · slide 2"},
  {"id": "d8ad818db84c", "topic": "reactions", "outcome": "rxn-n1", "src": "img/slides/d8ad818db84c.webp", "caption": "Ionic compounds form by transfer of electrons: NaCl (1 Na : 1 Cl), MgCl<sub>2</sub> (one Mg gives an electron to each of two Cl atoms) and Na<sub>2</sub>O (two Na each give one electron to one O). The ratio balances the charges.", "from": "Naming compounds · slide 5"},
  {"id": "2afd156edb77", "topic": "reactions", "outcome": "rxn-n1", "src": "img/slides/2afd156edb77.webp", "caption": "Covalent bonding: non-metal atoms share electron pairs to fill their outer shells. Cl<sub>2</sub> and HCl have single bonds, O<sub>2</sub> a double bond, N<sub>2</sub> a triple bond, and CO<sub>2</sub> two double bonds (O=C=O).", "from": "Naming compounds · slide 41"},
  {"id": "aeb1d68dd457", "topic": "reactions", "outcome": "rxn-n1", "src": "img/slides/aeb1d68dd457.webp", "caption": "Prefixes for naming covalent compounds: mono 1, di 2, tri 3, tetra 4, penta 5, hexa 6, hepta 7, octa 8, nona 9, deca 10. For example, N<sub>2</sub>O<sub>4</sub> is dinitrogen tetroxide; mono is left off the first element (CO<sub>2</sub> is carbon dioxide).", "from": "Naming compounds · slide 45"},
  {"id": "e5601c6d758a", "topic": "reactions", "outcome": "rxn-n3", "src": "img/slides/e5601c6d758a.webp", "caption": "Particle model of H<sub>2</sub> + Cl<sub>2</sub> → 2HCl: one hydrogen molecule and one chlorine molecule make two hydrogen chloride molecules, so the number of each type of atom is the same on both sides.", "from": "Chemical Reactions · slide 9"},
  {"id": "cd818df7fb77", "topic": "reactions", "outcome": "rxn-n4", "src": "img/slides/cd818df7fb77.webp", "caption": "pH ranges where common indicators change colour: methyl orange red→yellow (about 3–4.4), litmus red→blue (about 5–8), bromothymol blue yellow→blue (6–7.6), phenolphthalein colourless→pink (8.3–10). Universal indicator changes gradually from red through green to purple across the whole scale.", "from": "Investigating indicators · slide 8"},
  {"id": "4d36bafcc17c", "topic": "reactions", "outcome": "rxn-n4", "src": "img/slides/4d36bafcc17c.webp", "caption": "Indicator table: each indicator has a colour below and above its pH range. Using several indicators together narrows down the pH of an unknown solution.", "from": "Investigating indicators · slide 9"},
  {"id": "663727c0e9ae", "topic": "reactions", "outcome": "rxn-n4", "src": "img/slides/663727c0e9ae.webp", "caption": "The pH scale runs 0–14: below 7 is acidic (stomach acid, lemon, vinegar), 7 is neutral (pure water) and above 7 is alkaline (baking soda, ammonia, bleach). Each pH unit is a 10-fold change in H<sup>+</sup> concentration; blood is slightly alkaline at about 7.4.", "from": "Investigating indicators · slide 2"},
  {"id": "6087bfb831dc", "topic": "reactions", "outcome": "rxn-n4", "src": "img/slides/6087bfb831dc.webp", "caption": "Strength vs concentration: a strong acid (e.g. HCl) fully ionises into H<sup>+</sup> and Cl<sup>−</sup>, while a weak acid (e.g. CH<sub>3</sub>COOH) only partly ionises, so most of it stays as molecules. Concentration is how much acid is dissolved in a given volume.", "from": "Investigating indicators · slide 5"},
  {"id": "a42357e15c0e", "topic": "reactions", "outcome": "rxn-n4", "src": "img/slides/a42357e15c0e.webp", "caption": "Acid rain damage: limestone and marble (calcium carbonate) statues are worn away because acids react with metal carbonates, acid + carbonate → salt + water + carbon dioxide.", "from": "Chemical Reactions · slide 16"},
  {"id": "3d78fbf1ddc4", "topic": "reactions", "outcome": "rxn-n5", "src": "img/slides/3d78fbf1ddc4.webp", "caption": "Bunsen burner: with the air hole closed there is less oxygen, so <b>incomplete</b> combustion gives a yellow, sooty safety flame. With the air hole open, <b>complete</b> combustion gives a hot blue flame.", "from": "Combustion, Decomposition, Biological Reactions · slide 8"},
  {"id": "7a5c8920c24a", "topic": "reactions", "outcome": "rxn-n5", "src": "img/slides/7a5c8920c24a.webp", "caption": "Complete combustion: fuel + oxygen → carbon dioxide + water. Incomplete combustion (limited oxygen) can also produce carbon monoxide and carbon (soot).", "from": "Combustion, Decomposition, Biological Reactions · slide 6"},
  {"id": "6d5ce2665bf5", "topic": "reactions", "outcome": "rxn-n5", "src": "img/slides/6d5ce2665bf5.webp", "caption": "Why incomplete combustion matters: carbon monoxide is breathed in and binds to haemoglobin in red blood cells in place of oxygen, which reduces oxygen transport and can be fatal.", "from": "Combustion, Decomposition, Biological Reactions · slide 15"},
  {"id": "e39228c11a25", "topic": "reactions", "outcome": "rxn-n5", "src": "img/slides/e39228c11a25.webp", "caption": "A precipitation reaction: mixing two clear solutions produces a coloured insoluble solid (a precipitate) that clouds the liquid and settles out.", "from": "Precipitation · slide 5"},
  {"id": "cc0dd9569d33", "topic": "reactions", "outcome": "rxn-n5", "src": "img/slides/cc0dd9569d33.webp", "caption": "A white precipitate settling at the bottom of a test tube. A precipitate is an insoluble solid formed when two soluble ionic solutions react.", "from": "Precipitation · slide 4"},
  {"id": "740e5848f30e", "topic": "reactions", "outcome": "rxn-n5", "src": "img/slides/740e5848f30e.webp", "caption": "Solubility rules used to predict precipitates: all nitrates, ammonium and Group 1 salts are soluble; most chlorides and sulfates are soluble (except e.g. AgCl, BaSO<sub>4</sub>); most hydroxides, carbonates and phosphates are insoluble. Correction: in the hydroxide row the exceptions should read 'Group 1 hydroxides are soluble'; the table repeats (NH<sub>4</sub>)<sub>2</sub>CO<sub>3</sub> from the carbonate row.", "from": "Energy Transfer and Rates of Reaction · slide 31"},
  {"id": "7c26330dcb59", "topic": "reactions", "outcome": "rxn-n5", "src": "img/slides/7c26330dcb59.webp", "caption": "Soluble substances form clear solutions, which may be coloured but are never cloudy. Cloudiness or a solid settling out shows something is insoluble.", "from": "Precipitation · slide 2"},
  {"id": "6909bb64eba4", "topic": "reactions", "outcome": "rxn-n6", "src": "img/slides/6909bb64eba4.webp", "caption": "Thermal decomposition: heating green copper(II) carbonate gives black copper(II) oxide and carbon dioxide, CuCO<sub>3</sub> → CuO + CO<sub>2</sub>. The CO<sub>2</sub> turns the limewater milky.", "from": "Combustion, Decomposition, Biological Reactions · slide 20"},
  {"id": "139a309194f8", "topic": "reactions", "outcome": "rxn-n6", "src": "img/slides/139a309194f8.webp", "caption": "Photosynthesis (6CO<sub>2</sub> + 6H<sub>2</sub>O → C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>12</sub>O<sub>6</sub> + 6O<sub>2</sub>) uses solar energy and is endothermic. Cellular respiration is the reverse reaction and releases chemical energy (ATP) and heat.", "from": "Combustion, Decomposition, Biological Reactions · slide 21"},
  {"id": "92d68608f75f", "topic": "global", "outcome": "glo-o1", "src": "img/slides/92d68608f75f.webp", "caption": "Earth's four spheres: the <b>atmosphere</b> (air), <b>hydrosphere</b> (all water, including ice and groundwater), <b>lithosphere</b> (crust and rigid upper mantle) and <b>biosphere</b> (all living things). Global systems such as cycles, weather and natural disasters work through interactions between these spheres.", "from": "Earth's Spheres · slide 8"},
  {"id": "2247de1aacec", "topic": "global", "outcome": "glo-o1", "src": "img/slides/2247de1aacec.webp", "caption": "The spheres constantly exchange matter and energy, with the biosphere in the middle depending on all of them. For example, water evaporates from the hydrosphere into the atmosphere, rain weathers the lithosphere, and plants take CO<sub>2</sub> from the air and nutrients from the soil.", "from": "Earth's Spheres · slide 3"},
  {"id": "31436c93bcd4", "topic": "global", "outcome": "glo-o1", "src": "img/slides/31436c93bcd4.webp", "caption": "The lithosphere is the rigid outer shell of Earth, about 100 km thick, made of the crust plus the uppermost solid mantle; it sits on the softer asthenosphere, and its plates drive tectonics. The diagram's \"Crust 0–100 km\" label really gives the depth of the whole lithosphere: the crust itself is only about 5–10 km thick under oceans and 30–70 km under continents.", "from": "Earth's Spheres · slide 5"},
  {"id": "2e451c5ff665", "topic": "global", "outcome": "glo-o1", "src": "img/slides/2e451c5ff665.webp", "caption": "How the hydrosphere's water is shared out: 97% is salt water in the oceans and only 3% is fresh. Most fresh water is locked in ice caps and glaciers (68.7%) or underground (30.1%), and only 0.3% is surface water in lakes, swamps and rivers.", "from": "Earth's Spheres · slide 6"},
  {"id": "f980ca0dc6f9", "topic": "global", "outcome": "glo-o4", "src": "img/slides/f980ca0dc6f9.webp", "caption": "Layers of the atmosphere from the ground up: <b>troposphere</b> (about 0–12 km, where weather happens), <b>stratosphere</b> (to about 50 km, contains the ozone layer and gets warmer with height), <b>mesosphere</b> (meteors burn up, the coldest layer), <b>thermosphere</b> (auroras, very hot) and <b>exosphere</b> (satellites). Remember the order with temperature: it falls, rises, falls, then rises again.", "from": "Earth's Spheres · slide 4"},
  {"id": "3651d482ef45", "topic": "global", "outcome": "glo-o3c", "src": "img/slides/3651d482ef45.webp", "caption": "The phosphorus cycle. Weathering and erosion release phosphate from rock, plants absorb it from soil and water, animals get it by eating plants, and decomposers return it to the soil. Runoff and leaching carry phosphate into water, where it forms sediment and new rock that geological uplift slowly brings back to the surface. There is no gas phase: phosphorus moves through the lithosphere, hydrosphere and biosphere, but not the atmosphere.", "from": "Phosphorus Cycle · slide 7"},
  {"id": "d0f2749c83e4", "topic": "global", "outcome": "glo-o3c", "src": "img/slides/d0f2749c83e4.webp", "caption": "Eutrophication: fertiliser and animal waste wash nitrogen and phosphorus into water, causing an algal bloom. The algae block sunlight so water plants die, and bacteria decomposing the dead matter use up the dissolved oxygen, so fish suffocate and a \"dead zone\" forms.", "from": "Phosphorus Cycle · slide 15"},
  {"id": "fc57bcf9a67d", "topic": "global", "outcome": "glo-o3c", "src": "img/slides/fc57bcf9a67d.webp", "caption": "A real algal bloom: green scum of algae and cyanobacteria (blue-green algae) covering a lake shore after excess phosphate entered the water. Phosphorus is usually the limiting nutrient in fresh water, so a small amount of extra phosphate causes rapid growth, and some cyanobacteria release toxins.", "from": "Phosphorus Cycle · slide 14"},
  {"id": "b05e3f5d7d2b", "topic": "global", "outcome": "glo-o3c", "src": "img/slides/b05e3f5d7d2b.webp", "caption": "Cluster (proteoid) roots of Banksia at different stages of growth. Australian soils are old, weathered and very low in phosphorus, so these dense mats of rootlets release acids that dissolve phosphate locked in the soil. Because the plants cannot switch this off, extra fertiliser can cause phosphorus toxicity in many native plants.", "from": "Phosphorus Cycle · slide 12"},
  {"id": "b35abe1d01f6", "topic": "global", "outcome": "glo-o3c", "src": "img/slides/b35abe1d01f6.webp", "caption": "ATP (adenosine triphosphate) is made of adenine, the sugar ribose and three phosphate groups. It is the cell's energy carrier, which is one reason all living things need phosphorus (it is also in DNA, cell membranes, bones and teeth).", "from": "Phosphorus Cycle · slide 3"},
  {"id": "2eda4f70db52", "topic": "global", "outcome": "glo-o3c", "src": "img/slides/2eda4f70db52.webp", "caption": "A phospholipid has a phosphate head that is attracted to water (hydrophilic) and two fatty acid tails that repel water (hydrophobic). Phospholipids form every cell membrane, so phosphorus is essential to all life.", "from": "Phosphorus Cycle · slide 3"},
  {"id": "4685dbdccd56", "topic": "ws", "outcome": "ws-n4", "src": "img/slides/4685dbdccd56.webp", "caption": "An indicator chart used in a Working Scientifically question to predict and record results. Methyl orange is red below about pH 3 and yellow above about 4.4. Bromothymol blue is yellow below 6, blue above 7.6 and green in between. Phenolphthalein is colourless below about 8.3 and pink above 10. In the gaps the indicator shows an in-between colour, so a neutral salt such as NaCl (pH about 7) gives yellow, green and colourless.", "from": "Working Scientifically Questions and Solutions · page 2"},
  {"id": "f4e8024760f1", "topic": "motion", "outcome": "mot-n2", "src": "img/slides/f4e8024760f1.webp", "caption": "A car going around a roundabout at constant speed is still accelerating, because velocity is a vector and its direction keeps changing. The arrows pointing to the centre show the direction of the net force and acceleration.", "from": "Motion Textbook · page 27"},
  {"id": "98928a130369", "topic": "motion", "outcome": "mot-n3", "src": "img/slides/98928a130369.webp", "caption": "A ticker timer's vibrating arm strikes the tape through carbon paper 50 times a second (50 Hz), so dots are 0.02 s apart. Even spacing means constant speed, spacing that grows means the object is accelerating, and spacing that shrinks means it is decelerating.", "from": "6. Ticker Timers · slide 3"},
  {"id": "45d66248353d", "topic": "motion", "outcome": "mot-n3", "src": "img/slides/45d66248353d.webp", "caption": "Dot spacing on this tape grows from left to right (slow, getting faster, fast). A larger gap means more distance in the same 0.02 s, so the right side shows the greater velocity.", "from": "6. Ticker Timers · slide 4"},
  {"id": "fc34fd868f25", "topic": "motion", "outcome": "mot-n3", "src": "img/slides/fc34fd868f25.webp", "caption": "Each gap between neighbouring dots is 0.02 s on a 50 Hz timer, so 5 gaps (6 dots) take 0.10 s. Count the gaps, not the dots, when finding time.", "from": "6. Ticker Timers · slide 5"},
  {"id": "8fd27d416a2e", "topic": "motion", "outcome": "mot-n3", "src": "img/slides/8fd27d416a2e.webp", "caption": "The section of tape measured here is 0.081 m long and spans 5 intervals (0.10 s), so the average velocity = 0.081 ÷ 0.10 = 0.81 m/s. The gaps get wider along the tape, so the trolley was accelerating.", "from": "6. Ticker Timers · slide 5"},
  {"id": "f3f408894fc2", "topic": "motion", "outcome": "mot-n3", "src": "img/slides/f3f408894fc2.webp", "caption": "Shapes to recognise on a position–time graph: a steep straight line is fast and steady, a gentler line is slow and steady, a flat line is stationary, a curve getting steeper is accelerating, and a downward slope is returning to the start. The axis is labelled \"distance\", but distance can never decrease, so this is really a <b>displacement</b>–time graph.", "from": "7. Motion graphs · slide 4"},
  {"id": "11c1bd8fe48f", "topic": "motion", "outcome": "mot-n3", "src": "img/slides/11c1bd8fe48f.webp", "caption": "The gradient of a distance–time graph is the speed. Between 16 s (200 m) and 36 s (800 m) the speed is 600 m ÷ 20 s = 30 m/s. The curve steepens at the start (speeding up), flattens near the end (slowing down) and is flat after about 48 s (stopped).", "from": "7. Motion graphs · slide 3"},
  {"id": "cc535bae74a6", "topic": "motion", "outcome": "mot-n3", "src": "img/slides/cc535bae74a6.webp", "caption": "Displacement–time graph of a car at a traffic light. Stage 1 is constant velocity, in stage 2 the slope decreases (decelerating), stage 3 is flat (stopped), in stage 4 the slope increases (accelerating), and stage 5 is constant velocity again.", "from": "7. Motion graphs · slide 8"},
  {"id": "9acb82e843f6", "topic": "motion", "outcome": "mot-n3", "src": "img/slides/9acb82e843f6.webp", "caption": "On a speed–time graph the gradient is the acceleration. A line sloping up is uniform acceleration (steeper means larger acceleration), a horizontal line is steady speed, and a line sloping down is deceleration.", "from": "7. Motion graphs · slide 13"},
  {"id": "2b36bc82cbee", "topic": "motion", "outcome": "mot-n3", "src": "img/slides/2b36bc82cbee.webp", "caption": "The area under a speed–time graph is the distance travelled. Triangle (0–4 s): ½ × 4 × 8 = 16 m. Rectangle (4–10 s): 6 × 8 = 48 m. Total: 64 m.", "from": "7. Motion graphs · slide 17"},
  {"id": "de99e20f02d5", "topic": "motion", "outcome": "mot-n3", "src": "img/slides/de99e20f02d5.webp", "caption": "A velocity–time graph can go below the time axis, which means the object is moving in the negative direction. Here it speeds up to 8 m/s, holds that speed from 3 to 5 s, then slows down, stops and reverses at 7 s, reaching −8 m/s at 9 s.", "from": "7. Motion graphs · slide 22"},
  {"id": "62f3ab793e1f", "topic": "motion", "outcome": "mot-n4", "src": "img/slides/62f3ab793e1f.webp", "caption": "Free-body diagram of a box pulled along a table. The forces are the applied force F<sub>a</sub>, friction F<sub>f</sub> opposing the motion, weight mg acting down and the normal force F<sub>N</sub> acting up. If the box moves at constant speed, the forces are balanced (net force = 0).", "from": "8-11. Newton's Laws · slide 18"},
  {"id": "28f0709f2028", "topic": "motion", "outcome": "mot-n4", "src": "img/slides/28f0709f2028.webp", "caption": "Finding the net force: add forces that point the same way and subtract forces that point opposite ways. In part 1, 5 N + 20 N = 25 N to the right. In part 2, 7 N − 2 N = 5 N to the left.", "from": "8-11. Newton's Laws · slide 9"},
  {"id": "0b74ac5b01ad", "topic": "motion", "outcome": "mot-n4", "src": "img/slides/0b74ac5b01ad.webp", "caption": "Inertia (Newton's first law): when the card is flicked away quickly, the force acts only on the card. The coin's inertia keeps it at rest, so it drops straight into the glass.", "from": "8-11. Newton's Laws · slide 4"},
  {"id": "79805840ce22", "topic": "motion", "outcome": "mot-n4", "src": "img/slides/79805840ce22.webp", "caption": "Newton's third law: when your finger pushes on a wall, the wall pushes back on your finger with a force of equal size in the opposite direction. The two forces act on different objects.", "from": "8-11. Newton's Laws · slide 19"},
  {"id": "86874f8487bd", "topic": "motion", "outcome": "mot-n4", "src": "img/slides/86874f8487bd.webp", "caption": "A book resting on a table has its weight (Earth pulling it down) balanced by the normal force (the table pushing it up, at right angles to the surface). These are balanced forces on one object, <i>not</i> a third-law pair: the pair of the weight is the book pulling Earth up.", "from": "8-11. Newton's Laws · slide 16"},
  {"id": "92e2a06c55c2", "topic": "motion", "outcome": "mot-n4", "src": "img/slides/92e2a06c55c2.webp", "caption": "Mass and weight are different. This 60 kg person has a weight of W = mg = 60 × 9.8 = 588 N on Earth. Their mass is the same everywhere, but on the Moon their weight is only about one-sixth as much.", "from": "Motion Textbook · page 30"},
  {"id": "0a87d2b33842", "topic": "ws", "outcome": "ws-n2", "src": "img/slides/0a87d2b33842.webp", "caption": "A <b>scientific diagram</b> of equipment for heating water: retort stand, bosshead and clamp, thermometer, beaker of water, gauze mat, tripod and Bunsen burner. Draw these in pencil as 2D side-on outlines, with containers left open at the top and every part labelled by a straight, ruled line with no arrowhead and no crossing lines.", "from": "2026 SCIENCE STANDARD · page 9"},
  {"id": "973a0086b86a", "topic": "ws", "outcome": "ws-n2", "src": "img/slides/973a0086b86a.webp", "caption": "Standard 2D symbols for common lab equipment (beaker, conical flask, measuring cylinder, tripod, gauze, Bunsen burner shown as an arrow labelled HEAT, test tube, funnel, evaporating basin, crucible, thermometer and Petri dish). Use these simple outlines in a method diagram rather than drawing the equipment realistically.", "from": "Park's Party Official Science Notes (3) · page 3"},
  {"id": "77e1ac2b8d44", "topic": "ws", "outcome": "ws-n3", "src": "img/slides/77e1ac2b8d44.webp", "caption": "A <b>risk assessment matrix</b>: the <b>likelihood</b> of a hazard (frequent → improbable) is combined with its <b>severity</b> (catastrophic → negligible) to give an overall risk rating of high, serious, medium or low. In a risk assessment, list 2–3 hazards, rate each one, and give a specific control measure for it, not just 'wear PPE'.", "from": "Park's Party Official Science Notes (3) · page 4"},
  {"id": "31eff7068a8a", "topic": "ws", "outcome": "ws-n4", "src": "img/slides/31eff7068a8a.webp", "caption": "<b>Percentage uncertainty</b> = (absolute uncertainty ÷ measured value) × 100%. The <b>absolute uncertainty</b> is the ± margin (often half the smallest scale division for one reading), so a reading of 150.5 ± 0.1 cm has a percentage uncertainty of about 0.07%.", "from": "Park's Party Official Science Notes (3) · page 5"},
  {"id": "de915fd864a3", "topic": "ws", "outcome": "ws-n6", "src": "img/slides/de915fd864a3.webp", "caption": "Words for describing graph trends: a straight line increases or decreases at a <b>steady rate</b>; a curve that gets steeper changes at an <b>increasing rate</b>; a curve that flattens out changes at a <b>decreasing rate</b>. <b>Error in the notes picture:</b> the two bottom-left and bottom-right labels are swapped. The bottom-left curve (steep, then flattening) is decreasing at a <i>decreasing</i> rate, and the bottom-right curve (flat, then plunging) is decreasing at an <i>increasing</i> rate.", "from": "Park's Party Official Science Notes (3) · page 6"},
  {"id": "ce6f0fb8cdbc", "topic": "ws", "outcome": "ws-n6", "src": "img/slides/ce6f0fb8cdbc.webp", "caption": "A scatter graph with a straight <b>line of best fit</b>. The line does not join the dots: it follows the trend, with roughly equal numbers of points above and below it, and ignores obvious outliers. Plot points as small crosses, use a ruled line (or a smooth curve if the trend is curved), and label both axes with units.", "from": "2026 SCIENCE STANDARD · page 13"},
  {"id": "33eb7399536e", "topic": "ws", "outcome": "ws-n8", "src": "img/slides/33eb7399536e.webp", "caption": "Dartboard picture of errors. <b>Random error</b> scatters results unpredictably around the true value (low precision), and you reduce it by repeating and averaging. <b>Systematic error</b> shifts every result the same way, for example a scale that is not zeroed, so results can be precise but not accurate. Repeating does not fix a systematic error; you have to fix or recalibrate the equipment.", "from": "2026 SCIENCE STANDARD · page 16"},
  {"id": "ba5ffd9b045f", "topic": "motion", "outcome": "mot-n1", "src": "img/slides/ba5ffd9b045f.webp", "caption": "The seven <b>SI base units</b>: second (s) for time, metre (m) for length, kilogram (kg) for mass, ampere (A) for current, kelvin (K) for temperature, mole (mol) for amount of substance and candela (cd) for luminous intensity. Convert to SI units (for example km/h → m/s by dividing by 3.6) before using any equation.", "from": "Park's Party Official Science Notes (3) · page 9"},
  {"id": "78dbb8330776", "topic": "motion", "outcome": "mot-n2", "src": "img/slides/78dbb8330776.webp", "caption": "<b>Distance</b> (dashed path) is the total length travelled and is a <b>scalar</b>. <b>Displacement</b> (straight arrow from A to B) is the change in position, with a direction, so it is a <b>vector</b>. Displacement can be much smaller than distance, and it is zero for a round trip.", "from": "Park's Party Official Science Notes (3) · page 12"},
  {"id": "ded32bc842cf", "topic": "motion", "outcome": "mot-n2", "src": "img/slides/ded32bc842cf.webp", "caption": "A table of the equations of motion rearranged for each variable (each column leaves one variable out). <b>Errors in the notes table:</b> five entries are wrong. Correct versions: s = (v<sup>2</sup> − u<sup>2</sup>)/2a; u = (2s − at<sup>2</sup>)/2t; u = 2s/t − v; u = √(v<sup>2</sup> − 2as); v = (2s + at<sup>2</sup>)/2t. It is safer to learn the four core equations (v = u + at, s = ut + ½at<sup>2</sup>, v<sup>2</sup> = u<sup>2</sup> + 2as, s = ½(u + v)t) and rearrange them yourself.", "from": "2026 SCIENCE STANDARD · page 24"},
  {"id": "acccc5f5d87c", "topic": "motion", "outcome": "mot-n3", "src": "img/slides/acccc5f5d87c.webp", "caption": "Ticker timer set-up: tape attached to a trolley runs through the ticker timer as the trolley rolls down a ramp. The timer prints dots at a fixed rate (usually 50 per second), so dots that get further apart show the trolley is speeding up.", "from": "Park's Party Official Science Notes (3) · page 15"},
  {"id": "819ca691063c", "topic": "motion", "outcome": "mot-n3", "src": "img/slides/819ca691063c.webp", "caption": "Graph shapes for three kinds of motion (columns: constant acceleration, constant velocity, at rest). Position–time: parabola, diagonal line, flat line. Velocity–time: diagonal, flat, zero. Acceleration–time: flat (constant non-zero value), zero, zero.", "from": "Park's Party Official Science Notes (3) · page 15"},
  {"id": "d04330d59d05", "topic": "motion", "outcome": "mot-n3", "src": "img/slides/d04330d59d05.webp", "caption": "The gradient (slope) of a velocity–time graph is the acceleration: a = rise/run = (v<sub>2</sub> − v<sub>1</sub>)/(t<sub>2</sub> − t<sub>1</sub>) = Δv/Δt. A steeper line means a larger acceleration, and a horizontal line means zero acceleration.", "from": "2026 SCIENCE STANDARD · page 29"},
  {"id": "78b1f87319a5", "topic": "motion", "outcome": "mot-n3", "src": "img/slides/78b1f87319a5.webp", "caption": "On a velocity–time graph, lines above the time axis mean motion in the positive direction and lines below mean motion in the negative direction. An object is <b>speeding up</b> when its line moves away from the axis and <b>slowing down</b> when it moves towards the axis, whichever side it is on.", "from": "2026 SCIENCE STANDARD · page 30"},
  {"id": "f12ffbb0110e", "topic": "motion", "outcome": "mot-n4", "src": "img/slides/f12ffbb0110e.webp", "caption": "Practical for Newton's second law (F = ma): a hanging mass pulls a cart along a track over a smart pulley that records its motion. Change the hanging mass (force) while keeping the total mass constant, or change the cart's mass while keeping the force constant, then measure the acceleration.", "from": "2026 SCIENCE STANDARD · page 1"},
  {"id": "8a12fe15b78f", "topic": "motion", "outcome": "mot-n4", "src": "img/slides/8a12fe15b78f.webp", "caption": "Finding the <b>net force</b>: add forces acting in the same direction and subtract opposing ones. Here 100 N + 100 N forwards − 50 N drag = 150 N forwards, so the car accelerates forwards (a = F<sub>net</sub>/m).", "from": "Park's Party Official Science Notes (3) · page 17"},
  {"id": "d2114d679d30", "topic": "motion", "outcome": "mot-n4", "src": "img/slides/d2114d679d30.webp", "caption": "Common forces to include on free-body diagrams: gravity/weight F<sub>grav</sub> (always present), tension F<sub>tens</sub> (strings, ropes), spring force, friction F<sub>frict</sub> (surfaces sliding), air resistance F<sub>air</sub>, normal force F<sub>norm</sub> (surfaces pressing together) and applied force F<sub>app</sub> (a push or pull).", "from": "Park's Party Official Science Notes (3) · page 18"},
  {"id": "6e7b62d2f651", "topic": "motion", "outcome": "mot-n6", "src": "img/slides/6e7b62d2f651.webp", "caption": "A mass hung on a spring stretches it by an extension x until the upward spring force F balances the weight mg. <b>Hooke's law</b>: F = kx, where k is the spring constant (N/m). The stretched spring stores elastic potential energy (½kx<sup>2</sup>).", "from": "Park's Party Official Science Notes (3) · page 21"},
  {"id": "7c114c9a6653", "topic": "motion", "outcome": "mot-n6", "src": "img/slides/7c114c9a6653.webp", "caption": "<b>Conservation of momentum</b> in a collision where two objects stick together: m<sub>1</sub>v<sub>1i</sub> + m<sub>2</sub>v<sub>2i</sub> = (m<sub>1</sub> + m<sub>2</sub>)v<sub>f</sub>. Choose one direction as positive and give velocities in the opposite direction a negative sign. Total momentum is conserved, but some kinetic energy is lost as heat and sound.", "from": "Park's Party Official Science Notes (3) · page 22"},
  {"id": "7efeecad2e70", "topic": "chemistry", "outcome": "chem-pre", "src": "img/slides/7efeecad2e70.webp", "caption": "The four acid reaction patterns to learn: acid + metal → salt + hydrogen; acid + base (metal oxide) → salt + water; acid + hydroxide → salt + water; acid + carbonate → salt + carbon dioxide + water. The salt is named from the metal plus the acid (hydrochloric → chloride, sulfuric → sulfate, nitric → nitrate).", "from": "2026 SCIENCE STANDARD · page 39"},
  {"id": "3e348de20633", "topic": "chemistry", "outcome": "chem-pre", "src": "img/slides/3e348de20633.webp", "caption": "Heating a metal carbonate in a test tube and bubbling the gas through <b>limewater</b> via a delivery tube. The carbonate thermally decomposes (e.g. CuCO<sub>3</sub> → CuO + CO<sub>2</sub>), and the limewater turning milky/cloudy confirms <b>carbon dioxide</b>. Remove the delivery tube from the limewater before you stop heating, or suck-back can occur.", "from": "2026 SCIENCE STANDARD · page 3"},
  {"id": "b238b10f2713", "topic": "chemistry", "outcome": "chem-pre", "src": "img/slides/b238b10f2713.webp", "caption": "<b>Test for hydrogen</b>: collect the gas (here from a reactive metal reacting with water) and hold a <b>lighted</b> splint at the mouth of the test tube. A squeaky <b>'pop'</b> means hydrogen, which burns rapidly with oxygen in the air: 2H<sub>2</sub> + O<sub>2</sub> → 2H<sub>2</sub>O.", "from": "2026 SCIENCE STANDARD · page 4"},
  {"id": "548e3363c933", "topic": "chemistry", "outcome": "chem-pre", "src": "img/slides/548e3363c933.webp", "caption": "<b>Test for oxygen</b>: put a <b>glowing</b> (not burning) splint into the test tube; if it relights, the gas is oxygen. Don't mix it up with the hydrogen test, which uses a <i>lighted</i> splint and gives a pop.", "from": "2026 SCIENCE STANDARD · page 5"},
  {"id": "6d45c812e941", "topic": "chemistry", "outcome": "chem-o2", "src": "img/slides/6d45c812e941.webp", "caption": "The <b>system</b> is the reacting chemicals and the <b>surroundings</b> are everything else. <b>Endothermic</b> (ΔH &gt; 0): heat flows into the system, so the surroundings get cooler. <b>Exothermic</b> (ΔH &lt; 0): heat flows out, so the surroundings get warmer, because more energy is released when the new bonds form than was absorbed breaking the old ones.", "from": "2026 SCIENCE STANDARD · page 85"},
  {"id": "9468a39889f3", "topic": "chemistry", "outcome": "chem-o7", "src": "img/slides/9468a39889f3.webp", "caption": "A catalyst provides an <b>alternative pathway</b> (pink, here with several small steps) whose highest barrier, the activation energy, is lower than without a catalyst (black). The reactants (X, Y) and product (Z) energies are the same on both paths, so the overall energy change is unchanged. Note: the picture labels this ΔG; in this course call it <b>ΔH</b>, the enthalpy change.", "from": "Park's Party Official Science Notes (3) · page 43"},
  {"id": "4d3483e30730", "topic": "chemistry", "outcome": "chem-o8", "src": "img/slides/4d3483e30730.webp", "caption": "The <b>induced fit model</b> of enzyme action: the active site is not a perfect match at first, but changes shape slightly as the substrate binds, gripping it to form the enzyme–substrate complex. The products are released and the enzyme returns to its original shape, ready to be reused. Compare the lock-and-key model, where the active site is a rigid, exact fit.", "from": "2026 SCIENCE STANDARD · page 95"},
  {"id": "9929807f9d4c", "topic": "chemistry", "outcome": "chem-o9", "src": "img/slides/9929807f9d4c.webp", "caption": "The <b>reactivity (activity) series</b> from most reactive (K, Na, Ca, Mg, Al) to least reactive (Cu, Hg, Ag, Au, Pt). A metal displaces any metal <i>below</i> it from a solution of its ions, and metals above hydrogen react with dilute acids to release H<sub>2</sub>.", "from": "Park's Party Official Science Notes (3) · page 44"},
  {"id": "ba2a36bc5bab", "topic": "chemistry", "outcome": "chem-o9", "src": "img/slides/ba2a36bc5bab.webp", "caption": "Halogens also take part in displacement reactions: a more reactive halogen displaces a less reactive one from its salt (reactivity: Cl<sub>2</sub> &gt; Br<sub>2</sub> &gt; I<sub>2</sub>). <b>Error in the notes picture:</b> bromine and iodine cannot displace chloride, so 'bromine/iodine + potassium chloride' would give no reaction. The colours shown match <b>chlorine + potassium bromide</b> (yellow-orange bromine forms) and <b>chlorine + potassium iodide</b> (brown iodine forms), e.g. Cl<sub>2</sub> + 2KBr → 2KCl + Br<sub>2</sub>.", "from": "2026 SCIENCE STANDARD · page 96"},
  {"id": "b97e49c278e7", "topic": "chemistry", "outcome": "chem-o10b", "src": "img/slides/b97e49c278e7.webp", "caption": "Galvanic cell schematic. At the <b>anode</b> the more reactive metal is oxidised and releases electrons, which flow through the external wire to the <b>cathode</b>, where reduction absorbs them. In the salt bridge, positive ions migrate towards the cathode and negative ions towards the anode to keep both solutions electrically neutral.", "from": "2026 SCIENCE STANDARD · page 99"},
  {"id": "8d2927f178b5", "topic": "universe", "outcome": "uni-o9", "src": "img/slides/8d2927f178b5.webp", "caption": "A star's <b>colour shows its surface temperature</b>: red about 3 500 K (coolest), yellow about 5 000 K, white about 7 000 K and blue about 25 000 K (hottest). The headlight comparison is about brightness: like headlights, stars look dimmer the further away they are, which is why apparent brightness depends on distance as well as luminosity.", "from": "2026 SCIENCE STANDARD · page 105"},
  {"id": "74f8182abea0", "topic": "universe", "outcome": "uni-o9", "src": "img/slides/74f8182abea0.webp", "caption": "Spectral classes <b>O B A F G K M</b> run from hottest (O, blue, above 28 000 K) to coolest (M, red, about 2 500–3 500 K), and each class shows different absorption lines in its spectrum. The Sun is a G-type star at about 5 800 K. Memory aid: 'Oh Be A Fine Girl/Guy, Kiss Me'.", "from": "2026 SCIENCE STANDARD · page 112"},
  {"id": "37cfbb2e43c9", "topic": "universe", "outcome": "uni-o9", "src": "img/slides/37cfbb2e43c9.webp", "caption": "<b>Black-body curves</b> for stars at different temperatures. Hotter stars give out more energy at every wavelength, and their peak moves to shorter wavelengths (<b>Wien's law</b>: λ<sub>peak</sub> × T = 2.898 × 10<sup>−3</sup> m K). A 6 000 K star peaks at 483 nm (visible), while a 3 000 K star peaks at 966 nm (infrared) and looks red.", "from": "Park's Party Official Science Notes (3) · page 51"},
  {"id": "fa616af90ddf", "topic": "universe", "outcome": "uni-o4", "src": "img/slides/fa616af90ddf.webp", "caption": "Star life cycles: all stars form in a <b>stellar nebula</b>. An average (Sun-like) star becomes a <b>red giant</b>, then a <b>planetary nebula</b> and a <b>white dwarf</b>. A massive star becomes a <b>red supergiant</b>, explodes as a <b>supernova</b> and leaves a <b>neutron star</b> or, for the most massive stars, a <b>black hole</b>.", "from": "2026 SCIENCE STANDARD · page 108"},
  {"id": "2fe5f0720155", "topic": "universe", "outcome": "uni-o5", "src": "img/slides/2fe5f0720155.webp", "caption": "How a black hole forms: (1) a very massive star (very roughly 20+ times the Sun's mass) (2) runs out of fuel, so fusion can no longer push outwards and gravity makes the core collapse in under a second; (3) the outer layers are blasted away in a <b>supernova</b>; (4) the core is crushed into a <b>black hole</b>, whose gravity is so strong that not even light can escape.", "from": "2026 SCIENCE STANDARD · page 110"},
  {"id": "e309afaf6099", "topic": "universe", "outcome": "uni-o5", "src": "img/slides/e309afaf6099.webp", "caption": "A giant star in a binary system losing gas to a compact companion (such as a white dwarf) through an <b>accretion disc</b>. If a white dwarf gains enough mass to pass about 1.4 solar masses, it can explode as a <b>Type Ia supernova</b>. This is the second type of supernova, different from the core-collapse supernova of a single massive star.", "from": "Park's Party Official Science Notes (3) · page 48"},
  {"id": "3889800e3935", "topic": "universe", "outcome": "uni-o11", "src": "img/slides/3889800e3935.webp", "caption": "H–R diagram labelled with star masses and main-sequence lifetimes. Going up the main sequence, stars are <b>more massive, hotter, bluer, bigger and more luminous</b>, but they use up their fuel much faster: about 10<sup>7</sup> years for a 10-solar-mass star, 10<sup>10</sup> years for the Sun and 10<sup>11</sup> years for small red dwarfs.", "from": "2026 SCIENCE STANDARD · page 113"},
  {"id": "1b3e840febdd", "topic": "universe", "outcome": "uni-o11", "src": "img/slides/1b3e840febdd.webp", "caption": "H–R diagram with named stars: luminosity (Sun = 1) and absolute magnitude on the vertical axes, and spectral type/temperature on the horizontal axis (hot on the left). The main sequence runs from Rigel-like hot stars down past the Sun to red dwarfs such as Proxima. Giants such as Betelgeuse and Aldebaran are top right (cool but very bright because they are huge), and white dwarfs such as Sirius B are bottom left (hot but dim because they are tiny).", "from": "2026 SCIENCE STANDARD · page 114"},
  {"id": "61b35f027eaa", "topic": "universe", "outcome": "uni-o13", "src": "img/slides/61b35f027eaa.webp", "caption": "Features of a <b>spiral galaxy</b>: a central <b>bulge</b> of older stars, a flat rotating <b>disc</b> with <b>spiral arms</b> of gas, dust and young blue stars, and a faint spherical <b>halo</b> of old stars and globular clusters. Note: the photos show other spiral galaxies, not the Milky Way. We are inside the Milky Way and cannot photograph it from outside, but it has the same features (and is actually a barred spiral).", "from": "2026 SCIENCE STANDARD · page 116"},
  {"id": "102b15e5ac20", "topic": "universe", "outcome": "uni-o13", "src": "img/slides/102b15e5ac20.webp", "caption": "A <b>barred spiral galaxy</b> (NGC 1300): a straight bar of stars runs through the centre, and the spiral arms start from the ends of the bar rather than from the bulge. The Milky Way is a barred spiral.", "from": "2026 SCIENCE STANDARD · page 117"},
  {"id": "2e38f67417c8", "topic": "universe", "outcome": "uni-o13", "src": "img/slides/2e38f67417c8.webp", "caption": "<b>Hubble's 'tuning fork' classification</b>. Ellipticals go from E0 (round) to E7 (very elongated); lenticulars (S0) sit at the fork; normal spirals (Sa, Sb, Sc) and barred spirals (SBa, SBb, SBc) form the two prongs, with arms getting looser and bulges smaller from a to c; and irregulars (Irr) have no regular shape.", "from": "2026 SCIENCE STANDARD · page 115"},
  {"id": "dbad0868581f", "topic": "universe", "outcome": "uni-o16", "src": "img/slides/dbad0868581f.webp", "caption": "<b>Stellar parallax</b>: a nearby star is observed from opposite sides of Earth's orbit (January and July, a baseline of 2 AU), so it appears to shift against the distant background stars. The parallax angle p is half the total shift, and the distance in parsecs is <b>d = 1/p</b> (p in arcseconds). Closer stars show a bigger shift.", "from": "2026 SCIENCE STANDARD · page 122"},
  {"id": "14e9704c4564", "topic": "universe", "outcome": "uni-o17", "src": "img/slides/14e9704c4564.webp", "caption": "Galileo's drawing of <b>sunspots</b> from his 1613 book on sunspots. With his telescope (from 1609) he also saw mountains and craters on the Moon, the four large moons of Jupiter and the phases of Venus. This evidence showed the heavens were not perfect and unchanging, and it supported Copernicus' Sun-centred model.", "from": "Park's Party Official Science Notes (3) · page 55"},
  {"id": "14f50949188f", "topic": "universe", "outcome": "uni-o17", "src": "img/slides/14f50949188f.webp", "caption": "One of the twin Keck telescopes in Hawaii. Its 10 m primary mirror is made of 36 hexagonal segments that computers keep aligned. A bigger mirror collects more light, so it can see fainter objects in more detail, and the two telescopes can be used together.", "from": "2026 SCIENCE STANDARD · page 123"},
  {"id": "00bd8f50da07", "topic": "universe", "outcome": "uni-o17", "src": "img/slides/00bd8f50da07.webp", "caption": "A reflecting telescope with a <b>charge-coupled device (CCD)</b>: the primary mirror collects light and the secondary mirror sends it to the CCD, a light-sensitive silicon chip made of pixels. CCDs record far more of the incoming light than photographic film and produce digital images, so they are a good choice for the 'one other technology' part of the outcome.", "from": "2026 SCIENCE STANDARD · page 125"},
  {"id": "b7214af81c86", "topic": "universe", "outcome": "uni-o19", "src": "img/slides/b7214af81c86.webp", "caption": "Visible light from violet (shortest wavelength, about 400 nm) to red (longest, about 700 nm). '<b>Red shift</b>' means spectral lines move towards the longer-wavelength red end, and '<b>blue shift</b>' means they move towards the shorter-wavelength blue end.", "from": "2026 SCIENCE STANDARD · page 128"},
  {"id": "8d23c66db3f9", "topic": "universe", "outcome": "uni-o19", "src": "img/slides/8d23c66db3f9.webp", "caption": "The same absorption lines (Hγ, Hβ, Hα) for a source at rest (top), a source moving away (middle: <b>red-shifted</b> to longer wavelengths) and a source moving towards us (bottom: <b>blue-shifted</b>). Hubble found that almost all galaxies are red-shifted, which is key evidence that the universe is expanding.", "from": "2026 SCIENCE STANDARD · page 128"},
  {"id": "5e4751371a8a", "topic": "universe", "outcome": "uni-o19", "src": "img/slides/5e4751371a8a.webp", "caption": "An <b>emission spectrum</b> is bright coloured lines on a dark background, given out by a hot, low-density gas. An <b>absorption spectrum</b> is a continuous rainbow with dark lines, produced when light from a hot, dense source passes through a cooler gas. Each element absorbs and emits at the same wavelengths, so the lines act as a 'fingerprint' for elements in stars.", "from": "Park's Party Official Science Notes (3) · page 58"},
  {"id": "85889e6600a5", "topic": "universe", "outcome": "uni-o20", "src": "img/slides/85889e6600a5.webp", "caption": "Recession velocity against distance for galaxies: the straight-line trend is <b>Hubble's law</b>, v = H<sub>0</sub>d, so more distant galaxies are moving away faster. Hubble's original 1929 data cover only the small red corner near the origin, and modern data extend the same straight line much further, supporting an <b>expanding universe</b>.", "from": "2026 SCIENCE STANDARD · page 130"},
  {"id": "db458eb56684", "topic": "evolution", "outcome": "evo-o3", "src": "img/slides/db458eb56684.webp", "caption": "Natural vs artificial selection. In <b>natural selection</b> the environment 'chooses': individuals with favourable variations survive and reproduce more (e.g. finch beaks suited to the available food). In <b>artificial selection</b> humans choose which individuals breed for desired traits (e.g. Chihuahuas, high-milk cattle), which is usually much faster. <b>Correction:</b> the chart says artificial selection 'does not facilitate evolution', but it does change allele frequencies over generations, which is evolution (all dog breeds evolved from wolves). It just reduces variety rather than increasing biodiversity.", "from": "Park's Party Official Science Notes (3) · page 61"},
  {"id": "0f52519f2274", "topic": "evolution", "outcome": "evo-o3b", "src": "img/slides/0f52519f2274.webp", "caption": "<b>Adaptive radiation</b> of the Galápagos finches: one ancestral seed-eating ground finch gave rise to many species. Their beaks are adapted to different foods: large crushing beaks for seeds, thin pointed beaks for insects, and others for buds, fruits and cactus. This is <b>divergent evolution</b> driven by natural selection on different islands and food sources.", "from": "2026 SCIENCE STANDARD · page 137"},
  {"id": "53fd097814cb", "topic": "evolution", "outcome": "evo-o3b", "src": "img/slides/53fd097814cb.webp", "caption": "A <b>phylogenetic tree</b> for three <i>Drosophila</i> (fruit fly) species. Each yellow node is a <b>speciation event</b>, where one ancestral population split into two separately evolving species. Species joined by a more recent node share a more recent common ancestor and are more closely related.", "from": "2026 SCIENCE STANDARD · page 141"},
  {"id": "8cd8c9c05e1c", "topic": "evolution", "outcome": "evo-o4a", "src": "img/slides/8cd8c9c05e1c.webp", "caption": "<b>Homologous structures</b> (rat leg and bat wing) have the same basic bone structure but different functions because they come from a common ancestor (divergent evolution). <b>Analogous structures</b> (bat wing and bee wing) do the same job but have completely different structures because they evolved separately (convergent evolution).", "from": "2026 SCIENCE STANDARD · page 146"},
  {"id": "426c4fa584a7", "topic": "evolution", "outcome": "evo-o4a", "src": "img/slides/426c4fa584a7.webp", "caption": "<b>Comparative embryology</b>: early embryos of a fish, turtle, chicken, pig and human look very similar (all have a tail and pharyngeal pouches) and only become different later in development. This suggests the vertebrates share a common ancestor. Simplified drawings like these exaggerate the similarity a little, but the general pattern is real.", "from": "2026 SCIENCE STANDARD · page 148"},
  {"id": "fe51a63fb5da", "topic": "evolution", "outcome": "evo-o4b", "src": "img/slides/fe51a63fb5da.webp", "caption": "<b>Biochemical evidence</b>: the same 22 amino acids of the haemoglobin beta chain compared across species. The number of differences from humans increases from monkey (1) to dog (3), bird (5) and fish (12). Fewer differences means a more recent common ancestor and a closer evolutionary relationship.", "from": "2026 SCIENCE STANDARD · page 151"},
  {"id": "c7d9d2ee9eea", "topic": "evolution", "outcome": "evo-o4b", "src": "img/slides/c7d9d2ee9eea.webp", "caption": "<b>Nuclear DNA</b> is inherited from both parents and gets reshuffled every generation, so it comes from all your ancestors. <b>Mitochondrial DNA (mtDNA)</b> is passed down only from the mother, almost unchanged except for occasional mutations, so it can trace maternal lineages far back in time (e.g. to 'Mitochondrial Eve') and compare how closely related populations or species are.", "from": "2026 SCIENCE STANDARD · page 152"},
  {"id": "4fdee85a74a3", "topic": "evolution", "outcome": "evo-o5", "src": "img/slides/4fdee85a74a3.webp", "caption": "A spider <b>preserved in amber</b> (fossilised tree resin). The sticky resin trapped the whole organism and sealed it from oxygen and decomposers, so even fine soft details survive. This is one of the rare ways whole organisms are preserved, along with freezing in ice and tar pits.", "from": "2026 SCIENCE STANDARD · page 153"},
  {"id": "d126809623d4", "topic": "evolution", "outcome": "evo-o5", "src": "img/slides/d126809623d4.webp", "caption": "<b>Petrified wood</b>, formed by <b>permineralisation</b>, the most common fossilisation process. Wood buried in sediment is soaked by mineral-rich groundwater, and minerals such as silica fill the spaces in the cells and harden, so the original structure is kept in stone.", "from": "2026 SCIENCE STANDARD · page 153"},
  {"id": "5abc7fdd9c43", "topic": "evolution", "outcome": "evo-o5", "src": "img/slides/5abc7fdd9c43.webp", "caption": "A <b>mould</b> fossil of a shell (scale bar 3 mm): the shell was buried, the sediment hardened, and the shell later dissolved away, leaving its impression in the rock. If minerals later fill the hollow, they form a <b>cast</b>, a 3D copy of the shell's shape.", "from": "2026 SCIENCE STANDARD · page 154"},
  {"id": "f0c6b90e4a0b", "topic": "evolution", "outcome": "evo-o5", "src": "img/slides/f0c6b90e4a0b.webp", "caption": "Fossil fern-like leaves preserved as a dark compression/impression in rock (scale bar 20 mm). Plant leaves are often preserved by <b>carbonisation</b>, where heat and pressure leave a thin film of carbon showing the outline. Note: in the notes this picture sits next to the trace-fossil text, but it is a <b>body fossil</b> (part of the organism), not a trace fossil like a footprint or burrow.", "from": "2026 SCIENCE STANDARD · page 155"},
  {"id": "368327508740", "topic": "evolution", "outcome": "evo-o6", "src": "img/slides/368327508740.webp", "caption": "Using <b>index fossils</b> to date unknown fossils. A, C and E are index fossils with known age ranges. Fossil B is found in the same layer as A and C, so it must date from when the age ranges of A and C overlap; fossil D is dated the same way using C and E.", "from": "2026 SCIENCE STANDARD · page 156"},
  {"id": "2ab2f3e69ecf", "topic": "evolution", "outcome": "evo-o6", "src": "img/slides/2ab2f3e69ecf.webp", "caption": "<b>Transitional fossils</b> show features of both an ancestral group and its descendants. <i>Archaeopteryx</i> has reptile features (teeth, clawed fingers, a long bony tail) and bird features (feathers, wings). <i>Darwinius masillae</i> ('Ida') was announced in 2009 as a link between early primates and monkeys/apes, but most scientists now think it is related to lemurs, so its 'missing link' status is disputed. This shows how identifying transitional fossils can be controversial.", "from": "2026 SCIENCE STANDARD · page 157"},
  {"id": "7a79fb0b9bca", "topic": "global", "outcome": "glo-o3a", "src": "img/slides/7a79fb0b9bca.webp", "caption": "The <b>carbon cycle</b>. <b>Photosynthesis</b> and <b>ocean uptake</b> remove CO<sub>2</sub> from the atmosphere, while plant, animal and root respiration, decay by decomposers, and the burning of fossil fuels (factory and car emissions) return it. Carbon stored in dead organisms can become fossil fuels over millions of years, and burning them quickly releases this long-stored carbon, which is the main cause of the enhanced greenhouse effect.", "from": "Park's Party Official Science Notes (3) · page 68"},
  {"id": "9d702a334df9", "topic": "global", "outcome": "glo-o4", "src": "img/slides/9d702a334df9.webp", "caption": "Temperature profile of the atmosphere. Temperature falls through the <b>troposphere</b> (to about −55 °C at the tropopause, about 12 km), rises through the <b>stratosphere</b> (ozone absorbs UV, about 0 °C at about 50 km), falls again through the <b>mesosphere</b> (about −90 °C at about 85 km, the coldest part), then rises sharply in the <b>thermosphere</b>. The boundaries ('-pauses') are where the trend changes direction.", "from": "2026 SCIENCE STANDARD · page 167"},
  {"id": "652567ba9606", "topic": "global", "outcome": "glo-o5", "src": "img/slides/652567ba9606.webp", "caption": "Ozone concentration against altitude. Most ozone forms the <b>ozone layer</b> in the stratosphere (peaking around 20–30 km), where it absorbs harmful UV-B radiation ('good ozone'). Ozone near the ground in the troposphere is an air pollutant that harms breathing ('bad ozone').", "from": "2026 SCIENCE STANDARD · page 168"},
  {"id": "8999ce9c4d68", "topic": "global", "outcome": "glo-o5", "src": "img/slides/8999ce9c4d68.webp", "caption": "How <b>CFCs</b> destroy ozone: (1) CFCs are released, (2) they rise into the stratosphere, (3) UV light breaks them apart and releases chlorine atoms, (4) each chlorine atom destroys many ozone molecules in a chain reaction, (5) the thinner ozone layer lets more UV reach the ground, and (6) this causes more skin cancer and eye damage. The <b>Montreal Protocol</b> (1987) phased out CFCs, and the ozone layer is slowly recovering.", "from": "2026 SCIENCE STANDARD · page 184"},
  {"id": "72d37a92918f", "topic": "global", "outcome": "glo-x2", "src": "img/slides/72d37a92918f.webp", "caption": "The Ocean Cleanup system: two vessels tow a long U-shaped barrier that funnels floating plastic into a collection zone at the back. Projects like this target ocean garbage patches, where plastic breaks into microplastics that enter food chains, but reducing plastic waste at its source is still essential.", "from": "2026 SCIENCE STANDARD · page 182"},
  {"id": "8224c8e42052", "topic": "genetics", "outcome": "gen-bg", "src": "img/slides/8224c8e42052.webp", "caption": "Structure of DNA: a <b>double helix</b> of two antiparallel strands with a sugar–phosphate backbone on the outside. Each <b>nucleotide</b> has a deoxyribose sugar, a phosphate and a base, and the bases pair in the middle by hydrogen bonds: <b>A with T</b> and <b>C with G</b>.", "from": "2026 SCIENCE STANDARD · page 64"},
  {"id": "f86ca64f82e8", "topic": "genetics", "outcome": "gen-bg", "src": "img/slides/f86ca64f82e8.webp", "caption": "<b>Semi-conservative replication</b>: the double helix unzips and each old strand acts as a template for a new complementary strand (A–T, C–G). Each new DNA molecule has one old and one new strand, and the two molecules are identical to each other and to the original.", "from": "2026 SCIENCE STANDARD · page 66"},
  {"id": "84e57b76da5a", "topic": "genetics", "outcome": "gen-bg", "src": "img/slides/84e57b76da5a.webp", "caption": "<b>Photo 51</b>, taken in 1952 in Rosalind Franklin's lab using <b>X-ray crystallography</b> (X-ray diffraction). The X-shaped pattern showed that DNA is a helix, and the spacing gave its dimensions. Watson and Crick used this data to build their double-helix model in 1953.", "from": "Park's Party Official Science Notes (3) · page 33"},
  {"id": "03cfae8d8202", "topic": "genetics", "outcome": "gen-bg", "src": "img/slides/03cfae8d8202.webp", "caption": "How DNA is packaged: the DNA double helix wraps around <b>histone</b> proteins (like beads on a string) to form <b>chromatin</b>, which coils further and condenses into a visible <b>chromosome</b> before cell division. Most of the time DNA is loosely packed chromatin so that genes can be read.", "from": "2026 SCIENCE STANDARD · page 46"},
  {"id": "e925604c04de", "topic": "genetics", "outcome": "gen-bg", "src": "img/slides/e925604c04de.webp", "caption": "The <b>cell cycle</b>: interphase (G<sub>1</sub> growth, <b>S phase</b> when DNA is copied, G<sub>2</sub> preparation) takes up most of the cycle, followed by mitosis (prophase, metaphase, anaphase, telophase) and cytokinesis, which splits the cell in two. Cells that stop dividing enter a resting phase, G<sub>0</sub>.", "from": "2026 SCIENCE STANDARD · page 49"},
  {"id": "6c389b248f73", "topic": "genetics", "outcome": "gen-bg", "src": "img/slides/6c389b248f73.webp", "caption": "Mitosis vs meiosis. <b>Mitosis</b> is one division that gives 2 genetically identical diploid (2n) cells, used for growth and repair. <b>Meiosis</b> is two divisions that give 4 genetically different haploid (n) gametes: homologous chromosomes pair up and cross over in prophase I, separate in meiosis I, and then the sister chromatids separate in meiosis II.", "from": "2026 SCIENCE STANDARD · page 63"},
  {"id": "ca3c143f447b", "topic": "genetics", "outcome": "gen-bg", "src": "img/slides/ca3c143f447b.webp", "caption": "<b>Crossing over</b> in prophase I of meiosis: homologous chromosomes pair up (synapsis, forming a bivalent), non-sister chromatids swap sections at a chiasma, and the result is <b>recombinant chromosomes</b> with new combinations of alleles. Together with independent assortment, this makes every gamete genetically unique.", "from": "2026 SCIENCE STANDARD · page 57"},
  {"id": "c01ecd515369", "topic": "genetics", "outcome": "gen-bg", "src": "img/slides/c01ecd515369.webp", "caption": "Meiosis in summary: after the DNA is copied in interphase, meiosis I separates the homologous chromosomes (giving 2 cells), and meiosis II separates the sister chromatids (giving 4 haploid daughter cells, each with one chromosome from every pair).", "from": "2026 SCIENCE STANDARD · page 55"},
  {"id": "ac76f059979d", "topic": "genetics", "outcome": "gen-o6", "src": "img/slides/ac76f059979d.webp", "caption": "Mendel's cross of true-breeding violet- and white-flowered pea plants. All F<sub>1</sub> plants were violet, so violet is <b>dominant</b>. When the F<sub>1</sub> plants self-fertilised, the white trait reappeared in the F<sub>2</sub>: 705 violet to 224 white, about <b>3 : 1</b>. This showed that the white 'factor' (allele) was hidden in the F<sub>1</sub>, not lost.", "from": "2026 SCIENCE STANDARD · page 67"},
  {"id": "a2b2502794c4", "topic": "genetics", "outcome": "gen-o7", "src": "img/slides/a2b2502794c4.webp", "caption": "<b>Genotype</b> is the alleles an organism has (BB = homozygous dominant, Bb = heterozygous, bb = homozygous recessive). <b>Phenotype</b> is the observable trait. BB and Bb both give purple flowers because purple (B) is dominant, and only bb gives white. Phenotype = genotype + environment.", "from": "2026 SCIENCE STANDARD · page 69"},
  {"id": "90b5e874efb6", "topic": "genetics", "outcome": "gen-o8a", "src": "img/slides/90b5e874efb6.webp", "caption": "Results of the monohybrid cross Pp × Pp: the <b>genotype ratio</b> is 1 PP : 2 Pp : 1 pp, and the <b>phenotype ratio</b> is 3 purple : 1 white, because PP and Pp both look purple.", "from": "2026 SCIENCE STANDARD · page 68"},
  {"id": "c29a05c8d510", "topic": "genetics", "outcome": "gen-o8d", "src": "img/slides/c29a05c8d510.webp", "caption": "Pedigree of an <b>autosomal recessive</b> trait with genotypes written in (f = recessive allele, ff = affected and shaded). Unaffected parents 1 and 2 in generation I (both Ff carriers) have affected children (ff), which proves the trait is recessive. An unaffected person with two carrier parents could be FF or Ff, which is why the chart writes 'FF/Ff'.", "from": "2026 SCIENCE STANDARD · page 70"},
  {"id": "22e1919a65c8", "topic": "genetics", "outcome": "gen-o8d", "src": "img/slides/22e1919a65c8.webp", "caption": "<b>Autosomal dominant</b> pedigree: the trait appears in every generation, males and females are affected about equally, and every affected person has at least one affected parent. The key clue is that two affected parents (1 and 2) have an unaffected son (7), so both parents must be heterozygous and the trait must be dominant.", "from": "2026 SCIENCE STANDARD · page 72"},
  {"id": "c2bf2ac2ac28", "topic": "genetics", "outcome": "gen-o8d", "src": "img/slides/c2bf2ac2ac28.webp", "caption": "<b>Autosomal recessive</b> pedigree: unaffected parents (8 and 9) have an affected daughter (17), so the trait must be recessive and both parents are carriers. It cannot be X-linked recessive, because an affected daughter would then need an affected father, and 9 is unaffected.", "from": "2026 SCIENCE STANDARD · page 73"},
  {"id": "44b3880c14db", "topic": "genetics", "outcome": "gen-o8d", "src": "img/slides/44b3880c14db.webp", "caption": "<b>X-linked dominant</b> pedigree: an affected father passes the trait to <b>all his daughters and none of his sons</b>, because daughters get his X and sons get his Y. An affected heterozygous mother passes it to about half of her sons and daughters. Overall, more females are affected than males.", "from": "2026 SCIENCE STANDARD · page 73"},
  {"id": "f2ab4a5184aa", "topic": "genetics", "outcome": "gen-o8d", "src": "img/slides/f2ab4a5184aa.webp", "caption": "<b>X-linked recessive</b> pedigree (e.g. colour blindness, haemophilia): mostly males are affected, because they have only one X. An affected mother (1) passes the allele to every child, so all her sons are affected and her daughters are carriers. A carrier mother (4) has a 50% chance of having an affected son.", "from": "2026 SCIENCE STANDARD · page 74"},
  {"id": "d76d36902836", "topic": "genetics", "outcome": "gen-o8d", "src": "img/slides/d76d36902836.webp", "caption": "<b>Y-linked</b> pedigree: only males are affected, and an affected father passes the trait to <b>every son</b> (who all get his Y chromosome) and to no daughters. Sons of unaffected fathers (e.g. 12 and 13, whose father 7 is unaffected) are never affected.", "from": "2026 SCIENCE STANDARD · page 74"},
  {"id": "a84f6575f8dc", "topic": "genetics", "outcome": "gen-o9b", "src": "img/slides/a84f6575f8dc.webp", "caption": "A student factsheet on <b>genetic modification</b>: changing an organism's DNA, often by inserting a gene from another species (e.g. with CRISPR-Cas9). Examples are Flavr Savr tomatoes (slower softening), non-browning Arctic apples and fast-growing AquAdvantage salmon. To evaluate a technology, weigh benefits (higher yields, pest and drought resistance, medical uses) against limitations (ethical concerns, consumer distrust, uncertain long-term effects).", "from": "2026 SCIENCE STANDARD · page 82"}
);
SD.mcq.push(
  {"id": "pic-7286b005e340", "topic": "genetics", "outcome": "gen-o7b", "q": "<figure class=\"q-fig\"><img class=\"zoom\" src=\"img/slides/7286b005e340.webp\" alt=\"\" data-cap=\"&lt;b&gt;Nondisjunction&lt;/b&gt; means chromosomes fail to separate during meiosis. (A) shows normal meiosis, (B) shows failure in meiosis I, where all four gametes are abnormal, and (C) shows failure in meiosis II, where two gametes are normal. Gametes end up with an extra chromosome (disomic) or a missing one (nullisomic).\" data-from=\"Mutations · slide 12\"></figure>In panel (B) of this diagram, when does nondisjunction occur?", "options": ["Meiosis I", "Meiosis II", "Mitosis", "Fertilisation"], "answer": 0, "explain": "In (B) the homologous pair fails to separate at MI, so one cell gets both chromosomes and the other gets none. As a result, all four gametes are abnormal.", "source": "Slide picture", "pic": true},
  {"id": "pic-f6d9d684c1a6", "topic": "genetics", "outcome": "gen-o7b", "q": "<figure class=\"q-fig\"><img class=\"zoom\" src=\"img/slides/f6d9d684c1a6.webp\" alt=\"\" data-cap=\"A karyotype lines up the 22 pairs of &lt;b&gt;autosomes&lt;/b&gt; (1–22) and the &lt;b&gt;sex chromosomes&lt;/b&gt; (X, Y). This one has two X chromosomes and one Y, which is 47,XXY (Klinefelter syndrome), an abnormal number of sex chromosomes.\" data-from=\"Mutations · slide 9\"></figure>What does this karyotype show?", "options": ["A normal male (46, XY)", "A normal female (46, XX)", "Klinefelter syndrome (47, XXY)", "Down syndrome (trisomy 21)"], "answer": 2, "explain": "There are two X chromosomes plus a Y, and only two copies of chromosome 21. That makes 47 chromosomes with XXY sex chromosomes.", "source": "Slide picture", "pic": true},
  {"id": "pic-4c5323dae901", "topic": "genetics", "outcome": "gen-o8a", "q": "<figure class=\"q-fig\"><img class=\"zoom\" src=\"img/slides/4c5323dae901.webp\" alt=\"\" data-cap=\"A heterozygous plant (Yy) crossed with a homozygous recessive plant (yy) gives 2 Yy : 2 yy, so 50% show the dominant phenotype and 50% the recessive. This 1 : 1 result is what a test cross gives when the parent is heterozygous. (The slide shows yellow pods as dominant, but in Mendel&#x27;s peas green pod colour is dominant; yellow is the dominant &lt;i&gt;seed&lt;/i&gt; colour.)\" data-from=\"Inheritance Patterns_ PUNNETT &amp; PEDIGREES · slide 8\"></figure>What phenotypic ratio does the cross in this Punnett square give?", "options": ["3 : 1", "1 : 2 : 1", "All show the dominant phenotype", "1 : 1"], "answer": 3, "explain": "Yy × yy gives two Yy (dominant phenotype) and two yy (recessive phenotype) boxes, which is a 1 : 1 ratio.", "source": "Slide picture", "pic": true},
  {"id": "pic-f591da5df5c8", "topic": "genetics", "outcome": "gen-o8a", "q": "<figure class=\"q-fig\"><img class=\"zoom\" src=\"img/slides/f591da5df5c8.webp\" alt=\"\" data-cap=\"A test cross in &lt;i&gt;Drosophila&lt;/i&gt;: a long-winged fly of unknown genotype is crossed with a short-winged (vv) fly. If all offspring have long wings, the parent was VV (Cross 1); if half have short wings, the parent was Vv (Cross 2).\" data-from=\"Inheritance Patterns_ PUNNETT &amp; PEDIGREES · slide 23\"></figure>In Cross 2 of this test cross, what does the result tell you about the long-winged parent?", "options": ["It is homozygous dominant (VV)", "It is heterozygous (Vv)", "It is homozygous recessive (vv)", "Its genotype cannot be determined"], "answer": 1, "explain": "Half the offspring are vv with short wings, so the long-winged parent must have passed on a v allele. Its genotype is therefore Vv.", "source": "Slide picture", "pic": true},
  {"id": "pic-e8a02e79121e", "topic": "genetics", "outcome": "gen-o8b", "q": "<figure class=\"q-fig\"><img class=\"zoom\" src=\"img/slides/e8a02e79121e.webp\" alt=\"\" data-cap=\"Punnett square for red (R&lt;sub&gt;1&lt;/sub&gt;R&lt;sub&gt;1&lt;/sub&gt;) × white (R&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt;R&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt;) snapdragons. Every F&lt;sub&gt;1&lt;/sub&gt; plant is R&lt;sub&gt;1&lt;/sub&gt;R&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt; and pink; crossing two of these pink plants gives 1 red : 2 pink : 1 white.\" data-from=\"Inheritance Patterns_ PUNNETT &amp; PEDIGREES · slide 28\"></figure>All the offspring in this cross are R<sub>1</sub>R<sub>2</sub>. What colour are their flowers?", "options": ["Red", "White", "Pink", "Red and white patches"], "answer": 2, "explain": "Snapdragon flower colour shows incomplete dominance, so the heterozygote has an intermediate (pink) phenotype. Separate red and white patches would be co-dominance.", "source": "Slide picture", "pic": true},
  {"id": "pic-d64e4187d65c", "topic": "genetics", "outcome": "gen-o8b", "q": "<figure class=\"q-fig\"><img class=\"zoom\" src=\"img/slides/d64e4187d65c.webp\" alt=\"\" data-cap=\"Roan cattle show &lt;b&gt;co-dominance&lt;/b&gt;: a red (C&lt;sup&gt;R&lt;/sup&gt;C&lt;sup&gt;R&lt;/sup&gt;) × white (C&lt;sup&gt;W&lt;/sup&gt;C&lt;sup&gt;W&lt;/sup&gt;) cross gives C&lt;sup&gt;R&lt;/sup&gt;C&lt;sup&gt;W&lt;/sup&gt; calves. Both alleles are fully expressed, so each hair is either red or white, which gives the mixed roan coat. Unlike incomplete dominance, the colours are not blended.\" data-from=\"Inheritance Patterns_ PUNNETT &amp; PEDIGREES · slide 29\"></figure>The roan coat of this cow (a mixture of separate red hairs and white hairs) is an example of:", "options": ["Co-dominance", "Complete dominance", "Incomplete dominance", "Sex-linked inheritance"], "answer": 0, "explain": "Both alleles are fully expressed side by side, as individual red and white hairs, rather than blended. That makes it co-dominance.", "source": "Slide picture", "pic": true},
  {"id": "pic-991661034957", "topic": "genetics", "outcome": "gen-o8d", "q": "<figure class=\"q-fig\"><img class=\"zoom\" src=\"img/slides/991661034957.webp\" alt=\"\" data-cap=\"Autosomal dominant pedigree (freckles, F dominant). The trait appears in every generation and affects males and females about equally, and every affected child has an affected parent.\" data-from=\"Inheritance Patterns_ PUNNETT &amp; PEDIGREES · slide 45\"></figure>Freckles (F) are dominant to no freckles (f). What is the genotype of individual I-2 in this pedigree?", "options": ["ff", "FF", "X<sup>F</sup>X<sup>f</sup>", "Ff"], "answer": 3, "explain": "I-2 is affected, so she has at least one F. She also has an unaffected child (ff), so she must have passed on an f, which makes her Ff.", "source": "Slide picture", "pic": true},
  {"id": "pic-799d85dc4b9e", "topic": "genetics", "outcome": "gen-o8d", "q": "<figure class=\"q-fig\"><img class=\"zoom\" src=\"img/slides/799d85dc4b9e.webp\" alt=\"\" data-cap=\"Unaffected parents (I-1 × I-2 and II-3 × II-4) have affected children, so the trait is recessive. The affected daughter III-10 has an unaffected father, which rules out X-linked recessive, so this is autosomal recessive.\" data-from=\"Inheritance Patterns_ PUNNETT &amp; PEDIGREES · slide 48\"></figure>What type of inheritance does this pedigree most likely show?", "options": ["Autosomal recessive", "Autosomal dominant", "X-linked recessive", "X-linked dominant"], "answer": 0, "explain": "Two unaffected parents having an affected child shows the trait is recessive. An affected daughter (III-10) with an unaffected father cannot happen with X-linked recessive, so the pattern is autosomal recessive.", "source": "Slide picture", "pic": true},
  {"id": "pic-f58a7f958fe6", "topic": "genetics", "outcome": "gen-o8d", "q": "<figure class=\"q-fig\"><img class=\"zoom\" src=\"img/slides/f58a7f958fe6.webp\" alt=\"\" data-cap=\"X-linked recessive pedigree (colour blindness). The trait mostly affects males and seems to skip a generation: the colour-blind grandfather passes the allele to his daughters (carriers), who then pass it to some of their sons.\" data-from=\"Inheritance Patterns_ PUNNETT &amp; PEDIGREES · slide 63\"></figure>In this colour-blindness pedigree, what is the genotype of the two daughters in generation II?", "options": ["X<sup>B</sup>X<sup>B</sup>", "X<sup>B</sup>X<sup>b</sup>", "X<sup>b</sup>X<sup>b</sup>", "X<sup>B</sup>Y"], "answer": 1, "explain": "Each daughter must receive her father's X<sup>b</sup> but has normal vision, so she is a carrier (X<sup>B</sup>X<sup>b</sup>). She then passes X<sup>b</sup> to some of her sons in generation III.", "source": "Slide picture", "pic": true},
  {"id": "pic-15df563e67fd", "topic": "genetics", "outcome": "gen-o8d", "q": "<figure class=\"q-fig\"><img class=\"zoom\" src=\"img/slides/15df563e67fd.webp\" alt=\"\" data-cap=\"X-linked dominant pedigree (vitamin D-resistant rickets). The affected father II-2 passes his X to every daughter, so all his daughters are affected and none of his sons are. An affected heterozygous mother passes the trait to about half of her children of either sex.\" data-from=\"Inheritance Patterns_ PUNNETT &amp; PEDIGREES · slide 38\"></figure>What type of inheritance does this pedigree show?", "options": ["Autosomal recessive", "Y-linked", "X-linked dominant", "X-linked recessive"], "answer": 2, "explain": "The affected father II-2 has only affected daughters and only unaffected sons, and the trait appears in every generation. That pattern is X-linked dominant.", "source": "Slide picture", "pic": true},
  {"id": "pic-903a214ca785", "topic": "genetics", "outcome": "gen-o8d", "q": "<figure class=\"q-fig\"><img class=\"zoom\" src=\"img/slides/903a214ca785.webp\" alt=\"\" data-cap=\"Y-linked pedigree: only males are affected, and every son of an affected father is affected. The trait never passes through females, because the Y chromosome goes only from father to son.\" data-from=\"Inheritance Patterns_ PUNNETT &amp; PEDIGREES · slide 65\"></figure>Which inheritance pattern does this pedigree show?", "options": ["X-linked recessive", "Autosomal dominant", "Autosomal recessive", "Y-linked"], "answer": 3, "explain": "Only males are affected, every son of an affected father is affected, and daughters never are. This father-to-son pattern is Y-linked.", "source": "Slide picture", "pic": true},
  {"id": "pic-8f5c9cb41e19", "topic": "genetics", "outcome": "gen-o8d", "q": "<figure class=\"q-fig\"><img class=\"zoom\" src=\"img/slides/8f5c9cb41e19.webp\" alt=\"\" data-cap=\"Ruling out sex linkage: an affected father (I-1) with an unaffected daughter (II-6) rules out X-linked dominant. In the same pedigree, an affected mother with an unaffected son rules out X-linked recessive, and an affected father with an unaffected son rules out Y-linked, leaving an autosomal pattern.\" data-from=\"Inheritance Patterns_ PUNNETT &amp; PEDIGREES · slide 68\"></figure>The circled individuals (affected father I-1 and his unaffected daughter II-6) rule out which inheritance pattern?", "options": ["Y-linked", "Autosomal dominant", "X-linked recessive", "X-linked dominant"], "answer": 3, "explain": "A father passes his only X chromosome to every daughter. If the trait were X-linked dominant, all of I-1's daughters would be affected.", "source": "Slide picture", "pic": true},
  {"id": "pic-3df251dfec31", "topic": "evolution", "outcome": "evo-o2", "q": "<figure class=\"q-fig\"><img class=\"zoom\" src=\"img/slides/3df251dfec31.webp\" alt=\"\" data-cap=\"Peppered moths (&lt;i&gt;Biston betularia&lt;/i&gt;) on soot-darkened bark: the dark form is camouflaged while the light form stands out to birds. During the Industrial Revolution, birds ate more light moths, so dark moths survived and reproduced more and became common.\" data-from=\"Evolution ~ Theories · slide 30\"></figure>On the dark, soot-covered bark in this image, which moth is more likely to survive and reproduce?", "options": ["The light, speckled moth, because it is more visible to birds", "The dark moth, because it is camouflaged from predatory birds", "Both equally, because birds do not eat moths", "The dark moth, because the soot made it darker during its life"], "answer": 1, "explain": "Birds are the selection pressure, and the dark moth is harder for them to see on dark bark. It survives to pass on its dark-colour allele (the colour is inherited, not caused by soot).", "source": "Slide picture", "pic": true},
  {"id": "pic-dde9f50af814", "topic": "evolution", "outcome": "evo-o3b", "q": "<figure class=\"q-fig\"><img class=\"zoom\" src=\"img/slides/dde9f50af814.webp\" alt=\"\" data-cap=\"A species is a group that can interbreed to produce fertile offspring. A horse (2n = 64) and a donkey (2n = 62) can produce a mule (2n = 63), but its odd chromosome number means the chromosomes cannot pair in meiosis, so the mule is infertile and the horse and donkey are separate species.\" data-from=\"Evolution ~ Theories · slide 17\"></figure>Why are the horse and donkey in this image classified as separate species?", "options": ["They look different from each other", "They live in different habitats", "Their offspring, the mule, is infertile", "They cannot mate at all"], "answer": 2, "explain": "Horses and donkeys can mate, but the mule (2n = 63) cannot form normal gametes. Since they cannot produce fertile offspring, they are different species.", "source": "Slide picture", "pic": true},
  {"id": "pic-69d6a0380a9e", "topic": "evolution", "outcome": "evo-o3b", "q": "<figure class=\"q-fig\"><img class=\"zoom\" src=\"img/slides/69d6a0380a9e.webp\" alt=\"\" data-cap=\"A shark (fish), an ichthyosaur (reptile) and a dolphin (mammal) all evolved a streamlined body, fins and a tail fin independently. The same selection pressure (moving fast through water) produced similar shapes in unrelated groups.\" data-from=\"Evolution ~ Theories · slide 38\"></figure>The shark (fish), ichthyosaur (reptile) and dolphin (mammal) in this image have similar streamlined bodies. What does this show?", "options": ["Homologous structures", "Divergent evolution", "Artificial selection", "Convergent evolution"], "answer": 3, "explain": "Unrelated groups evolved similar features because they faced similar selection pressures in water. That is convergent evolution, and their body shapes are analogous, not homologous.", "source": "Slide picture", "pic": true},
  {"id": "pic-c526f49204ad", "topic": "evolution", "outcome": "evo-o4a", "q": "<figure class=\"q-fig\"><img class=\"zoom\" src=\"img/slides/c526f49204ad.webp\" alt=\"\" data-cap=\"The &lt;b&gt;pentadactyl limb&lt;/b&gt;: bat wing, mouse forelimb, whale flipper and human arm all have the same bones (humerus, radius, ulna, metacarpals, fingers), modified for different jobs. These &lt;b&gt;homologous structures&lt;/b&gt; point to a common ancestor.\" data-from=\"Evolution ~ Evidence (10SCL) · slide 7\"></figure>The bat wing, mouse forelimb, whale flipper and human arm in this diagram are examples of:", "options": ["Analogous structures", "Vestigial structures", "Homologous structures", "Transitional fossils"], "answer": 2, "explain": "They share the same basic arrangement of bones but have different functions. Homologous structures like these are evidence of a common ancestor.", "source": "Slide picture", "pic": true},
  {"id": "pic-b7217194a488", "topic": "evolution", "outcome": "evo-o4a", "q": "<figure class=\"q-fig\"><img class=\"zoom\" src=\"img/slides/b7217194a488.webp\" alt=\"\" data-cap=\"Early embryos of a fish, reptile, bird and human look very alike, all with a tail and pharyngeal slits. The slide calls these &#x27;gill slits&#x27;, but only fish develop them into gills; in humans they become parts of the jaw, ear and throat. Shared embryo features suggest a common ancestor (comparative embryology).\" data-from=\"Evolution ~ Evidence (10SCL) · slide 11\"></figure>Which piece of evidence for evolution does this image show?", "options": ["Comparative embryology", "Comparative anatomy of adult limbs", "Biogeography", "The fossil record"], "answer": 0, "explain": "It compares the early embryos of different vertebrates, which share a tail and pharyngeal slits. This is comparative embryology.", "source": "Slide picture", "pic": true},
  {"id": "pic-743c790883c0", "topic": "evolution", "outcome": "evo-o4b", "q": "<figure class=\"q-fig\"><img class=\"zoom\" src=\"img/slides/743c790883c0.webp\" alt=\"\" data-cap=\"DNA hybridisation: single strands from two species are joined and then heated. The more similar the DNA, the more bases pair up and the higher the temperature needed to separate the hybrid strands, so the more closely related the species are.\" data-from=\"Evolution ~ Evidence (10SCL) · slide 20\"></figure>According to this DNA hybridisation diagram, which species are most closely related?", "options": ["Species A and B, because their hybrid strands separate easily", "Species A and C, because their hybrid strands are difficult to separate", "Species B and C, because their DNA is a different colour", "None, because DNA hybridisation cannot show relationships"], "answer": 1, "explain": "The A–C hybrid strands have many matching base pairs, so a higher temperature is needed to separate them. Similar sequences mean a more recent common ancestor.", "source": "Slide picture", "pic": true},
  {"id": "pic-e25685061d1c", "topic": "evolution", "outcome": "evo-o4b", "q": "<figure class=\"q-fig\"><img class=\"zoom\" src=\"img/slides/e25685061d1c.webp\" alt=\"\" data-cap=\"Biochemical evidence compares the amino acid sequence of the same protein (haemoglobin) in different species. Fewer differences mean a more recent common ancestor (&#x27;Iso&#x27; on the slide is isoleucine, normally abbreviated Ile).\" data-from=\"Evolution ~ Evidence (10SCL) · slide 23\"></figure>Based on this haemoglobin table, which species is most closely related to humans?", "options": ["Horse", "Gorilla", "Zebra", "Chimpanzee"], "answer": 3, "explain": "The chimpanzee sequence (Lys–Glu–His–Iso) is identical to the human one, while the gorilla differs by one amino acid and the horse and zebra by more.", "source": "Slide picture", "pic": true},
  {"id": "pic-03a2e8418d30", "topic": "evolution", "outcome": "evo-o4c", "q": "<figure class=\"q-fig\"><img class=\"zoom\" src=\"img/slides/03a2e8418d30.webp\" alt=\"\" data-cap=\"Absolute (radiometric) dating uses the constant decay rate of a radioactive isotope. Carbon-14 has a half-life of about 5,730 years, so after each half-life only half of the remaining &lt;sup&gt;14&lt;/sup&gt;C is left. It is only useful for remains up to about 50,000 years old; older rocks are dated with isotopes such as potassium-40.\" data-from=\"Evolution ~ Evidence (10SCL) · slide 30\"></figure>Using this graph, what percentage of the original carbon-14 remains after 2 half-lives?", "options": ["50%", "25%", "12.5%", "0%"], "answer": 1, "explain": "Each half-life halves the amount remaining: 100% becomes 50% after 1 half-life and 25% after 2.", "source": "Slide picture", "pic": true},
  {"id": "pic-30ea295f0e61", "topic": "evolution", "outcome": "evo-o4c", "q": "<figure class=\"q-fig\"><img class=\"zoom\" src=\"img/slides/30ea295f0e61.webp\" alt=\"\" data-cap=\"The horse fossil record over about 55 million years runs from the small, many-toed &lt;i&gt;Hyracotherium&lt;/i&gt; to modern &lt;i&gt;Equus&lt;/i&gt;. Body size increased and the forefoot reduced to one hoofed toe, changes linked to the shift from forest to open grassland, where speed helped escape predators.\" data-from=\"Evolution ~ Evidence (10SCL) · slide 42\"></figure>What trend in the forefoot does this horse fossil series show?", "options": ["The number of toes increased over time", "The forefoot stayed the same for 55 million years", "The number of toes reduced to a single hoofed toe", "The forefoot became webbed for swimming"], "answer": 2, "explain": "<i>Hyracotherium</i> had several toes on its forefoot, but <i>Equus</i> walks on a single hoofed toe. The side toes became smaller over time.", "source": "Slide picture", "pic": true},
  {"id": "pic-5f17dd3eb748", "topic": "evolution", "outcome": "evo-o5", "q": "<figure class=\"q-fig\"><img class=\"zoom\" src=\"img/slides/5f17dd3eb748.webp\" alt=\"\" data-cap=\"How a fossil forms: (a) the organism dies, (b) soft tissues decay and the remains are quickly buried in sediment, (c) more sediment builds up and hardens into rock while minerals replace the bone, and (d) uplift and erosion expose it until (e) it is discovered.\" data-from=\"Evolution ~ Evidence (10SCL) · slide 33\"></figure>Which stage in this sequence must happen quickly for a fossil to form?", "options": ["Burial in sediment (b)", "Uplift and erosion (d)", "Discovery (e)", "Sediment accumulation over millions of years (c)"], "answer": 0, "explain": "Rapid burial protects the remains from scavengers, decomposers and weathering. Without it the remains are destroyed before they can fossilise.", "source": "Slide picture", "pic": true},
  {"id": "pic-9ad13a7e2370", "topic": "evolution", "outcome": "evo-o6", "q": "<figure class=\"q-fig\"><img class=\"zoom\" src=\"img/slides/9ad13a7e2370.webp\" alt=\"\" data-cap=\"Combining an index fossil with relative dating: fossil B occurs at both sites. At Site One A lies above B, and at Site Two B lies above C, so A is younger than C even though they are never found together.\" data-from=\"Evolution ~ Evidence (10SCL) · slide 39\"></figure>Using index fossil B to match the two sites, what is the order of the fossils from oldest to youngest?", "options": ["A, B, C", "B, A, C", "C, B, A", "A, C, B"], "answer": 2, "explain": "At Site Two, C lies below B, so C is older than B. At Site One, A lies above B, so A is younger, giving the order C, B, A.", "source": "Slide picture", "pic": true},
  {"id": "pic-a986ba3eca01", "topic": "universe", "outcome": "uni-o1", "q": "<figure class=\"q-fig\"><img class=\"zoom\" src=\"img/slides/a986ba3eca01.webp\" alt=\"\" data-cap=\"Side-on view of the Milky Way: a flat disc of stars about 100 000 light-years across with a bulging centre. The Sun sits in the disc about 28 000 light-years from the galactic centre.\" data-from=\"The Universe Summary + TECHNOLOGIES Powerpoint · slide 16\"></figure>According to this diagram, about how far is the Sun from the centre of the Milky Way?", "options": ["22 000 light-years", "50 000 light-years", "28 000 light-years", "100 000 light-years"], "answer": 2, "explain": "The diagram marks 28 000 LY from the Sun to the galactic central core. 50 000 LY is the radius of the disc and 100 000 LY is its full diameter.", "source": "Slide picture", "pic": true},
  {"id": "pic-0260c1286033", "topic": "universe", "outcome": "uni-o4", "q": "<figure class=\"q-fig\"><img class=\"zoom\" src=\"img/slides/0260c1286033.webp\" alt=\"\" data-cap=\"Every star begins in a nebula. A low- or medium-mass star like the Sun goes main sequence → red giant → planetary nebula → white dwarf, while a high-mass star goes main sequence → red supergiant → supernova → neutron star or (if very massive) black hole.\" data-from=\"Colours of Stars and their Age, Size, Distance from Earth and the HR Diagram · slide 4\"></figure>According to this diagram, what will the Sun finally become?", "options": ["A black hole", "A neutron star", "A red supergiant", "A white dwarf"], "answer": 3, "explain": "The Sun is a low/medium-mass star (top row), which ends as a white dwarf after shedding a planetary nebula. Only high-mass stars become neutron stars or black holes.", "source": "Slide picture", "pic": true},
  {"id": "pic-ee6678d5868d", "topic": "universe", "outcome": "uni-o4", "q": "<figure class=\"q-fig\"><img class=\"zoom\" src=\"img/slides/ee6678d5868d.webp\" alt=\"\" data-cap=\"A detailed life cycle: a Sun-like star goes main sequence → red giant → planetary nebula → white dwarf, which slowly cools (a &#x27;black dwarf&#x27; is predicted, but the universe is too young for any to exist yet). A more massive star becomes a supergiant and explodes as a supernova, leaving a neutron star or a black hole; the core does not really &#x27;vanish&#x27;, it collapses so densely that not even light can escape.\" data-from=\"Major Features of the Universe · slide 10\"></figure>In this diagram, which stage comes directly after the red giant for a star of lower mass, like the Sun?", "options": ["Supernova", "Planetary nebula", "Neutron star", "Supergiant"], "answer": 1, "explain": "A red giant puffs off its outer layers of gas as a planetary nebula, exposing the hot core as a white dwarf. Supernovae and neutron stars belong to the high-mass path.", "source": "Slide picture", "pic": true},
  {"id": "pic-23959ff0113c", "topic": "universe", "outcome": "uni-o4", "q": "<figure class=\"q-fig\"><img class=\"zoom\" src=\"img/slides/23959ff0113c.webp\" alt=\"\" data-cap=\"The Helix Nebula, a &lt;b&gt;planetary nebula&lt;/b&gt;: the glowing shell of gas puffed off by a dying Sun-like star, with the hot white-dwarf core at its centre. Despite the name, it has nothing to do with planets.\" data-from=\"The Universe Summary + TECHNOLOGIES Powerpoint · slide 6\"></figure>This image shows a ring-shaped shell of glowing gas around a tiny, hot central star. What is it?", "options": ["A planetary nebula from a dying Sun-like star", "A supernova remnant from a massive star", "A spiral galaxy", "A protostar forming inside a nebula"], "answer": 0, "explain": "A planetary nebula forms when a red giant gently puffs off its outer layers, leaving a white dwarf in the middle. It is unrelated to planets.", "source": "Slide picture", "pic": true},
  {"id": "pic-1a502ca2ec70", "topic": "universe", "outcome": "uni-o5", "q": "<figure class=\"q-fig\"><img class=\"zoom\" src=\"img/slides/1a502ca2ec70.webp\" alt=\"\" data-cap=\"A massive star before (left) and the expanding supernova remnant after (right). When a supergiant runs out of fuel its core collapses and the outer layers are blasted into space, spreading heavy elements that later form new stars and planets.\" data-from=\"Major Features of the Universe · slide 19\"></figure>These images show a massive star 'before' and 'after'. What event happened in between?", "options": ["The star cooled into a white dwarf", "The star shed a planetary nebula", "The star exploded as a supernova", "The star collapsed into a protostar"], "answer": 2, "explain": "A massive (supergiant) star ends its life in a supernova explosion, leaving an expanding cloud of debris called a supernova remnant.", "source": "Slide picture", "pic": true},
  {"id": "pic-d45b23008527", "topic": "universe", "outcome": "uni-o7", "q": "<figure class=\"q-fig\"><img class=\"zoom\" src=\"img/slides/d45b23008527.webp\" alt=\"\" data-cap=\"The apparent magnitude scale runs &#x27;backwards&#x27;: the more negative the number, the brighter an object looks from Earth. The Sun (about −27) is brightest, the unaided eye can see down to about +6, and the James Webb Space Telescope can detect objects fainter than +29.\" data-from=\"Colours of Stars and their Age, Size, Distance from Earth and the HR Diagram · slide 7\"></figure>Using this scale, why can't Pluto (apparent magnitude about +14) be seen with the unaided eye?", "options": ["Its magnitude is lower than the eye limit, so it is too bright", "Its magnitude is higher than the unaided-eye limit (about +6), so it is too faint", "Its apparent magnitude is negative", "Apparent magnitude only applies to stars"], "answer": 1, "explain": "Larger positive magnitudes mean fainter objects. +14 is far beyond the unaided-eye limit of about +6, so a telescope is needed.", "source": "Slide picture", "pic": true},
  {"id": "pic-6d93494b1a35", "topic": "universe", "outcome": "uni-o8", "q": "<figure class=\"q-fig\"><img class=\"zoom\" src=\"img/slides/6d93494b1a35.webp\" alt=\"\" data-cap=\"Apparent magnitude (m) is how bright a star looks from Earth; absolute magnitude (M) is how bright it would look from a standard distance of 10 parsecs. Sirius (m = −1.5) looks about 4 times brighter than Rigel (m = +0.1) only because it is about 100 times closer; Rigel (M = −7.0) is really around 2000 times more luminous.\" data-from=\"Colours of Stars and their Age, Size, Distance from Earth and the HR Diagram · slide 10\"></figure>Using the table, why does Sirius look brighter than Rigel from Earth, even though Rigel is far more luminous?", "options": ["Sirius is hotter than Rigel", "Rigel is a white dwarf", "Sirius is much closer to Earth (8.6 ly vs about 860 ly)", "Rigel has a more negative apparent magnitude"], "answer": 2, "explain": "Apparent brightness depends on both luminosity and distance. Rigel's absolute magnitude (−7.0) shows it is far more luminous, but it is about 100 times further away.", "source": "Slide picture", "pic": true},
  {"id": "pic-35332f335968", "topic": "universe", "outcome": "uni-o9", "q": "<figure class=\"q-fig\"><img class=\"zoom\" src=\"img/slides/35332f335968.webp\" alt=\"\" data-cap=\"The spectral classes O, B, A, F, G, K, M run from hottest (blue, about 30 000 K and above) to coolest (red, about 3 000 K). The Sun is a yellowish G-type star at about 5 800 K.\" data-from=\"Colours of Stars and their Age, Size, Distance from Earth and the HR Diagram · slide 15\"></figure>A star has a surface temperature of about 5 800 K. Using this chart, which spectral class is it?", "options": ["B", "G", "O", "M"], "answer": 1, "explain": "On the temperature bar, about 5 000–6 000 K falls in class G, the same class as the Sun. O and B stars are much hotter and M stars are cooler.", "source": "Slide picture", "pic": true},
  {"id": "pic-465912b21301", "topic": "universe", "outcome": "uni-o11", "q": "<figure class=\"q-fig\"><img class=\"zoom\" src=\"img/slides/465912b21301.webp\" alt=\"\" data-cap=\"A Hertzsprung–Russell diagram plots absolute magnitude (brightness) against spectral type/temperature, with the hottest stars on the &lt;b&gt;left&lt;/b&gt;. Most stars lie on the main sequence; giants and supergiants (top right) are cool but bright because they are huge, and white dwarfs (bottom left) are hot but dim because they are small.\" data-from=\"Colours of Stars and their Age, Size, Distance from Earth and the HR Diagram · slide 17\"></figure>In this H–R diagram, where are the stars that are hot but dim?", "options": ["Top right (giants and supergiants)", "Top left (hot O and B main-sequence stars)", "Bottom right (cool M-type main-sequence stars)", "Bottom left (white dwarfs)"], "answer": 3, "explain": "Temperature increases to the left and brightness increases upwards, so hot but dim stars sit bottom left. These are white dwarfs, which are dim because they are very small.", "source": "Slide picture", "pic": true},
  {"id": "pic-bbd3d77172e5", "topic": "universe", "outcome": "uni-o11", "q": "<figure class=\"q-fig\"><img class=\"zoom\" src=\"img/slides/bbd3d77172e5.webp\" alt=\"\" data-cap=\"An H–R diagram with luminosity (Sun = 1) going up and temperature decreasing to the right; dot size shows star size. The Sun is an average main-sequence star, while supergiants like Betelgeuse and Antares are cooler than the Sun but tens of thousands of times more luminous.\" data-from=\"Colours of Stars and their Age, Size, Distance from Earth and the HR Diagram · slide 9\"></figure>In this diagram, Betelgeuse is cooler than the Sun yet far more luminous. What is the best explanation?", "options": ["It is much closer to Earth than the Sun", "It is enormous, so its huge surface gives off far more light", "It is a white dwarf", "Cooler stars always give off more light"], "answer": 1, "explain": "Luminosity depends on temperature and surface area. Betelgeuse is a red supergiant, so its gigantic size more than makes up for its cooler surface.", "source": "Slide picture", "pic": true},
  {"id": "pic-232c91b6600d", "topic": "universe", "outcome": "uni-o11", "q": "<figure class=\"q-fig\"><img class=\"zoom\" src=\"img/slides/232c91b6600d.webp\" alt=\"\" data-cap=\"Along the main sequence, more massive stars are bigger, hotter, bluer and brighter, but they use up their fuel much faster. An O star (~40 000 K) lasts only about 10 million years, the Sun (G) about 10 billion years, and a small red M star over 100 billion years.\" data-from=\"Colours of Stars and their Age, Size, Distance from Earth and the HR Diagram · slide 19\"></figure>According to this image, which kind of main-sequence star has the shortest lifetime?", "options": ["O (massive, blue)", "A", "G (like the Sun)", "M (small, red)"], "answer": 0, "explain": "O stars are the most massive and hottest, so they fuse their hydrogen fastest and last only about 10 million years, while M stars last over 100 billion years.", "source": "Slide picture", "pic": true},
  {"id": "pic-76262d7b4bef", "topic": "universe", "outcome": "uni-o13", "q": "<figure class=\"q-fig\"><img class=\"zoom\" src=\"img/slides/76262d7b4bef.webp\" alt=\"\" data-cap=\"Artist&#x27;s impression of the Milky Way seen from above (we cannot photograph our own galaxy from outside). It is a barred spiral galaxy: notice the straight bar of stars through the bright centre, with spiral arms curving out from its ends.\" data-from=\"The Universe Summary + TECHNOLOGIES Powerpoint · slide 18\"></figure>What type of galaxy is shown in this image?", "options": ["Elliptical", "Irregular", "Barred spiral", "Spiral without a bar"], "answer": 2, "explain": "It has spiral arms that begin at the ends of a straight bar of stars through the centre, which makes it a barred spiral. This is how astronomers picture our Milky Way.", "source": "Slide picture", "pic": true},
  {"id": "pic-0dfeb5ddc5b3", "topic": "universe", "outcome": "uni-o14", "q": "<figure class=\"q-fig\"><img class=\"zoom\" src=\"img/slides/0dfeb5ddc5b3.webp\" alt=\"\" data-cap=\"A scale of distances in kilometres where each tick is 100× further than the last. Use kilometres for Earth-sized distances, astronomical units (AU) within the solar system, and light-years or parsecs for distances to stars and galaxies, so the numbers stay sensible.\" data-from=\"Measuring Distances in Space · slide 10\"></figure>Using this scale, which unit is most sensible for the distance to Proxima Centauri?", "options": ["Light-years", "Kilometres", "Astronomical units", "Metres"], "answer": 0, "explain": "Proxima Centauri lies well beyond the edge of the solar system, in the range covered by light-years/parsecs (it is about 4.2 light-years away).", "source": "Slide picture", "pic": true},
  {"id": "pic-4554f5bb5095", "topic": "universe", "outcome": "uni-o14", "q": "<figure class=\"q-fig\"><img class=\"zoom\" src=\"img/slides/4554f5bb5095.webp\" alt=\"\" data-cap=\"Light-travel distances from Earth: the Moon is 1.3 light-seconds away, the Sun 8 light-minutes, Proxima Centauri (the nearest star) 4.2 light-years and the Andromeda Galaxy 2.5 million light-years. Because light takes time to travel, we see distant objects as they were in the past.\" data-from=\"Measuring Distances in Space · slide 9\"></figure>Based on this image, when you look at the Andromeda Galaxy you see light that left it about how long ago?", "options": ["8 minutes ago", "4.2 years ago", "39 years ago", "2.5 million years ago"], "answer": 3, "explain": "Andromeda is 2.5 million light-years away, so its light has been travelling for 2.5 million years; we see it as it was then.", "source": "Slide picture", "pic": true},
  {"id": "pic-da86655caf14", "topic": "universe", "outcome": "uni-o16", "q": "<figure class=\"q-fig\"><img class=\"zoom\" src=\"img/slides/da86655caf14.webp\" alt=\"\" data-cap=\"&lt;b&gt;Stellar parallax&lt;/b&gt;: a nearby star is observed from opposite sides of Earth&#x27;s orbit, 6 months apart, and appears to shift against the much more distant background stars. The smaller the parallax angle &lt;i&gt;p&lt;/i&gt;, the further away the star (distance &lt;i&gt;d&lt;/i&gt;).\" data-from=\"The Universe Summary + TECHNOLOGIES Powerpoint · slide 33\"></figure>In this diagram, why are the two observations made 6 months apart?", "options": ["So the star has time to move through space", "So Earth is on opposite sides of its orbit, giving the widest baseline (2 AU)", "So the Sun is not in the way", "So the background stars have time to move"], "answer": 1, "explain": "In 6 months Earth moves to the opposite side of its orbit, giving the largest possible baseline and therefore the largest, most measurable shift in the star's apparent position.", "source": "Slide picture", "pic": true},
  {"id": "pic-5c9c93f2cf97", "topic": "universe", "outcome": "uni-o17", "q": "<figure class=\"q-fig\"><img class=\"zoom\" src=\"img/slides/5c9c93f2cf97.webp\" alt=\"\" data-cap=\"A reflecting telescope: a large curved mirror gathers light and reflects it to a small mirror, which sends the image into the eyepiece. Bigger mirrors collect more light, so astronomers can see fainter, more distant objects.\" data-from=\"The Universe Summary + TECHNOLOGIES Powerpoint · slide 37\"></figure>What type of telescope is shown in this diagram?", "options": ["Reflecting telescope", "Refracting telescope", "Radio telescope", "X-ray telescope"], "answer": 0, "explain": "It uses mirrors (a large one to gather light and a small one to send it to the eyepiece), so it is a reflecting telescope. A refracting telescope uses lenses instead.", "source": "Slide picture", "pic": true},
  {"id": "pic-9bd16d24c744", "topic": "universe", "outcome": "uni-o17", "q": "<figure class=\"q-fig\"><img class=\"zoom\" src=\"img/slides/9bd16d24c744.webp\" alt=\"\" data-cap=\"Jupiter with four of its moons (Io, Europa, Ganymede and Callisto) through a small telescope, the view Galileo recorded night after night in 1610. Seeing bodies orbit Jupiter showed that not everything orbits Earth, supporting the Sun-centred model.\" data-from=\"The Universe Summary + TECHNOLOGIES Powerpoint · slide 36\"></figure>Galileo saw these small points of light near Jupiter change position from night to night. What did he conclude?", "options": ["They were distant stars behind Jupiter", "They were comets passing by", "They were craters on Jupiter's surface", "They were moons orbiting Jupiter"], "answer": 3, "explain": "The points stayed close to Jupiter but moved from side to side, so they had to be orbiting it. This showed that not everything orbits Earth.", "source": "Slide picture", "pic": true},
  {"id": "pic-f5791f5118d2", "topic": "universe", "outcome": "uni-o20", "q": "<figure class=\"q-fig\"><img class=\"zoom\" src=\"img/slides/f5791f5118d2.webp\" alt=\"\" data-cap=\"Hubble&#x27;s graph of galaxies&#x27; recession velocity against distance. The straight-line trend (Hubble&#x27;s law, v = H&lt;sub&gt;0&lt;/sub&gt; × d) shows that the further away a galaxy is, the faster it is moving away (the more red-shifted its light), which is evidence that the universe is expanding.\" data-from=\"Big Bang Theory Summarised · slide 10\"></figure>What does the trend in this graph show?", "options": ["Nearer galaxies move away faster than distant ones", "Galaxies are moving towards us", "The further away a galaxy is, the faster it is moving away from us", "All galaxies move away at the same speed"], "answer": 2, "explain": "Recession velocity rises steadily with distance (a positive straight-line trend). This is Hubble's law and it shows the universe is expanding.", "source": "Slide picture", "pic": true},
  {"id": "pic-98353797f69f", "topic": "universe", "outcome": "uni-o21", "q": "<figure class=\"q-fig\"><img class=\"zoom\" src=\"img/slides/98353797f69f.webp\" alt=\"\" data-cap=\"Map of the &lt;b&gt;cosmic microwave background radiation (CMBR)&lt;/b&gt; across the whole sky, made by the WMAP satellite. The CMBR is the leftover heat of the Big Bang, now cooled to about 2.7 K; the colours show tiny temperature differences (about 1 part in 100 000) that later grew into galaxies.\" data-from=\"The Universe Summary + TECHNOLOGIES Powerpoint · slide 21\"></figure>This oval all-sky map shows tiny temperature differences in what?", "options": ["The cosmic microwave background radiation", "Stars in the Milky Way", "The Sun's surface", "Galaxy filaments seen in visible light"], "answer": 0, "explain": "This is the famous CMBR map: microwave radiation left over from the Big Bang, almost perfectly even across the sky, with tiny temperature variations shown in colour.", "source": "Slide picture", "pic": true},
  {"id": "pic-96b46ea27b93", "topic": "chemistry", "outcome": "chem-pre", "q": "<figure class=\"q-fig\"><img class=\"zoom\" src=\"img/slides/96b46ea27b93.webp\" alt=\"\" data-cap=\"Test for carbon dioxide: bubbling CO&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt; through clear limewater (calcium hydroxide solution) turns it milky. Acids react with carbonates and hydrogen carbonates to release CO&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt;, so this test identifies them.\" data-from=\"Chemical Reactions · slide 19\"></figure>This image shows a gas test. Which solution is in the test tubes?", "options": ["Sodium chloride solution", "Limewater (calcium hydroxide solution)", "Universal indicator", "Dilute hydrochloric acid"], "answer": 1, "explain": "Limewater is clear and turns milky (cloudy white) when carbon dioxide is bubbled through it, as shown.", "source": "Slide picture", "pic": true},
  {"id": "pic-6827be91f489", "topic": "chemistry", "outcome": "chem-o2", "q": "<figure class=\"q-fig\"><img class=\"zoom\" src=\"img/slides/6827be91f489.webp\" alt=\"\" data-cap=\"Endothermic: heat flows &lt;i&gt;into&lt;/i&gt; the reaction from the surroundings, so the surroundings feel colder. Exothermic: heat flows &lt;i&gt;out&lt;/i&gt; to the surroundings, so they feel hotter. Energy is conserved in both.\" data-from=\"Energy Transfer and Rates of Reaction · slide 17\"></figure>In the left-hand picture of this image, the heat arrows point into the reaction. What would a thermometer in the surroundings show?", "options": ["The temperature rises", "The temperature stays the same", "The temperature falls", "The temperature rises then falls"], "answer": 2, "explain": "This is an endothermic reaction: it absorbs heat from the surroundings, so their temperature drops.", "source": "Slide picture", "pic": true},
  {"id": "pic-c5dd7186a680", "topic": "chemistry", "outcome": "chem-o3", "q": "<figure class=\"q-fig\"><img class=\"zoom\" src=\"img/slides/c5dd7186a680.webp\" alt=\"\" data-cap=\"Labelled energy profiles. Activation energy is measured from the reactants up to the peak. In an endothermic reaction the products are higher than the reactants (energy absorbed); in an exothermic reaction they are lower (energy released).\" data-from=\"Energy Transfer and Rates of Reaction · slide 24\"></figure>In the endothermic profile in this image, the products sit higher than the reactants. What does this show?", "options": ["Energy is released to the surroundings", "Energy is absorbed from the surroundings", "No activation energy is needed", "A catalyst has been added"], "answer": 1, "explain": "Products with more energy than reactants means the reaction took in energy from the surroundings, which is what makes it endothermic.", "source": "Slide picture", "pic": true},
  {"id": "pic-b9e5545b6547", "topic": "chemistry", "outcome": "chem-o4", "q": "<figure class=\"q-fig\"><img class=\"zoom\" src=\"img/slides/b9e5545b6547.webp\" alt=\"\" data-cap=\"Collision theory: particles only react if they collide with the correct orientation &lt;b&gt;and&lt;/b&gt; with energy greater than or equal to the activation energy. Otherwise they bounce apart unchanged.\" data-from=\"Energy Transfer and Rates of Reaction · slide 4\"></figure>According to this diagram, why does the top (unsuccessful) collision not produce a product?", "options": ["The particles were too large to react", "No catalyst was present", "There were too many particles in the container", "The particles collided with the wrong orientation and less than the activation energy"], "answer": 3, "explain": "A successful collision needs the correct orientation and energy at least equal to the activation energy; the top collision has neither.", "source": "Slide picture", "pic": true},
  {"id": "pic-27a79402f23f", "topic": "chemistry", "outcome": "chem-o4", "q": "<figure class=\"q-fig\"><img class=\"zoom\" src=\"img/slides/27a79402f23f.webp\" alt=\"\" data-cap=\"Distribution of particle energies. Only the particles in the shaded tail to the right of the activation energy line have enough energy to react when they collide; most particles do not.\" data-from=\"Energy Transfer and Rates of Reaction · slide 4\"></figure>On this graph, what does the small shaded area to the right of the activation energy line represent?", "options": ["Particles that have enough energy to react", "Particles that have stopped moving", "The total number of particles", "Particles that have already formed products"], "answer": 0, "explain": "Only particles with energy at or above the activation energy (the shaded tail) can react when they collide.", "source": "Slide picture", "pic": true},
  {"id": "pic-03153693596c", "topic": "chemistry", "outcome": "chem-o5", "q": "<figure class=\"q-fig\"><img class=\"zoom\" src=\"img/slides/03153693596c.webp\" alt=\"\" data-cap=\"As the reaction A → B proceeds, reactant A (purple) decreases and product B (green) increases. The curves are steepest at the start, so the rate is fastest at the beginning and slows as A is used up.\" data-from=\"Energy Transfer and Rates of Reaction · slide 2\"></figure>Using this graph, during which time interval is the reaction rate fastest?", "options": ["0–10 s", "20–30 s", "40–50 s", "50–60 s"], "answer": 0, "explain": "The curves are steepest between 0 and 10 s (A drops from 50 to about 24), because the reactant concentration is highest at the start.", "source": "Slide picture", "pic": true},
  {"id": "pic-11adb7ec05b7", "topic": "chemistry", "outcome": "chem-o6", "q": "<figure class=\"q-fig\"><img class=\"zoom\" src=\"img/slides/11adb7ec05b7.webp\" alt=\"\" data-cap=\"Effect of temperature: at a higher temperature the energy curve flattens and shifts right, so a larger fraction of particles has energy above the activation (threshold) energy. Particles also collide more often, so the rate increases.\" data-from=\"Energy Transfer and Rates of Reaction · slide 8\"></figure>According to this graph, why does a higher temperature increase reaction rate?", "options": ["It lowers the activation energy", "More molecules have kinetic energy above the threshold energy", "It increases the number of molecules", "It changes the products formed"], "answer": 1, "explain": "The shaded area beyond the threshold is larger for the higher-temperature curve, so more collisions have enough energy to react. The activation energy itself does not change.", "source": "Slide picture", "pic": true},
  {"id": "pic-7ca764dac437", "topic": "chemistry", "outcome": "chem-o7", "q": "<figure class=\"q-fig\"><img class=\"zoom\" src=\"img/slides/7ca764dac437.webp\" alt=\"\" data-cap=\"A catalyst provides an alternative pathway with a lower activation energy (E&lt;sub&gt;a1&lt;/sub&gt; &amp;lt; E&lt;sub&gt;a2&lt;/sub&gt;). The reactant and product energies, and so the overall energy change, are unchanged.\" data-from=\"Energy Transfer and Rates of Reaction · slide 34\"></figure>According to this diagram, what does adding a catalyst change?", "options": ["The energy of the products", "The overall energy released", "The activation energy", "The energy of the reactants"], "answer": 2, "explain": "The catalysed (red) curve has a lower peak, but it starts and ends at the same energy levels as the uncatalysed (blue) curve.", "source": "Slide picture", "pic": true},
  {"id": "pic-d6b67dfc852a", "topic": "chemistry", "outcome": "chem-o8", "q": "<figure class=\"q-fig\"><img class=\"zoom\" src=\"img/slides/d6b67dfc852a.webp\" alt=\"\" data-cap=\"Lock-and-key model: the substrate fits the enzyme&#x27;s specifically shaped active site to form an enzyme–substrate complex. The products are released and the enzyme is unchanged, ready to be used again.\" data-from=\"Energy Transfer and Rates of Reaction · slide 35\"></figure>In this diagram, what happens to the enzyme after the products are released?", "options": ["It is unchanged and can be used again", "It becomes part of the product", "It is broken down into amino acids", "It permanently changes shape"], "answer": 0, "explain": "Enzymes are biological catalysts, so they are not used up. The enzyme leaves the reaction unchanged.", "source": "Slide picture", "pic": true},
  {"id": "pic-a248638c49d5", "topic": "chemistry", "outcome": "chem-o10", "q": "<figure class=\"q-fig\"><img class=\"zoom\" src=\"img/slides/a248638c49d5.webp\" alt=\"\" data-cap=\"Using oxidation numbers: Mn goes from +7 in MnO&lt;sub&gt;4&lt;/sub&gt;&lt;sup&gt;−&lt;/sup&gt; to +4 in MnO&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt; (decrease = reduction), and I goes from −1 in I&lt;sup&gt;−&lt;/sup&gt; to 0 in I&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt; (increase = oxidation).\" data-from=\"REDOX (Oxidation numbers) · page 8\"></figure>In the reaction shown in this image, which species is oxidised?", "options": ["MnO<sub>4</sub><sup>−</sup>", "MnO<sub>2</sub>", "I<sub>2</sub>", "I<sup>−</sup>"], "answer": 3, "explain": "Iodine's oxidation number rises from −1 in I<sup>−</sup> to 0 in I<sub>2</sub>, which is oxidation. Mn falls from +7 to +4, so MnO<sub>4</sub><sup>−</sup> is reduced.", "source": "Slide picture", "pic": true},
  {"id": "pic-3b5ad463fba7", "topic": "chemistry", "outcome": "chem-o10", "q": "<figure class=\"q-fig\"><img class=\"zoom\" src=\"img/slides/3b5ad463fba7.webp\" alt=\"\" data-cap=\"Oxidation numbers of each element in common substances: elements are 0, O is usually −2, H is usually +1, and the numbers add up to the overall charge. Corrections: in NH&lt;sub&gt;3&lt;/sub&gt;, N is &lt;b&gt;−3&lt;/b&gt; and H is &lt;b&gt;+1&lt;/b&gt; (the slide shows +3 and −1). An oxidation number is the charge an atom would have if all its bonds were ionic.\" data-from=\"REDOX (Oxidation numbers) · page 2\"></figure>One compound in this image has incorrect oxidation numbers. Which one?", "options": ["H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub>", "KMnO<sub>4</sub>", "NH<sub>3</sub>", "Cr<sub>2</sub>O<sub>7</sub><sup>2−</sup>"], "answer": 2, "explain": "H is +1, so in NH<sub>3</sub> N must be −3 (−3 + 3 × (+1) = 0), not +3 and −1 as shown.", "source": "Slide picture", "pic": true},
  {"id": "pic-a07e870688d4", "topic": "chemistry", "outcome": "chem-o10b", "q": "<figure class=\"q-fig\"><img class=\"zoom\" src=\"img/slides/a07e870688d4.webp\" alt=\"\" data-cap=\"Copper–silver cell: Cu is more reactive, so it is the anode (oxidised to Cu&lt;sup&gt;2+&lt;/sup&gt;), and electrons flow through the wire to the silver cathode, where Ag&lt;sup&gt;+&lt;/sup&gt; is reduced to Ag. In the KNO&lt;sub&gt;3&lt;/sub&gt; salt bridge, NO&lt;sub&gt;3&lt;/sub&gt;&lt;sup&gt;−&lt;/sup&gt; moves toward the anode and K&lt;sup&gt;+&lt;/sup&gt; toward the cathode to keep each solution neutral.\" data-from=\"REDOX (Galvanic Cells) · page 6\"></figure>In this cell, which way do the NO<sub>3</sub><sup>−</sup> ions in the salt bridge move?", "options": ["Into the wire", "Toward the Ag (cathode) beaker", "Toward the Cu (anode) beaker", "They do not move"], "answer": 2, "explain": "Cu<sup>2+</sup> ions build up in the anode beaker, so negative NO<sub>3</sub><sup>−</sup> ions move there to balance the charge. Ions never travel through the wire.", "source": "Slide picture", "pic": true},
  {"id": "pic-d376b73a5da3", "topic": "chemistry", "outcome": "chem-o10b", "q": "<figure class=\"q-fig\"><img class=\"zoom\" src=\"img/slides/d376b73a5da3.webp\" alt=\"\" data-cap=\"Voltaic (galvanic) cell: zinc in ZnSO&lt;sub&gt;4&lt;/sub&gt; is the anode (oxidation half-cell) and copper in CuSO&lt;sub&gt;4&lt;/sub&gt; is the cathode (reduction half-cell). The salt bridge completes the circuit by letting ions move between the half-cells.\" data-from=\"REDOX (Galvanic Cells) · page 3\"></figure>In this cell, which way do electrons flow through the external circuit?", "options": ["From Cu to Zn", "From Zn to Cu", "Through the salt bridge from Zn to Cu", "They do not flow"], "answer": 1, "explain": "Zinc is more reactive, so it is the anode and is oxidised. The electrons it releases travel through the wire to the copper cathode.", "source": "Slide picture", "pic": true},
  {"id": "pic-6988e611a9ba", "topic": "chemistry", "outcome": "chem-o10b", "q": "<figure class=\"q-fig\"><img class=\"zoom\" src=\"img/slides/6988e611a9ba.webp\" alt=\"\" data-cap=\"Practice cell: a lead electrode in Pb&lt;sup&gt;2+&lt;/sup&gt; solution and a zinc electrode in Zn&lt;sup&gt;2+&lt;/sup&gt; solution, joined by a salt bridge and voltmeter. Zinc is more reactive, so it is the anode (−), lead is the cathode (+), and electrons flow from Zn to Pb.\" data-from=\"REDOX (Galvanic Cells) · page 15\"></figure>In this cell, which electrode is the anode and which way do electrons flow in the wire?", "options": ["Pb is the anode; electrons flow from Pb to Zn", "Pb is the anode; electrons flow from Zn to Pb", "Zn is the anode; electrons flow from Pb to Zn", "Zn is the anode; electrons flow from Zn to Pb"], "answer": 3, "explain": "Zinc is higher in the activity series than lead, so it is oxidised at the anode and its electrons flow through the wire to the lead cathode.", "source": "Slide picture", "pic": true},
  {"id": "pic-d8ad818db84c", "topic": "reactions", "outcome": "rxn-n1", "q": "<figure class=\"q-fig\"><img class=\"zoom\" src=\"img/slides/d8ad818db84c.webp\" alt=\"\" data-cap=\"Ionic compounds form by transfer of electrons: NaCl (1 Na : 1 Cl), MgCl&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt; (one Mg gives an electron to each of two Cl atoms) and Na&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt;O (two Na each give one electron to one O). The ratio balances the charges.\" data-from=\"Naming compounds · slide 5\"></figure>According to this diagram, why is the formula of magnesium chloride MgCl<sub>2</sub>?", "options": ["Magnesium shares two electrons with one chlorine atom", "Magnesium loses two electrons and each chlorine atom gains one", "Chlorine loses two electrons to magnesium", "Magnesium gains two electrons from two chlorine atoms"], "answer": 1, "explain": "Mg forms Mg<sup>2+</sup> by losing 2 electrons, and each Cl gains 1 to form Cl<sup>−</sup>, so two chloride ions balance one Mg<sup>2+</sup>.", "source": "Slide picture", "pic": true},
  {"id": "pic-e5601c6d758a", "topic": "reactions", "outcome": "rxn-n3", "q": "<figure class=\"q-fig\"><img class=\"zoom\" src=\"img/slides/e5601c6d758a.webp\" alt=\"\" data-cap=\"Particle model of H&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt; + Cl&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt; → 2HCl: one hydrogen molecule and one chlorine molecule make two hydrogen chloride molecules, so the number of each type of atom is the same on both sides.\" data-from=\"Chemical Reactions · slide 9\"></figure>Which balanced equation matches the particle model in this image?", "options": ["H + Cl → HCl", "H<sub>2</sub> + Cl<sub>2</sub> → HCl", "H<sub>2</sub> + Cl<sub>2</sub> → 2HCl", "2H<sub>2</sub> + Cl<sub>2</sub> → 2HCl"], "answer": 2, "explain": "The model shows one H<sub>2</sub> and one Cl<sub>2</sub> forming two HCl molecules: 2 H atoms and 2 Cl atoms on each side.", "source": "Slide picture", "pic": true},
  {"id": "pic-cd818df7fb77", "topic": "reactions", "outcome": "rxn-n4", "q": "<figure class=\"q-fig\"><img class=\"zoom\" src=\"img/slides/cd818df7fb77.webp\" alt=\"\" data-cap=\"pH ranges where common indicators change colour: methyl orange red→yellow (about 3–4.4), litmus red→blue (about 5–8), bromothymol blue yellow→blue (6–7.6), phenolphthalein colourless→pink (8.3–10). Universal indicator changes gradually from red through green to purple across the whole scale.\" data-from=\"Investigating indicators · slide 8\"></figure>Using this chart, what colour is bromothymol blue in a solution of pH 9?", "options": ["Yellow", "Green", "Colourless", "Blue"], "answer": 3, "explain": "Bromothymol blue is yellow below pH 6 and blue above about 7.6, so at pH 9 it is blue.", "source": "Slide picture", "pic": true},
  {"id": "pic-4d36bafcc17c", "topic": "reactions", "outcome": "rxn-n4", "q": "<figure class=\"q-fig\"><img class=\"zoom\" src=\"img/slides/4d36bafcc17c.webp\" alt=\"\" data-cap=\"Indicator table: each indicator has a colour below and above its pH range. Using several indicators together narrows down the pH of an unknown solution.\" data-from=\"Investigating indicators · slide 9\"></figure>A solution is yellow with methyl orange and colourless with phenolphthalein. Using this chart, what is its pH range?", "options": ["Below 3.1", "Between 3.1 and 4.4", "Between 4.4 and 8.3", "Above 10.0"], "answer": 2, "explain": "Methyl orange is yellow above 4.4, and phenolphthalein stays colourless below 8.3, so the pH must be between 4.4 and 8.3.", "source": "Slide picture", "pic": true},
  {"id": "pic-6087bfb831dc", "topic": "reactions", "outcome": "rxn-n4", "q": "<figure class=\"q-fig\"><img class=\"zoom\" src=\"img/slides/6087bfb831dc.webp\" alt=\"\" data-cap=\"Strength vs concentration: a strong acid (e.g. HCl) fully ionises into H&lt;sup&gt;+&lt;/sup&gt; and Cl&lt;sup&gt;−&lt;/sup&gt;, while a weak acid (e.g. CH&lt;sub&gt;3&lt;/sub&gt;COOH) only partly ionises, so most of it stays as molecules. Concentration is how much acid is dissolved in a given volume.\" data-from=\"Investigating indicators · slide 5\"></figure>Which beaker in this image contains the highest concentration of H<sup>+</sup> ions?", "options": ["Concentrated weak acid", "Concentrated strong acid", "Dilute strong acid", "Dilute weak acid"], "answer": 1, "explain": "A strong acid ionises completely, and a concentrated one has many acid particles per volume, so it has the most free H<sup>+</sup> ions.", "source": "Slide picture", "pic": true},
  {"id": "pic-6909bb64eba4", "topic": "reactions", "outcome": "rxn-n6", "q": "<figure class=\"q-fig\"><img class=\"zoom\" src=\"img/slides/6909bb64eba4.webp\" alt=\"\" data-cap=\"Thermal decomposition: heating green copper(II) carbonate gives black copper(II) oxide and carbon dioxide, CuCO&lt;sub&gt;3&lt;/sub&gt; → CuO + CO&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt;. The CO&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt; turns the limewater milky.\" data-from=\"Combustion, Decomposition, Biological Reactions · slide 20\"></figure>What colour change happens to the solid in this experiment?", "options": ["Blue to white", "Green to black", "Black to green", "White to yellow"], "answer": 1, "explain": "Green copper(II) carbonate decomposes on heating into black copper(II) oxide and carbon dioxide gas.", "source": "Slide picture", "pic": true},
  {"id": "pic-31436c93bcd4", "topic": "global", "outcome": "glo-o1", "q": "<figure class=\"q-fig\"><img class=\"zoom\" src=\"img/slides/31436c93bcd4.webp\" alt=\"\" data-cap=\"The lithosphere is the rigid outer shell of Earth, about 100 km thick, made of the crust plus the uppermost solid mantle; it sits on the softer asthenosphere, and its plates drive tectonics. The diagram&#x27;s &quot;Crust 0–100 km&quot; label really gives the depth of the whole lithosphere: the crust itself is only about 5–10 km thick under oceans and 30–70 km under continents.\" data-from=\"Earth&#x27;s Spheres · slide 5\"></figure>According to this diagram, what makes up the lithosphere?", "options": ["The outer and inner core", "The asthenosphere and the lower mantle", "The crust only", "The crust and the uppermost solid part of the mantle"], "answer": 3, "explain": "The bracket labels the lithosphere as the crust plus the uppermost solid mantle. The asthenosphere underneath it is softer and is not part of the lithosphere.", "source": "Slide picture", "pic": true},
  {"id": "pic-2e451c5ff665", "topic": "global", "outcome": "glo-o1", "q": "<figure class=\"q-fig\"><img class=\"zoom\" src=\"img/slides/2e451c5ff665.webp\" alt=\"\" data-cap=\"How the hydrosphere&#x27;s water is shared out: 97% is salt water in the oceans and only 3% is fresh. Most fresh water is locked in ice caps and glaciers (68.7%) or underground (30.1%), and only 0.3% is surface water in lakes, swamps and rivers.\" data-from=\"Earth&#x27;s Spheres · slide 6\"></figure>According to this diagram, where is most of Earth's fresh water stored?", "options": ["Lakes and rivers", "Ice caps and glaciers", "Groundwater", "Swamps"], "answer": 1, "explain": "68.7% of fresh water is frozen in ice caps and glaciers. Groundwater is 30.1% and all surface water is only 0.3%.", "source": "Slide picture", "pic": true},
  {"id": "pic-f980ca0dc6f9", "topic": "global", "outcome": "glo-o4", "q": "<figure class=\"q-fig\"><img class=\"zoom\" src=\"img/slides/f980ca0dc6f9.webp\" alt=\"\" data-cap=\"Layers of the atmosphere from the ground up: &lt;b&gt;troposphere&lt;/b&gt; (about 0–12 km, where weather happens), &lt;b&gt;stratosphere&lt;/b&gt; (to about 50 km, contains the ozone layer and gets warmer with height), &lt;b&gt;mesosphere&lt;/b&gt; (meteors burn up, the coldest layer), &lt;b&gt;thermosphere&lt;/b&gt; (auroras, very hot) and &lt;b&gt;exosphere&lt;/b&gt; (satellites). Remember the order with temperature: it falls, rises, falls, then rises again.\" data-from=\"Earth&#x27;s Spheres · slide 4\"></figure>In this diagram, meteors are shown burning up in which layer of the atmosphere?", "options": ["Troposphere", "Stratosphere", "Mesosphere", "Thermosphere"], "answer": 2, "explain": "Meteors burn up in the mesosphere (about 50–85 km up) because of friction with the air. Auroras happen higher up, in the thermosphere.", "source": "Slide picture", "pic": true},
  {"id": "pic-3651d482ef45", "topic": "global", "outcome": "glo-o3c", "q": "<figure class=\"q-fig\"><img class=\"zoom\" src=\"img/slides/3651d482ef45.webp\" alt=\"\" data-cap=\"The phosphorus cycle. Weathering and erosion release phosphate from rock, plants absorb it from soil and water, animals get it by eating plants, and decomposers return it to the soil. Runoff and leaching carry phosphate into water, where it forms sediment and new rock that geological uplift slowly brings back to the surface. There is no gas phase: phosphorus moves through the lithosphere, hydrosphere and biosphere, but not the atmosphere.\" data-from=\"Phosphorus Cycle · slide 7\"></figure>This phosphorus cycle diagram has no arrows going into or out of the air. What key feature of the phosphorus cycle does this show?", "options": ["Phosphorus has no significant gas phase, so it cycles through rock, soil, water and living things", "Phosphorus is found only in living things", "Phosphorus is fixed from the air by bacteria", "Phosphorus moves fastest through rocks"], "answer": 0, "explain": "Unlike carbon and nitrogen, phosphorus has no stable gas, so it is a sedimentary cycle. Its slowest part is the rock stage (weathering and uplift over thousands to millions of years).", "source": "Slide picture", "pic": true},
  {"id": "pic-d0f2749c83e4", "topic": "global", "outcome": "glo-o3c", "q": "<figure class=\"q-fig\"><img class=\"zoom\" src=\"img/slides/d0f2749c83e4.webp\" alt=\"\" data-cap=\"Eutrophication: fertiliser and animal waste wash nitrogen and phosphorus into water, causing an algal bloom. The algae block sunlight so water plants die, and bacteria decomposing the dead matter use up the dissolved oxygen, so fish suffocate and a &quot;dead zone&quot; forms.\" data-from=\"Phosphorus Cycle · slide 15\"></figure>According to this diagram, what directly causes the fish to die during eutrophication?", "options": ["Nitrogen and phosphorus are directly poisonous to fish", "Algae eat the fishes' food", "Too much sunlight heats the water", "Bacteria decomposing dead algae use up the dissolved oxygen"], "answer": 3, "explain": "The extra nutrients cause the bloom, but the fish die because decomposing bacteria use up the oxygen in the water. Low oxygen (anoxia) suffocates them.", "source": "Slide picture", "pic": true},
  {"id": "pic-4685dbdccd56", "topic": "ws", "outcome": "ws-n4", "q": "<figure class=\"q-fig\"><img class=\"zoom\" src=\"img/slides/4685dbdccd56.webp\" alt=\"\" data-cap=\"An indicator chart used in a Working Scientifically question to predict and record results. Methyl orange is red below about pH 3 and yellow above about 4.4. Bromothymol blue is yellow below 6, blue above 7.6 and green in between. Phenolphthalein is colourless below about 8.3 and pink above 10. In the gaps the indicator shows an in-between colour, so a neutral salt such as NaCl (pH about 7) gives yellow, green and colourless.\" data-from=\"Working Scientifically Questions and Solutions · page 2\"></figure>Using this chart, a solution turns methyl orange yellow, bromothymol blue yellow and phenolphthalein colourless. What is its pH range?", "options": ["Below 3", "About 4.5 to 6", "About 7.5 to 8.3", "Above 10"], "answer": 1, "explain": "Methyl orange is yellow only above about 4.5, bromothymol blue is yellow only below 6, and phenolphthalein is colourless below about 8.3. The only range that fits all three is about pH 4.5–6.", "source": "Slide picture", "pic": true},
  {"id": "pic-8fd27d416a2e", "topic": "motion", "outcome": "mot-n3", "q": "<figure class=\"q-fig\"><img class=\"zoom\" src=\"img/slides/8fd27d416a2e.webp\" alt=\"\" data-cap=\"The section of tape measured here is 0.081 m long and spans 5 intervals (0.10 s), so the average velocity = 0.081 ÷ 0.10 = 0.81 m/s. The gaps get wider along the tape, so the trolley was accelerating.\" data-from=\"6. Ticker Timers · slide 5\"></figure>This tape came from a 50 Hz ticker timer, and the measured section contains 5 dot-to-dot intervals. What is the average velocity over this section?", "options": ["0.081 m/s", "0.41 m/s", "0.81 m/s", "4.05 m/s"], "answer": 2, "explain": "5 intervals × 0.02 s = 0.10 s, and v = d ÷ t = 0.081 m ÷ 0.10 s = 0.81 m/s.", "source": "Slide picture", "pic": true},
  {"id": "pic-de99e20f02d5", "topic": "motion", "outcome": "mot-n3", "q": "<figure class=\"q-fig\"><img class=\"zoom\" src=\"img/slides/de99e20f02d5.webp\" alt=\"\" data-cap=\"A velocity–time graph can go below the time axis, which means the object is moving in the negative direction. Here it speeds up to 8 m/s, holds that speed from 3 to 5 s, then slows down, stops and reverses at 7 s, reaching −8 m/s at 9 s.\" data-from=\"7. Motion graphs · slide 22\"></figure>According to this velocity–time graph, at what time does the object change direction?", "options": ["7 s", "5 s", "9 s", "3 s"], "answer": 0, "explain": "Direction changes when velocity changes sign, which is where the line crosses the time axis at 7 s. At 5 s the object only starts to slow down.", "source": "Slide picture", "pic": true},
  {"id": "pic-62f3ab793e1f", "topic": "motion", "outcome": "mot-n4", "q": "<figure class=\"q-fig\"><img class=\"zoom\" src=\"img/slides/62f3ab793e1f.webp\" alt=\"\" data-cap=\"Free-body diagram of a box pulled along a table. The forces are the applied force F&lt;sub&gt;a&lt;/sub&gt;, friction F&lt;sub&gt;f&lt;/sub&gt; opposing the motion, weight mg acting down and the normal force F&lt;sub&gt;N&lt;/sub&gt; acting up. If the box moves at constant speed, the forces are balanced (net force = 0).\" data-from=\"8-11. Newton&#x27;s Laws · slide 18\"></figure>This 10 kg box is pulled to the right at a constant speed by an applied force F<sub>a</sub> of 50 N. What is the friction force F<sub>f</sub>?", "options": ["0 N", "50 N to the left", "50 N to the right", "98 N to the left"], "answer": 1, "explain": "At constant speed the acceleration is zero, so the net force is zero (Newton's first law). Friction must therefore balance the 50 N pull and act in the opposite direction.", "source": "Slide picture", "pic": true},
  {"id": "pic-28f0709f2028", "topic": "motion", "outcome": "mot-n4", "q": "<figure class=\"q-fig\"><img class=\"zoom\" src=\"img/slides/28f0709f2028.webp\" alt=\"\" data-cap=\"Finding the net force: add forces that point the same way and subtract forces that point opposite ways. In part 1, 5 N + 20 N = 25 N to the right. In part 2, 7 N − 2 N = 5 N to the left.\" data-from=\"8-11. Newton&#x27;s Laws · slide 9\"></figure>What is the net force on the box in part 1 of this diagram?", "options": ["15 N right", "15 N left", "5 N left", "25 N right"], "answer": 3, "explain": "Both the 5 N and the 20 N forces point to the right, so they add to 25 N to the right. You would subtract only if they pointed in opposite directions.", "source": "Slide picture", "pic": true},
  {"id": "pic-86874f8487bd", "topic": "motion", "outcome": "mot-n4", "q": "<figure class=\"q-fig\"><img class=\"zoom\" src=\"img/slides/86874f8487bd.webp\" alt=\"\" data-cap=\"A book resting on a table has its weight (Earth pulling it down) balanced by the normal force (the table pushing it up, at right angles to the surface). These are balanced forces on one object, &lt;i&gt;not&lt;/i&gt; a third-law pair: the pair of the weight is the book pulling Earth up.\" data-from=\"8-11. Newton&#x27;s Laws · slide 16\"></figure>The weight and the normal force on this book are equal and opposite. Why are they NOT a Newton's third law action–reaction pair?", "options": ["They are not equal in size", "They act in the same direction", "They both act on the same object (the book)", "The normal force exists only when the book is moving"], "answer": 2, "explain": "The two forces in a third-law pair always act on different objects. Both of these act on the book, so they are just balanced forces (Newton's first law).", "source": "Slide picture", "pic": true},
  {"id": "pic-77e1ac2b8d44", "topic": "ws", "outcome": "ws-n3", "q": "<figure class=\"q-fig\"><img class=\"zoom\" src=\"img/slides/77e1ac2b8d44.webp\" alt=\"\" data-cap=\"A &lt;b&gt;risk assessment matrix&lt;/b&gt;: the &lt;b&gt;likelihood&lt;/b&gt; of a hazard (frequent → improbable) is combined with its &lt;b&gt;severity&lt;/b&gt; (catastrophic → negligible) to give an overall risk rating of high, serious, medium or low. In a risk assessment, list 2–3 hazards, rate each one, and give a specific control measure for it, not just &#x27;wear PPE&#x27;.\" data-from=\"Park&#x27;s Party Official Science Notes (3) · page 4\"></figure>According to this matrix, what is the risk rating for a hazard that is <i>occasional</i> (C) and <i>critical</i> (2)?", "options": ["High", "Serious", "Medium", "Low"], "answer": 1, "explain": "Read across from Occasional (C) and down from Critical (2): the cell says Serious. 'High' is the rating for Occasional + Catastrophic, one column to the left.", "source": "Slide picture", "pic": true},
  {"id": "pic-31eff7068a8a", "topic": "ws", "outcome": "ws-n4", "q": "<figure class=\"q-fig\"><img class=\"zoom\" src=\"img/slides/31eff7068a8a.webp\" alt=\"\" data-cap=\"&lt;b&gt;Percentage uncertainty&lt;/b&gt; = (absolute uncertainty ÷ measured value) × 100%. The &lt;b&gt;absolute uncertainty&lt;/b&gt; is the ± margin (often half the smallest scale division for one reading), so a reading of 150.5 ± 0.1 cm has a percentage uncertainty of about 0.07%.\" data-from=\"Park&#x27;s Party Official Science Notes (3) · page 5\"></figure>Using this formula, what is the percentage uncertainty of a length measured as 20.0 ± 0.5 cm?", "options": ["40%", "0.025%", "4%", "2.5%"], "answer": 3, "explain": "0.5 ÷ 20.0 = 0.025, and 0.025 × 100% = 2.5%. 0.025% comes from forgetting to multiply by 100, and 40% comes from dividing the wrong way (20 ÷ 0.5).", "source": "Slide picture", "pic": true},
  {"id": "pic-de915fd864a3", "topic": "ws", "outcome": "ws-n6", "q": "<figure class=\"q-fig\"><img class=\"zoom\" src=\"img/slides/de915fd864a3.webp\" alt=\"\" data-cap=\"Words for describing graph trends: a straight line increases or decreases at a &lt;b&gt;steady rate&lt;/b&gt;; a curve that gets steeper changes at an &lt;b&gt;increasing rate&lt;/b&gt;; a curve that flattens out changes at a &lt;b&gt;decreasing rate&lt;/b&gt;. &lt;b&gt;Error in the notes picture:&lt;/b&gt; the two bottom-left and bottom-right labels are swapped. The bottom-left curve (steep, then flattening) is decreasing at a &lt;i&gt;decreasing&lt;/i&gt; rate, and the bottom-right curve (flat, then plunging) is decreasing at an &lt;i&gt;increasing&lt;/i&gt; rate.\" data-from=\"Park&#x27;s Party Official Science Notes (3) · page 6\"></figure>A graph rises steeply at first and then gets flatter (top-right in the picture). How is this trend described?", "options": ["Increasing at a steady rate", "Increasing at an increasing rate", "Increasing at a decreasing rate", "Decreasing at a decreasing rate"], "answer": 2, "explain": "The values keep going up, so it is increasing, but the gradient keeps getting smaller, so the rate is decreasing. 'Increasing at an increasing rate' would mean the curve gets steeper, like the bottom-middle graph.", "source": "Slide picture", "pic": true},
  {"id": "pic-33eb7399536e", "topic": "ws", "outcome": "ws-n8", "q": "<figure class=\"q-fig\"><img class=\"zoom\" src=\"img/slides/33eb7399536e.webp\" alt=\"\" data-cap=\"Dartboard picture of errors. &lt;b&gt;Random error&lt;/b&gt; scatters results unpredictably around the true value (low precision), and you reduce it by repeating and averaging. &lt;b&gt;Systematic error&lt;/b&gt; shifts every result the same way, for example a scale that is not zeroed, so results can be precise but not accurate. Repeating does not fix a systematic error; you have to fix or recalibrate the equipment.\" data-from=\"2026 SCIENCE STANDARD · page 16\"></figure>In the right-hand target, the darts are tightly grouped but away from the bullseye. What does this show?", "options": ["Random error: accurate but not precise", "No error: accurate and precise", "Systematic error: precise but not accurate", "Random error: neither accurate nor precise"], "answer": 2, "explain": "Close grouping means high precision, but all the darts are off-centre in the same direction, which is a systematic error, so the results are not accurate. Random error would spread the darts out, as in the middle target.", "source": "Slide picture", "pic": true},
  {"id": "pic-819ca691063c", "topic": "motion", "outcome": "mot-n3", "q": "<figure class=\"q-fig\"><img class=\"zoom\" src=\"img/slides/819ca691063c.webp\" alt=\"\" data-cap=\"Graph shapes for three kinds of motion (columns: constant acceleration, constant velocity, at rest). Position–time: parabola, diagonal line, flat line. Velocity–time: diagonal, flat, zero. Acceleration–time: flat (constant non-zero value), zero, zero.\" data-from=\"Park&#x27;s Party Official Science Notes (3) · page 15\"></figure>According to this chart, what does a velocity–time graph look like for an object moving at constant velocity?", "options": ["A flat horizontal line above zero", "A diagonal straight line", "A parabola", "A line along the time axis (zero)"], "answer": 0, "explain": "Constant velocity means the velocity value does not change, so the v–t line is flat at that value. A diagonal v–t line means constant acceleration, and a line on the time axis means the object is at rest.", "source": "Slide picture", "pic": true},
  {"id": "pic-8a12fe15b78f", "topic": "motion", "outcome": "mot-n4", "q": "<figure class=\"q-fig\"><img class=\"zoom\" src=\"img/slides/8a12fe15b78f.webp\" alt=\"\" data-cap=\"Finding the &lt;b&gt;net force&lt;/b&gt;: add forces acting in the same direction and subtract opposing ones. Here 100 N + 100 N forwards − 50 N drag = 150 N forwards, so the car accelerates forwards (a = F&lt;sub&gt;net&lt;/sub&gt;/m).\" data-from=\"Park&#x27;s Party Official Science Notes (3) · page 17\"></figure>What is the net force on this car?", "options": ["50 N backwards", "250 N forwards", "150 N forwards", "100 N forwards"], "answer": 2, "explain": "The two thrusts add up to 200 N forwards and drag is 50 N backwards, so the net force is 200 − 50 = 150 N forwards. 250 N comes from wrongly adding the drag instead of subtracting it.", "source": "Slide picture", "pic": true},
  {"id": "pic-7efeecad2e70", "topic": "chemistry", "outcome": "chem-pre", "q": "<figure class=\"q-fig\"><img class=\"zoom\" src=\"img/slides/7efeecad2e70.webp\" alt=\"\" data-cap=\"The four acid reaction patterns to learn: acid + metal → salt + hydrogen; acid + base (metal oxide) → salt + water; acid + hydroxide → salt + water; acid + carbonate → salt + carbon dioxide + water. The salt is named from the metal plus the acid (hydrochloric → chloride, sulfuric → sulfate, nitric → nitrate).\" data-from=\"2026 SCIENCE STANDARD · page 39\"></figure>Using these patterns, what are the products when hydrochloric acid reacts with calcium carbonate?", "options": ["Calcium chloride and hydrogen", "Calcium hydroxide and carbon dioxide", "Calcium chloride and water only", "Calcium chloride, carbon dioxide and water"], "answer": 3, "explain": "Acid + carbonate → salt + carbon dioxide + water, and hydrochloric acid makes chloride salts. Hydrogen is only produced when an acid reacts with a metal.", "source": "Slide picture", "pic": true},
  {"id": "pic-6d45c812e941", "topic": "chemistry", "outcome": "chem-o2", "q": "<figure class=\"q-fig\"><img class=\"zoom\" src=\"img/slides/6d45c812e941.webp\" alt=\"\" data-cap=\"The &lt;b&gt;system&lt;/b&gt; is the reacting chemicals and the &lt;b&gt;surroundings&lt;/b&gt; are everything else. &lt;b&gt;Endothermic&lt;/b&gt; (ΔH &amp;gt; 0): heat flows into the system, so the surroundings get cooler. &lt;b&gt;Exothermic&lt;/b&gt; (ΔH &amp;lt; 0): heat flows out, so the surroundings get warmer, because more energy is released when the new bonds form than was absorbed breaking the old ones.\" data-from=\"2026 SCIENCE STANDARD · page 85\"></figure>According to this diagram, which statement describes an endothermic reaction?", "options": ["Heat flows from the surroundings into the system, the surroundings get cooler and ΔH &gt; 0", "Heat flows out of the system, the surroundings get warmer and ΔH &lt; 0", "Heat flows into the system, the surroundings get warmer and ΔH &lt; 0", "No heat is transferred, and ΔH = 0"], "answer": 0, "explain": "In diagram A the arrows point into the system, so energy is absorbed from the surroundings, which cool down, and ΔH is positive. Option B describes an exothermic reaction (diagram B).", "source": "Slide picture", "pic": true},
  {"id": "pic-9468a39889f3", "topic": "chemistry", "outcome": "chem-o7", "q": "<figure class=\"q-fig\"><img class=\"zoom\" src=\"img/slides/9468a39889f3.webp\" alt=\"\" data-cap=\"A catalyst provides an &lt;b&gt;alternative pathway&lt;/b&gt; (pink, here with several small steps) whose highest barrier, the activation energy, is lower than without a catalyst (black). The reactants (X, Y) and product (Z) energies are the same on both paths, so the overall energy change is unchanged. Note: the picture labels this ΔG; in this course call it &lt;b&gt;ΔH&lt;/b&gt;, the enthalpy change.\" data-from=\"Park&#x27;s Party Official Science Notes (3) · page 43\"></figure>According to the diagram, what does the catalyst NOT change?", "options": ["The activation energy", "The energy difference between reactants and products (ΔH)", "The pathway the reaction takes", "The rate of the reaction"], "answer": 1, "explain": "Both curves start at the same reactant energy and end at the same product energy, so ΔH is unchanged. The catalyst does lower the activation energy, which is exactly why the reaction goes faster.", "source": "Slide picture", "pic": true},
  {"id": "pic-9929807f9d4c", "topic": "chemistry", "outcome": "chem-o9", "q": "<figure class=\"q-fig\"><img class=\"zoom\" src=\"img/slides/9929807f9d4c.webp\" alt=\"\" data-cap=\"The &lt;b&gt;reactivity (activity) series&lt;/b&gt; from most reactive (K, Na, Ca, Mg, Al) to least reactive (Cu, Hg, Ag, Au, Pt). A metal displaces any metal &lt;i&gt;below&lt;/i&gt; it from a solution of its ions, and metals above hydrogen react with dilute acids to release H&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt;.\" data-from=\"Park&#x27;s Party Official Science Notes (3) · page 44\"></figure>Using this series, which mixture would produce a displacement reaction?", "options": ["Copper metal in magnesium sulfate solution", "Silver metal in zinc nitrate solution", "Zinc metal in copper(II) sulfate solution", "Gold metal in iron(II) chloride solution"], "answer": 2, "explain": "Zinc is above copper in the series, so it displaces copper: Zn + CuSO<sub>4</sub> → ZnSO<sub>4</sub> + Cu. In the other options the solid metal is below the metal in solution, so nothing happens.", "source": "Slide picture", "pic": true},
  {"id": "pic-b97e49c278e7", "topic": "chemistry", "outcome": "chem-o10b", "q": "<figure class=\"q-fig\"><img class=\"zoom\" src=\"img/slides/b97e49c278e7.webp\" alt=\"\" data-cap=\"Galvanic cell schematic. At the &lt;b&gt;anode&lt;/b&gt; the more reactive metal is oxidised and releases electrons, which flow through the external wire to the &lt;b&gt;cathode&lt;/b&gt;, where reduction absorbs them. In the salt bridge, positive ions migrate towards the cathode and negative ions towards the anode to keep both solutions electrically neutral.\" data-from=\"2026 SCIENCE STANDARD · page 99\"></figure>According to this diagram, how do electrons move in a galvanic cell?", "options": ["Through the salt bridge from cathode to anode", "Through the salt bridge from anode to cathode", "Through the external wire from cathode to anode", "Through the external wire from anode to cathode"], "answer": 3, "explain": "Electrons are released at the anode (oxidation) and travel through the metal wire to the cathode (reduction). Only ions move through the salt bridge, never electrons.", "source": "Slide picture", "pic": true},
  {"id": "pic-8d2927f178b5", "topic": "universe", "outcome": "uni-o9", "q": "<figure class=\"q-fig\"><img class=\"zoom\" src=\"img/slides/8d2927f178b5.webp\" alt=\"\" data-cap=\"A star&#x27;s &lt;b&gt;colour shows its surface temperature&lt;/b&gt;: red about 3 500 K (coolest), yellow about 5 000 K, white about 7 000 K and blue about 25 000 K (hottest). The headlight comparison is about brightness: like headlights, stars look dimmer the further away they are, which is why apparent brightness depends on distance as well as luminosity.\" data-from=\"2026 SCIENCE STANDARD · page 105\"></figure>According to this picture, which star colour has the highest surface temperature?", "options": ["Blue", "Yellow", "Red", "White"], "answer": 0, "explain": "Blue stars are shown at about 25 000 K, the hottest. It is tempting to think red means 'hot', but red stars are the coolest, at about 3 500 K.", "source": "Slide picture", "pic": true},
  {"id": "pic-3889800e3935", "topic": "universe", "outcome": "uni-o11", "q": "<figure class=\"q-fig\"><img class=\"zoom\" src=\"img/slides/3889800e3935.webp\" alt=\"\" data-cap=\"H–R diagram labelled with star masses and main-sequence lifetimes. Going up the main sequence, stars are &lt;b&gt;more massive, hotter, bluer, bigger and more luminous&lt;/b&gt;, but they use up their fuel much faster: about 10&lt;sup&gt;7&lt;/sup&gt; years for a 10-solar-mass star, 10&lt;sup&gt;10&lt;/sup&gt; years for the Sun and 10&lt;sup&gt;11&lt;/sup&gt; years for small red dwarfs.\" data-from=\"2026 SCIENCE STANDARD · page 113\"></figure>According to this H–R diagram, roughly how long does a star like the Sun spend on the main sequence?", "options": ["10<sup>10</sup> years", "10<sup>7</sup> years", "10<sup>8</sup> years", "10<sup>11</sup> years"], "answer": 0, "explain": "The lifetime label near the Sun's position reads 10<sup>10</sup> years (about 10 billion). 10<sup>7</sup> years belongs to the far more massive, hotter stars near the top-left, which burn out much faster.", "source": "Slide picture", "pic": true},
  {"id": "pic-1b3e840febdd", "topic": "universe", "outcome": "uni-o11", "q": "<figure class=\"q-fig\"><img class=\"zoom\" src=\"img/slides/1b3e840febdd.webp\" alt=\"\" data-cap=\"H–R diagram with named stars: luminosity (Sun = 1) and absolute magnitude on the vertical axes, and spectral type/temperature on the horizontal axis (hot on the left). The main sequence runs from Rigel-like hot stars down past the Sun to red dwarfs such as Proxima. Giants such as Betelgeuse and Aldebaran are top right (cool but very bright because they are huge), and white dwarfs such as Sirius B are bottom left (hot but dim because they are tiny).\" data-from=\"2026 SCIENCE STANDARD · page 114\"></figure>On this diagram, Sirius B sits at the bottom left. What kind of star is it?", "options": ["A red supergiant: cool and very bright", "A white dwarf: hot but very dim", "A main-sequence star like the Sun", "A red dwarf: cool and dim"], "answer": 1, "explain": "Bottom left means a high temperature but low luminosity, which is only possible for a very small star, so Sirius B is a white dwarf. Red dwarfs such as Proxima are dim too, but they are cool and sit at the bottom right.", "source": "Slide picture", "pic": true},
  {"id": "pic-2e38f67417c8", "topic": "universe", "outcome": "uni-o13", "q": "<figure class=\"q-fig\"><img class=\"zoom\" src=\"img/slides/2e38f67417c8.webp\" alt=\"\" data-cap=\"&lt;b&gt;Hubble&#x27;s &#x27;tuning fork&#x27; classification&lt;/b&gt;. Ellipticals go from E0 (round) to E7 (very elongated); lenticulars (S0) sit at the fork; normal spirals (Sa, Sb, Sc) and barred spirals (SBa, SBb, SBc) form the two prongs, with arms getting looser and bulges smaller from a to c; and irregulars (Irr) have no regular shape.\" data-from=\"2026 SCIENCE STANDARD · page 115\"></figure>In this classification scheme, a galaxy labelled SBb is…", "options": ["an elliptical galaxy of medium elongation", "a lenticular galaxy", "an irregular galaxy", "a barred spiral with medium-sized arms and bulge"], "answer": 3, "explain": "'SB' means barred spiral, and 'b' is the middle of the a–c range of arm tightness and bulge size. Ellipticals are labelled with E and a number (E0–E7).", "source": "Slide picture", "pic": true},
  {"id": "pic-dbad0868581f", "topic": "universe", "outcome": "uni-o16", "q": "<figure class=\"q-fig\"><img class=\"zoom\" src=\"img/slides/dbad0868581f.webp\" alt=\"\" data-cap=\"&lt;b&gt;Stellar parallax&lt;/b&gt;: a nearby star is observed from opposite sides of Earth&#x27;s orbit (January and July, a baseline of 2 AU), so it appears to shift against the distant background stars. The parallax angle p is half the total shift, and the distance in parsecs is &lt;b&gt;d = 1/p&lt;/b&gt; (p in arcseconds). Closer stars show a bigger shift.\" data-from=\"2026 SCIENCE STANDARD · page 122\"></figure>A star's parallax angle p is 0.5 arcseconds. Using d = 1/p, how far away is it?", "options": ["0.5 parsecs", "1.5 parsecs", "2 parsecs", "5 parsecs"], "answer": 2, "explain": "d = 1/p = 1/0.5 = 2 parsecs. Answering 0.5 pc confuses the angle with the distance: a smaller parallax angle means the star is further away.", "source": "Slide picture", "pic": true},
  {"id": "pic-8d23c66db3f9", "topic": "universe", "outcome": "uni-o19", "q": "<figure class=\"q-fig\"><img class=\"zoom\" src=\"img/slides/8d23c66db3f9.webp\" alt=\"\" data-cap=\"The same absorption lines (Hγ, Hβ, Hα) for a source at rest (top), a source moving away (middle: &lt;b&gt;red-shifted&lt;/b&gt; to longer wavelengths) and a source moving towards us (bottom: &lt;b&gt;blue-shifted&lt;/b&gt;). Hubble found that almost all galaxies are red-shifted, which is key evidence that the universe is expanding.\" data-from=\"2026 SCIENCE STANDARD · page 128\"></figure>In the middle spectrum, the lines have moved towards the red end. What does this tell us about the source?", "options": ["It is moving away from us", "It is moving towards us", "It is hotter than the source at rest", "It is made of different elements"], "answer": 0, "explain": "A shift to longer (redder) wavelengths means the source is receding. Moving towards us would give a blue shift (bottom spectrum). The pattern of lines is the same, so the elements have not changed.", "source": "Slide picture", "pic": true},
  {"id": "pic-5e4751371a8a", "topic": "universe", "outcome": "uni-o19", "q": "<figure class=\"q-fig\"><img class=\"zoom\" src=\"img/slides/5e4751371a8a.webp\" alt=\"\" data-cap=\"An &lt;b&gt;emission spectrum&lt;/b&gt; is bright coloured lines on a dark background, given out by a hot, low-density gas. An &lt;b&gt;absorption spectrum&lt;/b&gt; is a continuous rainbow with dark lines, produced when light from a hot, dense source passes through a cooler gas. Each element absorbs and emits at the same wavelengths, so the lines act as a &#x27;fingerprint&#x27; for elements in stars.\" data-from=\"Park&#x27;s Party Official Science Notes (3) · page 58\"></figure>According to the diagram, how is an absorption spectrum produced?", "options": ["A hot gas emits light directly onto the prism", "Light from a hot, dense source passes through a cooler gas", "A cold gas emits light at all wavelengths", "Light is reflected off a planet's surface"], "answer": 1, "explain": "The cool gas absorbs particular wavelengths from the continuous spectrum of the hot source, leaving dark lines. A hot gas shining on its own gives an emission spectrum of bright lines instead.", "source": "Slide picture", "pic": true},
  {"id": "pic-53fd097814cb", "topic": "evolution", "outcome": "evo-o3b", "q": "<figure class=\"q-fig\"><img class=\"zoom\" src=\"img/slides/53fd097814cb.webp\" alt=\"\" data-cap=\"A &lt;b&gt;phylogenetic tree&lt;/b&gt; for three &lt;i&gt;Drosophila&lt;/i&gt; (fruit fly) species. Each yellow node is a &lt;b&gt;speciation event&lt;/b&gt;, where one ancestral population split into two separately evolving species. Species joined by a more recent node share a more recent common ancestor and are more closely related.\" data-from=\"2026 SCIENCE STANDARD · page 141\"></figure>According to this tree, which two species share the most recent common ancestor?", "options": ["<i>D. dentissima</i> and <i>D. obscura</i>", "<i>D. melanogaster</i> and <i>D. obscura</i>", "All three are equally related", "<i>D. dentissima</i> and <i>D. melanogaster</i>"], "answer": 3, "explain": "<i>D. dentissima</i> and <i>D. melanogaster</i> join at the upper (more recent) node. <i>D. obscura</i> branched off earlier at the lower node, so it is more distantly related to both.", "source": "Slide picture", "pic": true},
  {"id": "pic-8cd8c9c05e1c", "topic": "evolution", "outcome": "evo-o4a", "q": "<figure class=\"q-fig\"><img class=\"zoom\" src=\"img/slides/8cd8c9c05e1c.webp\" alt=\"\" data-cap=\"&lt;b&gt;Homologous structures&lt;/b&gt; (rat leg and bat wing) have the same basic bone structure but different functions because they come from a common ancestor (divergent evolution). &lt;b&gt;Analogous structures&lt;/b&gt; (bat wing and bee wing) do the same job but have completely different structures because they evolved separately (convergent evolution).\" data-from=\"2026 SCIENCE STANDARD · page 146\"></figure>According to this picture, the bat wing and the bee wing are…", "options": ["homologous structures, from divergent evolution", "vestigial structures", "analogous structures, from convergent evolution", "evidence that bats and bees share a recent common ancestor"], "answer": 2, "explain": "They have the same function (flight) but different structures (bone and skin vs chitin), so they are analogous and evolved independently. The bat wing is homologous with the rat leg, not with the bee wing.", "source": "Slide picture", "pic": true},
  {"id": "pic-fe51a63fb5da", "topic": "evolution", "outcome": "evo-o4b", "q": "<figure class=\"q-fig\"><img class=\"zoom\" src=\"img/slides/fe51a63fb5da.webp\" alt=\"\" data-cap=\"&lt;b&gt;Biochemical evidence&lt;/b&gt;: the same 22 amino acids of the haemoglobin beta chain compared across species. The number of differences from humans increases from monkey (1) to dog (3), bird (5) and fish (12). Fewer differences means a more recent common ancestor and a closer evolutionary relationship.\" data-from=\"2026 SCIENCE STANDARD · page 151\"></figure>According to this comparison, which species is most closely related to humans?", "options": ["Fish", "Monkey", "Dog", "Bird"], "answer": 1, "explain": "The monkey differs from humans at only 1 of the 22 positions, the fewest of any species shown. The fish has the most differences (12), so it is the most distant relative.", "source": "Slide picture", "pic": true},
  {"id": "pic-368327508740", "topic": "evolution", "outcome": "evo-o6", "q": "<figure class=\"q-fig\"><img class=\"zoom\" src=\"img/slides/368327508740.webp\" alt=\"\" data-cap=\"Using &lt;b&gt;index fossils&lt;/b&gt; to date unknown fossils. A, C and E are index fossils with known age ranges. Fossil B is found in the same layer as A and C, so it must date from when the age ranges of A and C overlap; fossil D is dated the same way using C and E.\" data-from=\"2026 SCIENCE STANDARD · page 156\"></figure>According to this diagram, how is the age of fossil B estimated?", "options": ["From the time when the age ranges of index fossils A and C overlap", "From the age range of fossil E alone", "By carbon dating fossil B directly", "By assuming it is the oldest fossil in the column"], "answer": 0, "explain": "B is found in the same rock layer as A and C, so it lived when both of them did, which is where their ranges overlap. E is found with D, not B, so it says nothing about B's age.", "source": "Slide picture", "pic": true},
  {"id": "pic-7a79fb0b9bca", "topic": "global", "outcome": "glo-o3a", "q": "<figure class=\"q-fig\"><img class=\"zoom\" src=\"img/slides/7a79fb0b9bca.webp\" alt=\"\" data-cap=\"The &lt;b&gt;carbon cycle&lt;/b&gt;. &lt;b&gt;Photosynthesis&lt;/b&gt; and &lt;b&gt;ocean uptake&lt;/b&gt; remove CO&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt; from the atmosphere, while plant, animal and root respiration, decay by decomposers, and the burning of fossil fuels (factory and car emissions) return it. Carbon stored in dead organisms can become fossil fuels over millions of years, and burning them quickly releases this long-stored carbon, which is the main cause of the enhanced greenhouse effect.\" data-from=\"Park&#x27;s Party Official Science Notes (3) · page 68\"></figure>According to this diagram, which process removes carbon dioxide from the atmosphere?", "options": ["Root respiration", "Factory emissions", "Photosynthesis", "Decay by decomposers"], "answer": 2, "explain": "Plants take in CO<sub>2</sub> during photosynthesis to make glucose (organic carbon). Respiration, decay and emissions all release CO<sub>2</sub> back into the atmosphere.", "source": "Slide picture", "pic": true},
  {"id": "pic-9d702a334df9", "topic": "global", "outcome": "glo-o4", "q": "<figure class=\"q-fig\"><img class=\"zoom\" src=\"img/slides/9d702a334df9.webp\" alt=\"\" data-cap=\"Temperature profile of the atmosphere. Temperature falls through the &lt;b&gt;troposphere&lt;/b&gt; (to about −55 °C at the tropopause, about 12 km), rises through the &lt;b&gt;stratosphere&lt;/b&gt; (ozone absorbs UV, about 0 °C at about 50 km), falls again through the &lt;b&gt;mesosphere&lt;/b&gt; (about −90 °C at about 85 km, the coldest part), then rises sharply in the &lt;b&gt;thermosphere&lt;/b&gt;. The boundaries (&#x27;-pauses&#x27;) are where the trend changes direction.\" data-from=\"2026 SCIENCE STANDARD · page 167\"></figure>According to this graph, where is the coldest part of the atmosphere?", "options": ["At the ground", "At the tropopause", "At the stratopause", "At the top of the mesosphere (mesopause)"], "answer": 3, "explain": "The line reaches its lowest temperature, about −90 °C, at the mesopause, about 85–90 km up. The tropopause is cold too, but only about −55 °C.", "source": "Slide picture", "pic": true},
  {"id": "pic-652567ba9606", "topic": "global", "outcome": "glo-o5", "q": "<figure class=\"q-fig\"><img class=\"zoom\" src=\"img/slides/652567ba9606.webp\" alt=\"\" data-cap=\"Ozone concentration against altitude. Most ozone forms the &lt;b&gt;ozone layer&lt;/b&gt; in the stratosphere (peaking around 20–30 km), where it absorbs harmful UV-B radiation (&#x27;good ozone&#x27;). Ozone near the ground in the troposphere is an air pollutant that harms breathing (&#x27;bad ozone&#x27;).\" data-from=\"2026 SCIENCE STANDARD · page 168\"></figure>According to this graph, at roughly what altitude is ozone concentration highest?", "options": ["About 20–25 km, in the stratosphere", "About 5 km, in the troposphere", "At ground level", "Above 35 km"], "answer": 0, "explain": "The red line bulges furthest to the right (highest concentration) at about 20–25 km, which is the stratospheric ozone layer. Ozone near the ground exists, but at much lower concentrations.", "source": "Slide picture", "pic": true},
  {"id": "pic-ac76f059979d", "topic": "genetics", "outcome": "gen-o6", "q": "<figure class=\"q-fig\"><img class=\"zoom\" src=\"img/slides/ac76f059979d.webp\" alt=\"\" data-cap=\"Mendel&#x27;s cross of true-breeding violet- and white-flowered pea plants. All F&lt;sub&gt;1&lt;/sub&gt; plants were violet, so violet is &lt;b&gt;dominant&lt;/b&gt;. When the F&lt;sub&gt;1&lt;/sub&gt; plants self-fertilised, the white trait reappeared in the F&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt;: 705 violet to 224 white, about &lt;b&gt;3 : 1&lt;/b&gt;. This showed that the white &#x27;factor&#x27; (allele) was hidden in the F&lt;sub&gt;1&lt;/sub&gt;, not lost.\" data-from=\"2026 SCIENCE STANDARD · page 67\"></figure>Mendel got 705 violet and 224 white F<sub>2</sub> plants. What ratio is this closest to?", "options": ["1 : 1", "1 : 2 : 1", "3 : 1", "9 : 3 : 3 : 1"], "answer": 2, "explain": "705 ÷ 224 ≈ 3.1, so the ratio is about 3 : 1, as expected from Pp × Pp. 1 : 2 : 1 is the <i>genotype</i> ratio (PP : Pp : pp), not the ratio of flower colours.", "source": "Slide picture", "pic": true},
  {"id": "pic-90b5e874efb6", "topic": "genetics", "outcome": "gen-o8a", "q": "<figure class=\"q-fig\"><img class=\"zoom\" src=\"img/slides/90b5e874efb6.webp\" alt=\"\" data-cap=\"Results of the monohybrid cross Pp × Pp: the &lt;b&gt;genotype ratio&lt;/b&gt; is 1 PP : 2 Pp : 1 pp, and the &lt;b&gt;phenotype ratio&lt;/b&gt; is 3 purple : 1 white, because PP and Pp both look purple.\" data-from=\"2026 SCIENCE STANDARD · page 68\"></figure>In this Pp × Pp cross, what fraction of the offspring are heterozygous?", "options": ["1/4", "1/2", "3/4", "All of them"], "answer": 1, "explain": "2 of the 4 genotypes are Pp, so 1/2 are heterozygous. 3/4 is the fraction with purple flowers, which also includes the PP plants.", "source": "Slide picture", "pic": true},
  {"id": "pic-c29a05c8d510", "topic": "genetics", "outcome": "gen-o8d", "q": "<figure class=\"q-fig\"><img class=\"zoom\" src=\"img/slides/c29a05c8d510.webp\" alt=\"\" data-cap=\"Pedigree of an &lt;b&gt;autosomal recessive&lt;/b&gt; trait with genotypes written in (f = recessive allele, ff = affected and shaded). Unaffected parents 1 and 2 in generation I (both Ff carriers) have affected children (ff), which proves the trait is recessive. An unaffected person with two carrier parents could be FF or Ff, which is why the chart writes &#x27;FF/Ff&#x27;.\" data-from=\"2026 SCIENCE STANDARD · page 70\"></figure>In generation II, an unaffected man and woman have an affected daughter in generation III. Why must they both be carriers (Ff)?", "options": ["Because they are both unaffected", "Because one of their parents is affected", "Because the trait appears in every generation", "Because their daughter is affected (ff), so each parent must have passed on an f allele"], "answer": 3, "explain": "An ff child must get one f from each parent, and since both parents are unaffected they must be Ff. Being unaffected alone does not prove someone is a carrier, because they could be FF.", "source": "Slide picture", "pic": true},
  {"id": "pic-22e1919a65c8", "topic": "genetics", "outcome": "gen-o8d", "q": "<figure class=\"q-fig\"><img class=\"zoom\" src=\"img/slides/22e1919a65c8.webp\" alt=\"\" data-cap=\"&lt;b&gt;Autosomal dominant&lt;/b&gt; pedigree: the trait appears in every generation, males and females are affected about equally, and every affected person has at least one affected parent. The key clue is that two affected parents (1 and 2) have an unaffected son (7), so both parents must be heterozygous and the trait must be dominant.\" data-from=\"2026 SCIENCE STANDARD · page 72\"></figure>Which observation in this pedigree proves that the trait is dominant?", "options": ["Affected parents 1 and 2 have an unaffected child, 7", "Unaffected parents 7 and 8 have unaffected children", "Both males and females are affected", "Individual 4 is an affected male"], "answer": 0, "explain": "Two affected parents can only have an unaffected child if both carry a hidden recessive (normal) allele, so the affected allele is dominant. Both sexes being affected only tells you it is probably autosomal, not whether it is dominant.", "source": "Slide picture", "pic": true},
  {"id": "pic-c2bf2ac2ac28", "topic": "genetics", "outcome": "gen-o8d", "q": "<figure class=\"q-fig\"><img class=\"zoom\" src=\"img/slides/c2bf2ac2ac28.webp\" alt=\"\" data-cap=\"&lt;b&gt;Autosomal recessive&lt;/b&gt; pedigree: unaffected parents (8 and 9) have an affected daughter (17), so the trait must be recessive and both parents are carriers. It cannot be X-linked recessive, because an affected daughter would then need an affected father, and 9 is unaffected.\" data-from=\"2026 SCIENCE STANDARD · page 73\"></figure>Which evidence shows this trait is <i>autosomal</i> recessive rather than X-linked recessive?", "options": ["Affected individuals appear in every generation", "Only males are affected", "Affected daughter 17 has an unaffected father (9)", "Affected mother 4 has an affected son (10)"], "answer": 2, "explain": "For an X-linked recessive trait, an affected daughter (X<sup>a</sup>X<sup>a</sup>) must get an X<sup>a</sup> from her father, who would then be affected. Father 9 is unaffected, so the gene must be on an autosome. Option D fits X-linked inheritance as well, so it proves nothing (but 4's <i>unaffected</i> son 9 would also rule out X-linkage).", "source": "Slide picture", "pic": true},
  {"id": "pic-f2ab4a5184aa", "topic": "genetics", "outcome": "gen-o8d", "q": "<figure class=\"q-fig\"><img class=\"zoom\" src=\"img/slides/f2ab4a5184aa.webp\" alt=\"\" data-cap=\"&lt;b&gt;X-linked recessive&lt;/b&gt; pedigree (e.g. colour blindness, haemophilia): mostly males are affected, because they have only one X. An affected mother (1) passes the allele to every child, so all her sons are affected and her daughters are carriers. A carrier mother (4) has a 50% chance of having an affected son.\" data-from=\"2026 SCIENCE STANDARD · page 74\"></figure>In this pedigree, the mother (1) is affected. What can you say about her sons?", "options": ["None of them can be affected", "All of them must be affected", "Half of them are expected to be affected", "They will all be carriers"], "answer": 1, "explain": "An affected mother is X<sup>a</sup>X<sup>a</sup>, so every son gets X<sup>a</sup> from her and Y from his father, and is affected (5 and 7). 'Half affected' is the pattern for a <i>carrier</i> mother, like 4.", "source": "Slide picture", "pic": true}
);
